{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command '$files = @('\"'CLAUDE.md','docs\\\\PRODUCTION_SPRINT_LESSONS.md','kibitz-runs\\\\2026-08-22-2026-08-22-ghost-prompt-v21\\\\r2\\\\input.md','.kibitz\\\\comfyui.local.md'); foreach (\"'$f in $files) { \"===== $f =====\"; Get-Content -Raw -LiteralPath $f }'","aggregated_output":"# OTR -- Project Operating Rules (Claude in Cowork)\n**Operator directives -- these win over any handoff, doc, or memory that disagrees.**\n- **Fix bugs properly, at the root cause -- never a shim or band-aid.** Don't wait for me to\n  fix anything: make the fix yourself, without asking. If it works, it's fine.\n- **NO CONTENT GUARDRAILS ON GENERATED EPISODES (operator directive 2026-08-03 -- supersedes\n  the older \"no curse words anywhere / SFW always\" line for EPISODE CONTENT).** Operator:\n  \"we [have] too [many] guardrails, no violence or swearing guardrails, they just cause\n  problems\" and \"I've given up chasing profanity.\" Do not add profanity or violence filtering\n  to the generation path, and do not reintroduce prompt clauses that forbid the source's own\n  content -- the adaptation packs were literally instructing the model to avoid \"blood, guns,\n  knives, and graphic violence\" while adapting MACBETH and KING LEAR, which is a fidelity\n  defect, not a safety win. On the adaptation lanes the author's own language is carried as\n  written; Wells' Editor may shout \"Story be damned!\" because Wells wrote it.\n  **Still true, and a different thing:** authoring style stays clean -- no curse words in\n  CODE, COMMENTS, LOGS or commit messages, and never the name \"dummy\". That rule is about what\n  WE write, not about what the pipeline generates from a source.\n- **STORY QUALITY IS DONE. STOP CHASING IT (operator directive 2026-08-04 -- hard).**\n  Operator: \"I am not chasing story quality anymore. It works. It works. I will publish it\n  as open source, and if someone else wants to do it, or in six months I wanna chase it\n  again when I've got better tools, I will.\" The scripts are ACCEPTED as they are. Do not\n  open writing-quality work, do not propose prompt-craft passes for better prose, do not\n  benchmark writer models for story quality, and do not spend on panels or cloud models to\n  improve scripts. This also settles the cloud-writer question: local stays the default and\n  no paid writer is adopted to raise prose quality.\n  **What this does NOT cover** -- these are CORRECTNESS defects, not quality chasing, and\n  remain open: a character's gender/voice contradicting the source, a character's face\n  changing between beats, voice-pool staleness, and any structural or ledger fault. Fixing\n  \"Malvolio speaks with a woman's voice\" is a bug fix; rewriting Malvolio's dialogue to be\n  better is not.\n- **NO WORD-COUNT CHASING (operator directive 2026-08-03).** \"We never chase word count.\"\n  The target words value is a REQUEST, not a gate: no refusals, no hard caps, no shunts. The\n  manifest `recommended_word_budget` upper bound is removed for exactly this reason. The only\n  real ceiling is structural (the beat topology tops out near 1,520 spoken words), and a\n  request beyond it simply delivers the closest performable episode.\n- **EVERY CODING ITEM GETS A FULL KIBITZ (operator directive 2026-08-04 -- hard, stands until\n  the operator withdraws it).** Operator: \"for the next coding sprint be sure any coding work\n  has a full `/kibitz-plugin:kibitz` review.\" **FULL means the default four-round arc** --\n  r1 arc -> r2 coding -> r3 wiring -> r4 convergence, 8 external calls (two reviewers x four\n  rounds). Not a scoped tail, not one round, not a continuation receipt; a partial campaign\n  may never be reported as a full arc. **Invoke the PLUGIN skill by name:\n  `kibitz-plugin:kibitz`** -- `anthropic-skills:kibitz` is the older duplicate and is not what\n  the operator asked for. Panel is driver-aware: Claude drives from Cowork, so **Codex +\n  Antigravity** review and you do NOT launch a second `claude -p` lane against your own family.\n  Use the ComfyUI profile overlay already in the repo, `.kibitz/comfyui.local.md` (written\n  2026-07-11 -- regenerate with `kibitz/scripts/comfyui_profile.py` if the tree has moved past\n  it). You still write the code-grounded `driver_anchor.md` FIRST and remain the sole judge:\n  the panel proposes, Claude disposes, and every panel claim is checked against the real\n  Windows files before it is folded in. This RAISES the two-strikes rule below -- a first-try\n  root fix now gets a panel too. It is local, it is $0, and it costs a wait, not a budget.\n- **AMENDED 2026-08-17 (operator): MATCH THE REVIEW TO THE TASK -- a full arc is not the\n  answer to everything.** Operator: *\"we aren't running a full kibitz on everything, it needs\n  to choose the best path for the right task.\"* The 08-04 directive above stands for what it\n  was aimed at; it was being applied to work that has no design in it, which wastes a wait and\n  teaches nobody anything.\n  **THE TEST, and it is one question: is there a design choice with more than one defensible\n  answer?**\n  * **YES -- new capability, architecture, a schema or ledger change, anything touching the\n    canonical workflow, anything where reasonable people would disagree** -> FULL four-round\n    arc BEFORE code, exactly as above. This is where the panel earns its keep: on the\n    2026-08-17 style build the panel killed a build-breaker (a required pack key would have\n    bricked every frozen embedded pack via its sha256 receipt), deleted the riskiest mechanism\n    outright, and corrected the driver's own execution-order claim.\n  * **NO -- one verifiable right answer: wiring conformance, a grep-and-fix, a stale comment,\n    a rename, an `/object_info` check, a deterministic edit** -> NO arc. An arc pressure-tests\n    DESIGN; there is nothing to pressure-test. Sonnet 5 QA on the finished diff before the\n    push is the correct and sufficient gate.\n  * **ONE CLEAN FINISHED-DIFF REVIEW IS ENOUGH (operator directive 2026-08-20).** Run one\n    independent reviewer after code. If it returns clean and the driver grounds that result\n    against the real files, STOP reviewing and move to tests/live proof. Add another reviewer\n    only when the first reports a blocker, two reviewers disagree, a material claim remains\n    unverifiable, or the two-strikes rule below fires. An available internal subagent is a\n    valid reviewer; do not wait on or multiply CLI/cloud lanes merely to increase the count.\n    Save the real roster and result in the handoff receipt.\n  * **UNSURE** -> treat it as YES. The arc is $0 and a missed design flaw is not.\n- **`otr/obs/` IS THE SUCCESS SIGNAL. ALWAYS PUBLISH TO IT (operator directive 2026-08-17 --\n  hard, and it OVERRIDES the tidiness instinct).** Operator, in his words: *\"always publish to\n  obs -- a test is not complete unless published to obs (or it's just testing one part). If I\n  see it in obs then it's somewhat a success.\"* And the failure half: *\"if I don't see it in\n  obs and it took more than 5 minutes, it's a fail.\"*\n  * **A leg that does not reach `otr/obs/` did not pass**, however green its logs are. The\n    published artifact IS the receipt -- that is how HE reads success without reading a log.\n  * **NEVER move, hide, sort or clean harness runs out of `otr/obs/`.** Soak, banksweep,\n    probe, acceptance and bank-gate legs BELONG there; seeing them is the point. **This rule\n    exists because I moved 17 of them into a `_diagnostics/` subfolder the same day, reading\n    them as pollution. They were his proof that the full path worked. Restored within\n    minutes; nothing was deleted.** The narrow, real complaint was only that the harness RUN\n    LABEL becomes the on-screen TITLE CARD -- cosmetic, and not a reason to touch the folder.\n  * **The `_bench_4arm` carve-out is NOT a precedent for this.** That exemption is scoped to\n    isolated stock-node BENCH graphs that never run the canonical workflow. A soak or bank-gate\n    leg DOES run the real canonical path end to end, which is exactly why its publication\n    proves something.\n  * **The 5-minute rule is a real gate:** if a leg has run longer than 5 minutes with nothing\n    in `otr/obs/`, treat it as failing and go read the leg log rather than waiting it out.\n  * **THE PRIORITY ORDER, in his words:** *\"long term yeah I want the title right, but for my\n    dailies it keeps me going to see episodes.\"* So the title-card label IS a real want and it\n    is a LONG-TERM one; the daily stream of published episodes is the thing that must never be\n    interrupted to get it. **Fix the title at the source (stop reusing `episode_title` as the\n    harness's scratch field) -- never by suppressing, relocating or gating the publish.** If a\n    proposed fix would reduce how many episodes he sees in `otr/obs/` tomorrow, it is the wrong\n    fix no matter how tidy it is.\n- **A MISSING REVIEWER NEVER BLOCKS THE ARC -- SUBSTITUTE AND KEEP GOING (operator directive\n  2026-08-17 -- hard).** Operator: *\"if out of budget then a kibitz reviewer is not needed,\n  ask Fable, Sonnet, or Opus, or `/anthropic-skills:roundtable` -- ANY model in lieu.\"*\n  **A quota-held, timed-out, or unavailable lane is NOT a reason to stop, to defer the item,\n  or to park a finished diff waiting for a lane to come back.** Fill the seat and run the\n  round.\n  * **Eligible substitutes, freely interchangeable:** Fable, Sonnet, Opus (as Cowork\n    subagents), the other `agy` model lanes (Gemini 3.1 Pro / 3.7 Flash are different\n    reviewers, not one), and `/anthropic-skills:roundtable` for a cloud panel. Any model in\n    lieu of any other.\n  * **WHAT STILL DOES NOT CHANGE:** you write the code-grounded `driver_anchor.md` FIRST and\n    you remain the sole judge; every panel claim is verified against the real Windows files\n    before it is folded in; and **the PROVENANCE is stated exactly** -- name which lanes\n    actually reviewed which rounds. A campaign a reviewer short is described as a campaign a\n    reviewer short, never as a full arc. Substituting is honest; misreporting the roster is\n    not.\n  * **This was written because it cost real time on 2026-08-17 (item F).** Codex was\n    quota-held to 08-19 and the driver treated that as a hard gate on the whole item --\n    \"F is not secretly cheap ... it wants the whole arc once Codex is back\". The operator's\n    correction: run it with who is available. Item F then ran r1-r4 on two agy lanes plus a\n    Fable gate and a Sonnet QA pass, caught three driver errors (two of them build-breakers:\n    an `UnboundLocalError` on four of six banks, and a lane collision that would have\n    announced an invented play on 57% of `media_archive`), and shipped proven on a live leg\n    -- **with Codex never participating in a single round.** The seat mattered; which model\n    sat in it did not.\n  * **A TIMEOUT IS NOT A QUOTA BLOCK.** `agy` failing with `Error: timeout waiting for\n    response` means the print timeout, not exhaustion -- `kibitz.py --timeout` does NOT reach\n    agy; the knob is **`KIBITZ_AGY_PRINT_TIMEOUT`** (default `5m`; `15m` fixed it first try).\n    Check `agy models` returns rc=0 before calling a lane dead.\n  **THE TWO-STRIKES FLOOR BELOW IS UNTOUCHED AND NEVER LAPSES.** A bug that survived two of\n  your fixes gets a panel on the third attempt no matter how mechanical it looks -- two failed\n  fixes IS the evidence that your model of the problem is wrong, which is a design problem\n  wearing mechanical clothes.\n  **Fable routing (section 9) is unchanged and is a separate axis:** never spend Fable on\n  mechanical work. Reserve it for narrative judgment and the final gate on a high-stakes,\n  hard-to-unwind structural change. Do not burn a scalpel on a screw.\n- **TWO STRIKES, THEN THE PANEL (operator directive 2026-07-14 -- hard).** You get **two**\n  attempts to fix a given problem on your own. If the **third** attempt is about to begin --\n  i.e. the same bug/failure survived two of your fixes -- you MUST `/kibitz` (local, $0,\n  file-grounded) BEFORE writing more code. No third solo swing, ever. Two failed fixes means\n  the model of the problem is wrong, and a third guess from the same wrong model just burns a\n  live roll. The panel's job is to break your framing, not to bless your patch. You remain the\n  judge: ground every panel claim against the real files and discard what does not survive.\n  (This is the FLOOR and it never lapses -- a third try ALWAYS gets a panel. The 2026-08-04\n  directive above is stricter while it stands: coding work gets a full panel on the first\n  swing too. The older \"kibitz on EVERY live failure\" wording is what this replaced.)\n- **RIPPING AN LLM IS ALLOWED. A HOLE IN THE LEDGER IS NOT (operator directive 2026-07-14 --\n  hard, non-negotiable).** Removing or repurposing an LLM pass is a legitimate and sometimes\n  necessary hard decision. But the LEDGER MUST STILL BE FILLED COMPLETELY for downstream\n  consumers -- TTS, per-beat audio slicing, video/shot direction, captions, credits,\n  `obs_publish`. They read FIELDS, not intentions. Before you remove a pass: (1) enumerate\n  EVERY ledger field it wrote; (2) give each field a new owner -- deterministic Python,\n  another pass, or an explicit default -- exactly one owner each; (3) only then delete the\n  call; (4) prove it on a LIVE leg (RESULT SUCCESS + obs_publish OK + the asset on disk).\n  A green unit suite does NOT prove the ledger is complete. A ripped pass with an unowned\n  field is a broken render, not a simplification.\n- **When genuinely torn between approaches, run the roundtable LIVE for convergence\n  (ChatGPT + Gemini + DeepSeek).** Skip the dry-run / cost estimate -- just run it, pronto.\n  You are the judge. Escalate to me only if the panel still leaves it unresolved.\n- `/kibitz` and `/roundtable` are also welcome for IDEAS at any time -- pressure-testing a\n  plan, hunting a defect class, hardening a doc -- not only as the two-strikes backstop.\n- This file is hard rules + the real Cowork operating model for this repo + hard-won gotchas.\n## 0. WORKFLOW SOURCE OF TRUTH (hard)\n`C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI\\custom_nodes\\ComfyUI-OldTimeRadio\\workflows\\otr_canonical.json`\nIS my workflow.\n- ANY json / node / wiring / widget change MUST be made IN that file, in the SAME change as the code.\n  Code that is not wired into this JSON is DEAD -- \"your updates are for naught\" (the 2026-06-13 A\u00154D\n  miss: a node + a new blend input shipped + tested but UNWIRED -> ran dormant in production).\n- EVERY API / headless / soak run MUST LOAD this real JSON -- never a stale copy, a generated\n  `.gen.json`, an ad-hoc graph, or the Linux-mount snapshot.\n- Schema: litegraph. Top level = `nodes[]` + `links[]` + `last_node_id` + `last_link_id`; a link is\n  `[link_id, src_node, src_slot, dst_node, dst_slot, type]`; one output fans out via its `links` list.\n  `widgets_values` is POSITIONAL -- only ever APPEND a new optional widget at the END (inserting mid-list\n  shifts every saved value -> silent drift, BUG-LOCAL-097). A widget converted to an input keeps its\n  value slot AND gains an input with `\"widget\": {\"name\": ...}`.\n- After editing it, re-validate: `OTR_WorkflowValidator` + a JSON round-trip + a link/widget audit\n  (widget-count vs live INPUT_TYPES, every wired input-name in INPUT_TYPES, link referential integrity).\n### 0A. ISOLATED-BENCH CARVE-OUT (operator ruling 2026-07-31, decision O6 -- NARROW)\nThe \"EVERY API / headless / soak run MUST LOAD this real JSON\" rule above has exactly ONE\nexemption, and this is it. `scripts/run_wan_ti2v_bakeoff.py` has submitted a stock-node API\ngraph since 2026-07-08 and shipped; that unwritten practice is now written down, and bounded.\n- **Who:** `scripts/run_video_arm_bakeoff.py` and `scripts/run_wan_ti2v_bakeoff.py`. No other runner.\n- **What:** ONLY the API-format graphs under `scripts/bench_graphs/` (+ the legacy\n  `scripts/otr_wan_ti2v_bakeoff_gguf.json`). Each is pinned by SHA-256 in the campaign manifest;\n  a graph whose digest does not match its pin FAILS the cell. No `.gen.json`, ever.\n- **Where the outputs go:** `otr/episodes/_bench_4arm/<arm>/` only -- diagnostic measurements,\n  never a deliverable, never `otr/obs/`, never quotable as a published episode.\n- **Validation gates that replace canonical loading:** every required node class and every model\n  filename confirmed live in `/object_info` before submit; graph JSON round-trip; asset validated\n  from `/history` (not a glob) then ffprobe'd for canvas, frame count, fps, codec and duration.\n- **What it does NOT license:** the exemption covers MEASUREMENT ONLY. No production render, no\n  soak, no published episode, and no engine/profile/tier change may ride a bench graph. Any\n  behaviour a bench discovers must be re-proved through `workflows/otr_canonical.json` before it\n  ships. A bench result may never be worded as qualification (see the SPEC, section 2).\n- Rationale + the full gate list: `docs/2026-07-31-four-arm-clamped-video-bench-SPEC.md` (G1/O6).\n## 1. HOW COWORK ACTUALLY WORKS HERE (read this first)\n- **Two separate filesystems.** The file tools (Read / Write / Edit) operate on the REAL Windows files --\n  that is your primary editor. **Desktop Commander** (`mcp__Desktop_Commander__*`) runs PowerShell on the\n  same real Windows box -- use it for git, the venv python, tests, and process control. The\n  `mcp__workspace__bash` Linux sandbox is a DIFFERENT machine: its mount LAGS the file-tool writes (shows\n  stale/truncated copies -> phantom \"corruption\") and has NO torch. Use bash only for quick greps of\n  UNCHANGED files; never trust it for current state and never run the suite there.\n- **The loop:** edit with the file tools -> verify/test with the Windows venv via Desktop Commander ->\n  commit AND push via DC git -> verify HEAD == origin.\n- **Test runner:** `C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI\\.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe` (torch 2.10). Run with\n  `$env:PYTHONUTF8=1`; `pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider`. The conftest sets `OTR_TEST_MODE`/`CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=''`.\n  Bug Bible lives in a SEPARATE repo: `C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI\\comfyui-custom-node-survival-guide`\n  -- `cd` to its root and use the RELATIVE path `tests\\bug_bible_regression.py` (an absolute forward-slash\n  path fails to collect).\n- **Knowledge gate before implementation or diagnosis:** read `docs\\PRODUCTION_SPRINT_LESSONS.md`, then the\n  relevant entries in `docs\\PROD_BUG_LOG.md`, and the matching portable rules in\n  `C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI\\comfyui-custom-node-survival-guide\\BUG_BIBLE.yaml`. The lessons are\n  mandatory project context, the production log is the staging record, and the Bible is the reusable\n  cross-project contract. A newly fixed, repeatable production failure must be recorded in the log and\n  promoted with a Bible entry plus executable coverage whenever its verify condition is automatable.\n- **Admission rule:** only a bug verified by a live production artifact, headless run, smoke, soak, or\n  published episode may enter `PROD_BUG_LOG.md` or be promoted to the Bug Bible. A review observation,\n  static-audit finding, or invented test fixture may verify a known production bug, but never creates a\n  new PBUG or Bible rule on its own.\n- **Bible delta-scrape discipline (2026-08-07):** the Bible repo carries `otr_coverage_index.yaml` --\n  all 369 OTR bug records through 2026-08-07 (BUG_LOG.md, BUG_LOG_2026-06.md, docs/handoffs, kibitz\n  runs, loose logs, git fix history) mapped to Bible ids against the 261-entry Bible (HEAD 3759ae5).\n  NEVER re-scrape indexed history -- the full scrape cost ~4M tokens once and the index exists so it\n  is never paid again. At session wrap-up / handoff, if the session recorded a NEW bug (admission rule\n  above): check it against the index + Bible, promote a genuinely uncovered one with a Bible entry,\n  and append its row to the index in the same change. Any cached Bible copy, entry count, or vendored\n  test snapshot re-syncs to the Bible repo's origin/main -- do not pin a stale local copy of the\n  Bible or its tests.\n- **PowerShell reality (DC runs powershell.exe):** use `;` to chain, NOT `&&`. Do NOT use\n  `python -c \"...\"` with nested quotes -- PowerShell mangles them; instead WRITE A TEMP `.py` file, run\n  it, then delete it. `2>&1` makes stderr render as scary red text -- that is NOT a failure; check the\n  exit code / output. Pipe noisy output through `Select-Object -Last N`.\n- **QUOTING / `$`-INTERPOLATION RULE (hard -- this bites EVERY session that ignores it):** any command\n  that would need NESTED quotes, backtick-escaped quotes (`` `\" ``), a `$` variable inside a quoted\n  argument, a here-string fed to another program, or `cmd.exe /c \"<...>\"` -- DO NOT attempt it inline\n  and DO NOT iterate on escaping (\"one more tweak\" never works; PowerShell mangles `$var` and eats\n  quote layers). IMMEDIATELY write a temp LAUNCHER SCRIPT instead -- a `.ps1` (or `.py`/`.cmd`) written\n  via the FILE TOOLS (Write, not echo/Set-Content with its own quoting problem), with all variables and\n  quoting inside the script where they are literal and safe -- run it via\n  `powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File <tmp.ps1>` (or `Start-Process -FilePath`), then delete it.\n  First quoting error = STOP escaping, switch to the script. Zero exceptions.\n- **The ~60s MCP ceiling:** any single DC command that blocks longer (a `Start-Sleep` > ~45s, a big render\n  loop, the full ~4200-test suite, a slow boot wait) TIMES OUT and orphans the process. Background it to a\n  log and poll the log, or shrink the job (a test subset, fewer frames). DC itself is NOT flaky -- the\n  command was too long.\n- **Subagents (Agent tool)** are excellent for read-only fan-out audits, but TELL them to use Desktop\n  Commander + the Windows venv/path -- left to default they read the lagging Linux mount and report\n  phantom truncation/corruption (happened 2026-06-13; the third agent that used the Windows path was right).\n- **Stale git `index.lock`:** if `git add`/`commit` fails with \"index.lock: File exists\" AND\n  `Get-Process git` is empty, the lock is STALE (a real git op finishes in seconds) -- remove\n  `.git\\index.lock` and retry. Do NOT remove it while a git process is actually running.\n- **One coder window in the code at a time** (serialize via `docs/GO_FORWARD_PLAN.md`). Two windows\n  editing the same file -- especially the workflow JSON -- is how it gets corrupted.\n- Use **AskUserQuestion** for genuine operator decisions; use the **task list** for any multi-step work.\n## 2. AUTONOMY / PRIME DIRECTIVE\n- NEVER ask me to run scripts, commands, or anything. YOU run it: Desktop Commander first; if DC can't,\n  Windows MCP; then the filesystem tools. Never hand me a bat/cmd/PowerShell block and say \"run this.\"\n- You can drive the 5080 GPU yourself -- spin up the headless ComfyUI API (port 8000) and run it; don't\n  ask me. (Reset the box first -- section 4.)\n- If a senior pair-programmer would just do it, just do it. Stuck choosing between options? Roundtable\n  2-3 panels (GPT + Gemini + DeepSeek) for opinions BEFORE asking me -- run it LIVE (skip the dry-run),\n  you are the judge.\n## 3. CODING DISCIPLINE\n- Keep coding until all sprints are done unless you genuinely need me.\n- Run the regression suite + the Bug Bible after EVERY code change (don't wait to be asked). Commit AND\n  push per green chunk (section 7).\n- Prefer editing the file you're already in; don't spray new throwaway files (and delete any temp probe\n  scripts before committing). Keep handoff files current.\n- Names: never \"dummy\" -- use \"placeholder\", \"stub\", or a descriptive name (\"dummy\" makes me feel bad).\n  SFW always. UTF-8, no BOM. Clean logs, meaningful names -- the reader matters.\n## 4. RESET BEFORE EVERY HEADLESS RUN (hard)\nThe soak/quick-smoke harness boots ONE server, runs all legs against it, and does NOT tear it down -- it\nsits RESIDENT holding ~60% VRAM. Never assume a prior run cleaned up. Before launching:\n- Kill SELECTIVELY by CommandLine (CIM) -- NOT a blanket `Stop-Process -Name python,pythonw`. A blanket\n  python kill ALSO kills the Claude MCP extension pythons (Desktop Commander / computer-use) and severs\n  your own tools mid-run. Use `Get-CimInstance Win32_Process -Filter \"Name='python.exe'\"` and kill the\n  ones whose CommandLine matches the ComfyUI server + the soak/sweep harness; plus the port kill via\n  `Get-NetTCPConnection -LocalPort 8000`. (`.Path` is BLANK for half-booted servers, so filter on\n  CommandLine, not Path.)\n- Confirm `Get-NetTCPConnection -LocalPort 8000 -State Listen` is EMPTY and\n  `nvidia-smi --query-gpu=memory.used --format=csv,noheader` dropped to the desktop baseline (~1.5 GB)\n  before booting fresh.\n## 5. HEADLESS BOOT + MONITORING GOTCHAS (2026-06-12 -- do not relose)\n- **A render that FINISHED leaves the server RESIDENT (~9-10 GB, 1% util) -- that is NOT a crash.** Before\n  declaring a run dead, read the server log: `Prompt executed in HH:MM:SS` + `obs_publish OK` = it\n  COMPLETED. The idle resident VRAM is the no-teardown behavior. (Misread twice 2026-06-12.)\n- **Use the watchdog for long renders** (`scripts/otr_render_watchdog.ps1 -LegLog <leg.log>`): declares the\n  run DEAD on a 5-min heartbeat stall OR a down :8000/queue endpoint (exit 2), exits 0 with the verdict\n  when the leg finishes. It REPORTS only; reset per section 4.\n- **Headless boot needs UTF-8.** A detached cmd inherits the Windows cp1252 codec, so OTR\n  `prestartup_script.py` crashes the instant it prints an emoji (UnicodeEncodeError on U+2705/U+2713) ->\n  boot dies ~13s, exit 1 (\"SERVER DID NOT COME UP\"). The launcher (`scripts/_otr_soak_server_launch.cmd`)\n  sets `PYTHONUTF8=1` + `PYTHONIOENCODING=utf-8`; any new boot path MUST too.\n- **Boot is ~20s, NOT 7-8 min.** If a boot \"hangs\", read the log -- it has already died.\n- **Launch the server via the .cmd as `-FilePath`, never `cmd.exe /c \"<cmd>\" \"<log>\"`.** The `/c`\n  two-quoted-token rule eats the outer quotes -> mangled path -> ZERO log output. Use\n  `Start-Process -FilePath $LAUNCHCMD -ArgumentList \"`\"$LOG`\"\"`.\n## 6. OUTPUT / ASSET PATHS (hard)\nRendered episode assets are deliverables -- they do NOT live in tmp/scratch, and they are NEVER left in a\nswept dir to be moved later.\n- Every rendered asset (audio, frames, intermediate video) -> `otr\\episodes\\<ep>\\`. The final published\n  episode -> `otr\\obs\\` (what `obs_publish` targets; `obs_publish OK` in the log = it landed there).\n- Point the render at its canonical path the FIRST time. Never stage an asset in tmp \"to move later\" --\n  the move-later step is where work dies.\n- A temp probe/script in tmp is fine (throwaway, per section 3) -- but the ASSET it produces is written\n  STRAIGHT to its canonical path, never parked in tmp.\n- Output destination is workflow config: if a node writes to the wrong place, fix the path IN the\n  workflow JSON (section 0), in the SAME change as any code -- not with a post-hoc move.\n- After any render leg, confirm the asset exists at its canonical path (`Test-Path otr\\episodes\\<ep>\\<file>`)\n  before continuing or declaring success. The file check -- NOT just VRAM -- is what distinguishes the\n  \"finished but resident\" server (section 5) from a real miss. Missing = STOP and report; do not continue.\n- Paths are relative to the repo root (`...\\ComfyUI-OldTimeRadio\\`). If `otr\\` actually resolves to\n  ComfyUI's real `output\\` base on disk, use that base -- but the episodes/obs split holds either way.\n## 7. GIT POLICY (operator directive 2026-06-10 -- never lose work)\n- ONE branch: `v2.0-alpha`. COMMIT AND PUSH TOGETHER: every green commit gets pushed to origin\n  immediately, same session, no exceptions. Local-only commits are the failure mode we guard against.\n- **`v2.0-alpha` IS THE GITHUB DEFAULT BRANCH as of 2026-08-22.** It was switched from `main`\n  because `main` sits 3,923 commits behind and still advertises `version = \"1.0.0\"` -- so every\n  bare repo link, every fresh `git clone`, and every ComfyUI-Manager NIGHTLY install was serving\n  stale v1 code while looking like it had worked. A fresh clone now lands on `v2.0-alpha` with the\n  real v2 tree (verified by actual clone, not assumption). Do NOT \"helpfully\" switch it back, and\n  do not treat `main` as current for ANY purpose -- it is a stale v1.7 release merge, nothing more.\n- The operator eyeball gates TAGS and PROMOTIONS (`v2.0-alpha-stable`, prod, main, v2 release) -- NEVER\n  pushes. Pushing to `v2.0-alpha` is always safe, expected, and required.\n- This SUPERSEDES any \"do not push until the eyeball passes\" line written before 2026-06-10 evening.\n- A stable branch only exists if the operator explicitly declares one.\n- After every push verify: HEAD == origin, no 0-byte files, no BOM, AST parse on touched .py files.\n### 7A. COMFY REGISTRY PUBLISHING (live since 2026-08-22 -- read before touching pyproject.toml)\nThe pack is published to registry.comfy.org as **`comfyui-old-time-radio`** under publisher\n**`fluxus`** (the operator's account). `.github/workflows/publish_action.yml` publishes via\n`Comfy-Org/publish-node-action`, keyed on the repo secret `REGISTRY_ACCESS_TOKEN`.\n- **EDITING `pyproject.toml` AUTO-FIRES A PUBLISH.** The workflow triggers on any push to\n  `v2.0-alpha` whose diff touches `pyproject.toml`. Treat that file as a release trigger, not a\n  config file: never edit it \"just to tidy\" mid-session, and never edit it while a version is\n  already pending. A push that does NOT touch it never publishes.\n- **Every publish needs a NEW version string.** `(node_id, version)` is uniquely indexed\n  server-side; re-publishing the same version is refused.\n- **`.comfyignore` decides what SHIPS** (gitignore syntax, layered on top of git tracking --\n  untracked files are excluded already). It currently strips `tests/`, `kibitz-runs/`,\n  `.github/`, `.claude/`, plus the exec()-using probe/smoke scripts that comfy-cli's security\n  scanner flags. Verify by downloading the published zip, never by assuming.\n- **PENDING IS A QUEUE, NOT A REJECTION.** A new version lands `NodeVersionStatusPending` and is\n  promoted to `Active` only by Comfy-Org's own Cloud Scheduler cron hitting their `/security-scan`\n  endpoint, which ONLY considers versions older than **30 minutes** (`registry.go:938`). Clean\n  scan -> Active; issues found -> `Flagged` (their private Discord, not us); missing zip ->\n  `Deleted`. The scanner itself is a PRIVATE repo and its schedule is not in any public config --\n  possibly nightly. **While a version is Pending, `latest_version` resolves to null, which is\n  exactly why ComfyUI Manager reports \"not a CNR node\" / \"Cannot resolve install target\".**\n  That error is NOT a local install fault -- do not send anyone chasing torch/dependency ghosts.\n- **There is NO publisher self-service path to Active.** Confirmed by reading\n  `Comfy-Org/registry-backend`. Waiting, or asking Comfy-Org, are the only moves.\n- **DELETE ASYMMETRY, and it is a trap:** deleting the NODE is a HARD delete (row removed,\n  versions cascade, every version string freed for reuse). Deleting a VERSION is a SOFT delete\n  (status flipped to `deleted`, ROW REMAINS) -- which BURNS that version string permanently.\n  Prefer node-delete for a clean slate; never version-delete a string you want back.\n- **The registry DELETE API needs the operator's browser (Firebase) session -- the publish token\n  returns 401 \"user not found\".** Claude cannot delete a listing; the operator clicks it. Deletion\n  is also EVENTUALLY consistent: the version list can serve stale reads from different replicas\n  for minutes afterward. Read it 2-3 times before concluding anything.\n## 8. ROUNDTABLE DEFAULTS (operator directive 2026-06-22)\nStanding shape for EVERY `/roundtable` in this repo. These OVERRIDE the skill's stock\n\"Claude is judge-only / panel only critiques\" and \"dry-run estimate first\" defaults.\n- **Panel = 2-3 FRONTIER models per round** (GPT + Gemini + DeepSeek/Grok class; `~latest`\n  aliases, record the resolved model in the manifest). Lean panel of genuinely different\n  families beats many near-duplicates -- diversity is the point, correctness comes from the\n  grounding step.\n- **Cowork (Claude) is ALWAYS a code-aware grounded PANELIST *and* the sole judge.** Write your\n  own grounded review FIRST (every claim checked against the real Windows files via Desktop\n  Commander, never the lagging Linux mount), THEN ground the panel's reviews, discard the\n  misreads, and synthesize. The panel proposes; Claude disposes -- never outsource synthesis.\n- **Four-round campaign, in order:** R1 high-level arc / creative approach -> R2 coding plan ->\n  R3 wiring (workflow JSON / nodes / widgets + any re-baseline procedure) -> R4 final\n  convergence (confirm no new must-fix). Re-loop a round only if it surfaces new material; stop\n  at convergence (don't grind passes to hear \"looks good\" in more accents).\n- **Never dry-run, never pre-compute the cost -- just spend and run it LIVE,** then state the\n  actual spend after. (Only backstop = the global >= $20-or-irreversible gate, which roundtable\n  passes never reach in practice.)\n- **ARC ROUTING (operator directive 2026-07-09): R1 (ideas/high-level) = cloud `/roundtable`\n  on the highest frontier models (that's where paid diversity earns its keep); R2-R4 (coding /\n  wiring / convergence) = `/kibitz` (local Codex + Antigravity) ALWAYS PREFERRED -- $0, file-\n  grounded. R1 via kibitz is also fine when economy matters. OpenRouter is paid, so default mechanical review to the local panel.\n- Artifacts under `docs/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<topic>/roundtable/` (pass00..passNN_plan.md +\n  passNN_judgment.md), UTF-8 no BOM, ASCII where practical.\n## 9. MODEL ROUTING -- when to spend Fable (operator directive 2026-07-03)\nFable is a scalpel, not a default. Spawn it ONLY when output quality depends on narrative\njudgment; keep everything mechanical off it. This is about WHICH MODEL a subagent runs --\northogonal to the filesystem rule in section 1 and the frontier roundtable panel in section 8\n(that panel is external cloud models via OpenRouter; this is the Fable model as a Cowork subagent).\n- **Use Fable for:** generative creative work where voice/taste matter -- story spine, character\n  interiority, dialogue passes, pitch-room ideation, style/tone calls. Divergent fanout when you\n  want real variance, not one answer (e.g. 3 subagents each pitching a different take on a scene --\n  only fan out on a genuine fork you'll actually select between). Judgment on already-generated\n  narrative -- \"which brief holds together,\" \"where does this arc sag.\"\n- **Do NOT use Fable for:** mechanical/deterministic work -- repo grep, mapping references, editing\n  JSON, validation, wiring checks, git. Route those to general-purpose / Explore, or just do them in\n  the main window. Also skip Fable for one-shot factual/structural questions with a single right answer.\n- **Keep the spawn count down:** batch context so each Fable call does a meaningful chunk (a whole\n  scene, not one line); gate the spawn on a real fork -- if you'd just take the first answer, use one\n  call or none.\n- **REALITY EXCEPTION -- Fable's grounded FAN-OUT audit DOES catch real code build-breakers the\n  mechanical panels miss (proven 2026-07-03).** On the VRAM-tier rip, a Fable end-to-end fan-out found\n  a CHUNK-ORDER KeyError (the workflow validator reads a profile key that was being removed mid-rip ->\n  every production render would KeyError) that BOTH codex and general-purpose review missed; it also did\n  the frame-budget arithmetic (29->33 frames) that broke two unlisted tests, and spotted a grep-invisible\n  hyphenated `--vram-ceiling` flag. So Fable is NOT only-narrative. Reserve it as the FINAL GATE on a\n  HIGH-STAKES, hard-to-unwind, production-touching STRUCTURAL change (a big rip/refactor about to be\n  executed / merged) -- ONE grounded pass, AFTER codex + general-purpose have gone first, when a missed\n  thread would break the build and cost a debugging-and-revert loop. This is the \"insight\" worth the\n  spend. It does NOT reopen Fable for ROUTINE review: everyday grep / wiring / validation / JSON still\n  defaults to general-purpose/Explore/codex. Fable = the expensive last set of eyes on the make-or-break,\n  not the default reviewer.\n\r\n===== docs\\PRODUCTION_SPRINT_LESSONS.md =====\r\n# Production Sprint Lessons\n\n## Mandatory use\n\nBefore an implementation, live diagnosis, source-bank change, or workflow edit,\nread this document and the relevant entry in `PROD_BUG_LOG.md`. Then consult the\nportable counterpart in the shared Bug Bible at\n`C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI\\comfyui-custom-node-survival-guide\\BUG_BIBLE.yaml`.\nThis document retains the OTR-specific causal history; the Bible carries the\ngeneralized rule and its executable guard. Record a newly discovered production\nfailure in the log first, and promote it to the Bible when it is repeatable and\ngeneralizable. Do not admit an invented fixture, code-review observation, or\nstatic-audit finding as a new production bug; those may only verify a failure\nalready observed in a live run, smoke, soak, or published artifact.\n\nUse this guide when planning any OTR coding sprint, including story banks,\ndynamic visual direction, audio/SFX layers, model integrations, and workflow\nchanges. The project rules in `AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md` remain authoritative.\n\n## 1. Define ownership before implementation\n\nFor every artifact and field, name:\n\n- the one authoritative writer;\n- every consumer;\n- whether it is authored, derived, or measured;\n- its lifecycle boundary (draft, accepted, frozen, or post-freeze extension);\n- its durable storage and replay receipt.\n\nIf an input list becomes output rows, state the mapping exactly: one row per\ninput item, one singular owned value per row, exact reference coverage, and\nwhich collections live at the top level. List the exact fields allowed in each\nnested row. Do not assume a model infers ownership or nesting from a JSON\nschema.\n\n## 2. Keep five representations in lockstep\n\nEvery structured LLM pass has five representations that must agree:\n\n1. base prompt;\n2. typed schema;\n3. worked fixture/example;\n4. parser and deterministic validator;\n5. repair prompt.\n\nA change to one requires an audit of the other four. Explicitly forbid common\npseudo-shapes: numbered fields, `_secondary`/`_tertiary` variants, schema-path\nkeys, singular-vs-list aliases, and valid collections nested at the wrong\ndepth.\n\n## 3. Separate authored decisions from mechanical repair\n\nPython may safely repair only facts already determined by accepted artifacts:\nIDs, ordering, references, exact enums, hashes, routing metadata, duplicate\nreferences with an unambiguous first owner, and relocation of values whose\ndestination is uniquely declared.\n\nPython must not invent story ownership, grounding, or authoritative narrative\ndecisions: motives, clues, causality, visual taste, sound-design decisions, or\nsource claims. If placement or meaning is ambiguous, return the defect to the\nowning model and fail closed when the bounded ladder exhausts.\n\nThe one narrow exception is a terminal spoken-surface hygiene floor after every\nbounded model repair rung has exhausted on a non-safety craft defect. It may\nstrip action/markup, normalize speech-only tokens, trim a clause, or substitute\na small generic SFW utterance whose value is delivery rather than story\nauthority. It must not alter immutable evidence, grounding, graph ownership, or\nfactual suffixes; it must validate the exact TTS projection, stamp the gate and\nresolving rung, and isolate an empty result to that row. Content safety and\nambiguous structural defects remain fail-closed.\n\nA deterministic repair must validate the complete downstream contract before\nreturning. A partially valid schema object can consume the repair rung and\nprevent the intended LLM fallback.\n\n## 4. Design retry ladders by failure class\n\n- JSON syntax failure: same prompt at lower temperature may help.\n- Typed/schema failure: send the failed artifact, exact error, schema, and\n  explicit correction rules to typed repair.\n- Semantic/graph failure: name the objective invariant and the owning item;\n  do not ask a lower-temperature retry to guess.\n- Incomplete repair JSON: retry the exact cached repair prompt only when the\n  configured budget permits it.\n\nLog which rung ran and why. Never silently truncate, silently coerce authored\ncontent, or fall back to a canned artifact.\n\n## 5. Size context from the real artifact\n\nDo not derive output or context budgets from `target_words` alone. Include the\nactual size drivers: accepted line count, evidence rows, graph width, schema\noverhead, prior artifact size, and repair envelope size. The repair prompt is\noften the largest call.\n\nResolve the true context cap for the selected model and fail loudly when a\nprovenance-sensitive prompt cannot fit. A model's native context and the\nproject's configured safe context are separate facts; measure both.\n\n## 6. Test model diversity, not just repeatability\n\nA prompt that works on one local LLM is not production-qualified. Different\nfamilies fail differently: integer IDs, extra fields, renamed forbidden keys,\nwrong nesting, copied request envelopes, enum synonyms, or over-wide output.\n\nQualification ladder for model-sensitive work:\n\n1. unit fixtures and full regression gates;\n2. canonical 30-word end-to-end smoke on at least two different local model\n   families and one configured cloud/frontier creative lane;\n3. the same pairings at 120 words;\n4. only then, 720-word qualification or bakeoff.\n\nRecord concrete model labels, slot assignments, prompt IDs, repair counts,\nledger paths, and final asset paths for every leg.\n\n## 7. Prove the real workflow end to end\n\nAlways load `workflows/otr_canonical.json`. Code that is not wired into that\nfile is dead. Any node, input, widget, or link change updates and validates the\ncanonical JSON in the same commit.\n\nAn API `SUCCESS`, idle VRAM, or a resident server is not final proof. Verify:\n\n- the saved ledger and required receipts;\n- the canonical episode asset under `otr/episodes/<ep>/`;\n- `obs_publish OK`;\n- the final file exists under `otr/obs/`.\n\nConversely, resident VRAM after `Prompt executed` is not evidence that a run is\nstill alive. Check the queue, runner exit code, history result, log, and file.\n\n## 8. Keep GPU experiments clean and comparable\n\nBefore every headless run, selectively stop only ComfyUI and its harnesses,\nclear port 8000, and confirm baseline VRAM. Never blanket-kill Python. Boot with\nUTF-8 through the canonical launcher. Save one server log and one leg log per\nrun, and use the watchdog for long renders.\n\nChange one meaningful variable per comparison. Do not mix a model change, a\nprompt change, a quant change, and a context change into one unexplained result.\n\n## 9. Treat live failures as reusable project knowledge\n\nEvery bug that actually fails a live smoke, soak, or published episode gets an\nappend-only entry in `docs/PROD_BUG_LOG.md` with symptom, root cause, fix,\nverification idea, and Bug Bible candidacy. Dev-only catches are fixed and\ntested but do not enter the production log.\n\nPromote recurring, machine-checkable failure classes to the Bug Bible later.\nAlso update the relevant planning guide and preflight so the next sprint avoids\nthe bug before implementation.\n\n## 10. Preserve concurrency boundaries\n\nOne window owns code and the canonical workflow. Parallel windows may perform\nread-only investigation or docs-only scoping, but they do not edit active code,\ntests, registries, or workflow JSON. Preserve unrelated dirty-tree files and\nstage only the owned change.\n\nAfter every code change: focused tests, full Windows suite, Bug Bible, AST/JSON\nchecks, commit, push to `v2.0-alpha`, and verify `HEAD == origin` before live\nqualification.\n\n## 11. Prove semantic provenance, not just structural references\n\nAn ID can remain valid while its meaning disappears. If artifact A carries a\ntyped clue and artifact B carries only that clue's ID, a later model can emit a\nself-consistent but unrelated story while every graph/reference validator\npasses.\n\nFor content-owned pipelines, build a small immutable grounding contract from\nthe accepted source/draw. Carry exact positive anchors through every authoring\nand retake seam, and verify their spoken evidence at the coordinates that own\nthem. Structural coverage, an LLM audit, a frozen ledger, and a published video\ncannot override a deterministic semantic-fidelity failure.\n\n## 12. Test channel isolation by changing one channel\n\nComments saying �?ovisual style does not affect story�?? are not proof. Run the\nsame mocked story pipeline twice while changing only the visual-style value and\nassert every captured story-model message is byte-identical. Apply the same\nmethod to SFX, voice, render, and other downstream channels.\n\nThe story may feed visual direction; visual direction must not feed back into\nstory authorship unless a separately named, explicitly designed mode owns that\ndecision. Store selectors in separate ledger namespaces so a forensic audit can\ndistinguish correlation from causation.\n\n## 13. Persist enough accepted artifacts to locate first drift\n\nResponse hashes prove identity only when the response is still available. They\ncannot reveal where meaning first changed after a production run. Persist a\nbounded receipt of accepted typed artifacts and their grounding evidence:\nselected input, truth/plan, score/manifest, final authored output, audit, and\nline/span coordinates.\n\nDo not retain raw prompts, rejected outputs, or unlimited retry prose by\ndefault. The goal is replayable accepted-state forensics, not an unbounded model\ntranscript.\n\n## 14. Close every nested object, not only the artifact root\n\nListing required schema paths tells a model what must exist; it does not tell\nthe model which plausible bookkeeping fields must not exist. For every strict\nstructured artifact, state the exact key set at each nested ownership boundary,\nespecially music, media, provenance, delivery, and file/path-shaped objects.\n\nPair that prompt contract with a narrow structural normalizer only where Python\ncan prove the removed fields are non-authoritative metadata. Such a normalizer\nmust preserve all required authored values byte-for-byte and rerun the complete\nstrict schema plus semantic/graph validators. Never project an arbitrary model\nobject onto a schema when an unknown key could carry story meaning.\n\n## 15. Make ordered graph topology executable\n\nAn array can contain every required typed row and still violate the graph's\nmeaning through order alone. State sequence rules with a concrete valid and\ninvalid example (for example A, A, B versus A, B, A), identify which manifest\nowns the canonical group order, and enforce the invariant in Python.\n\nNever reorder authored chronological rows as a structural repair: sequence is\nstory, even when every row object remains byte-identical. Repair only mechanical\nidentifier topology when it is provably unambiguous -- for example, split a\nreopened A/B/A shot into A/B/A-return by cloning the shot metadata and retagging\nthe later run -- while holding the authored row sequence fixed. Then rerun the\ncomplete graph, semantic, grounding, and landmark validators. Reject or fall\nthrough to typed repair whenever the identifier split is ambiguous.\n\n## 16. Run deterministic projections at every attempt boundary\n\nA typed-repair prompt factory sees the failed base response, but it does not\nnecessarily see or normalize the model's typed-repair response. If a safe\nprojection exists only inside that factory, a model can repeat the identical\nmechanical defect on the final repair attempt and bypass the projection.\n\nHash the actual raw model response first, then apply the same narrow projection\nat the slot-output boundary for every base, syntax-retry, and typed-repair\nresponse. Return the projected artifact only when its strict schema and complete\nsemantic/grounding validators pass; otherwise retain the raw response and let\nthe ordinary ladder report or repair the real remaining defect.\n\n## 17. Put typed structural repair at the accepted-object boundary\n\nRaw-string cleanup is appropriate for unambiguous JSON-shape defects, such as\nlifting a declared collection from the wrong nesting level. It is not enough\nfor a projection whose safety depends on the fully parsed artifact and its\ncomplete graph/grounding validator. Apply that kind of repair in the\nschema-validated post-check over the same typed object that will be returned to\nthe caller.\n\nTest the boundary itself: deliberately disable or bypass the earlier raw\nnormalizer, then prove both a base response and a typed-repair response still\naccept only after the typed projection preserves authored values and clears all\nvalidators. This prevents a duplicated pre-parse helper from becoming a\nfalse-green proxy for the production acceptance path.\n\n## 18. Group safe graph ownership repairs at one typed boundary\n\nFor a schema-valid score, related exact invariants such as contiguous shot\nownership and one-to-one clue ownership should share the same typed\npost-validation boundary. The model owns the first authored placement and all\nmeaningful prose; Python may only derive a later mechanical identity or remove\nan exact repeated reference when the complete typed graph proves that no story\nmeaning is lost.\n\nKeep raw cleanup limited to defects that prevent parsing at all. Test each\ntyped repair with that raw path disabled, including a typed-repair response,\nso a pre-parse helper cannot falsely appear to qualify the production guard.\n\n## 19. Compose independent safe projections before global validation\n\nDo not make each narrow mechanical repair demand that every other invariant is\nalready clean. A score can contain two independently provable defects, such as\na reopened shot run and a duplicate clue reference. Factor a projector from its\nfull-validation wrapper, apply only a small declared set of disjoint projectors\nin deterministic order, and then run the complete graph, grounding, and safety\nchecks once over their shared result.\n\nThe composition must be bounded, must preserve authored content and chronology,\nand must remain fail-closed for an unknown, missing, ambiguous, or still-invalid\ncondition. Add a regression where the base and typed-repair responses contain\nthe complete defect combination; testing each repair in isolation is not\nevidence that the production boundary can combine them.\n\n## 20. Repair localized semantic omissions with bounded typed patches\n\nDo not resend an entire accepted artifact merely because one LLM-owned leaf\nmisses an immutable semantic anchor. Whole-document regeneration increases\ncontext pressure, expands the failure surface, and can force the model to\nrecreate already-valid structure instead of correcting the actual omission.\n\nDefine a minimal patch schema that names the allowed targets and fields. Python\nmay derive those targets from the immutable contract and must verify exact\none-for-one coverage, literal anchor inclusion, and no changes outside the\ndeclared patch scope. The model still authors the replacement prose. After the\nmerge, rerun the complete artifact, grounding, and authored-surface validators;\nan unknown or broader defect must remain fail-closed rather than being squeezed\nthrough the narrow tool.\n\n## 21. Preserve accepted invariants in every replaced patch field\n\nA patch that replaces an entire leaf value can remove a different invariant that\nwas already correct in that same value. Include both the newly required facts\nand every immutable fact currently present in a selected target. This matters\nwhen one beat carries multiple contracts, such as a reveal or closure beat that\nalso owns a clue.\n\nValidate the *merged canonical artifact* in the structured-call post-validator,\nnot only the patch's local key set. A local patch can be schema-valid and carry\nall newly requested literals while still breaking a graph, grounding, safety, or\nauthorship invariant elsewhere in the target field. Return the precise merged\nerror to the typed repair ladder and remain fail-closed if it cannot be cleared.\n\n## 22. Apply localized semantic repair at every artifact boundary\n\nDo not stop the bounded-patch pattern at a planning artifact. A complete\nperformance script can be just as expensive and unreliable to regenerate for a\nsingle missing immutable phrase as a complete score. Keep the authoring model\nresponsible for replacement prose, but create a separate typed patch seam for\neach artifact whose individual leaves have clear ownership and a full validator.\n\nFor spoken-script patches, target line IDs only; retain every valid literal\nalready spoken by that line, require every newly required literal, and rerun the\ncomplete script graph, safety, and grounding checks after merge. The tool must\nnot become a broad retake in disguise: no title, roster, ordering, or unplanned\nline edits are permitted.\n\n## 23. Put the guarded artifact boundary behind every reauthoring route\n\nAn initial authoring pass is not the only place an artifact can regress. Blind\nlistener retakes, optional polish retakes, and final-audit retakes all create a\nfresh complete artifact and can reintroduce the same localized defect. Do not\nwire a bounded repair at only the first call site.\n\nFactor one guarded authoring helper that first accepts structural/safety-valid\noutput, invokes the narrow patch only for a localized full-contract failure,\nthen validates the merged canonical artifact. Declare the repair seam on every\npipeline pass that can traverse this helper and use pass-specific journal IDs so\nproduction receipts show where the correction occurred.\n\n## 24. Restoring an input is not authoring -- the \"lost anchor\" class\n\n(2026-07-13: this class cost twelve live rolls before it was named. Causal\nrecord: PBUG-20260713-15..18 in `PROD_BUG_LOG.md`; moved here from\nGO_FORWARD_PLAN in the 2026-07-15 baseline.)\n\nA pass hands an LLM an IMMUTABLE string Python already owns -- a constraint-draw\nfield, a dealt card, a locked speaker, a coordinate from an accepted artifact --\nand asks for it back verbatim. The model paraphrases. Python compares exactly\nand kills the episode over a copy of its own input.\n\nRestore when the correction is FORCED (exactly one value possible); return it to\nthe model when it is not. Three further laws proven live: a repair prompt that\ndoes not fit is worse than no repair (PROMPT_GUARD truncates the contract\nsilently); a bounded repair must ask for the unit the model can deliver (batch a\npatch and a partial success becomes a total failure); and \"it is broken\" is not\na repair prompt -- name the missing object, the unassigned clue, the exact\nstring.\n\n## 25. A bank lives in ~10 wired surfaces -- rip by the Teardown protocol\n\n(2026-07-18: the Sonnet bake-off retired 4 banks; the hard part was rediscovering where a bank is\nwired, not the decision. Runbook: the Teardown protocol in `SOURCE_BANK_PREFLIGHT.md`; proven by the\n`499386aa` roster trim and `docs/2026-07-18-rip-4-banks-plan.md`. 2026-07-31: the runner table left\nthe writer for `nodes/_otr_lane_specs.py` -- same surface, new address.)\n\nA source bank is not just its `banks.json` row -- it is wired across ~10 surfaces: the row, the pack\ndir, the `story_rules` file, the runner entry in `_otr_lane_specs.LANE_SPECS`, the pipeline object in\n`pipelines.json` (a SECOND registry -- the one hand-removal always forgets), the lane runner module, a\npossible `if base == \"<family>\"` route, the runnable<->executable registry law in `_otr_story_routing`,\nthe roster/bijection + bank-enumerating guard tests, and any PBUG the bank's live failures earned. Two\nrules that end the re-derivation: (1) removal DEPTH is set by whether a sibling version survives -- a\nvariant rip keeps the shared lane module, a full-family rip (the only version of its lane) deletes it;\n(2) retiring a bank that carried a live failure is a legitimate fix, but RECORD the PBUG -- ripping the\nlane must not rip the causal record. Gate on a green suite + retired-id absence, never a predicted count.\n\n## 26. Spoken craft gates repair and ship; they do not own episode liveness\n\nA craft or delivery-hygiene finding says that wording needs repair; it does not\nprove the ledger is structurally unrenderable. Spend the existing authored\nrepair, then a lower-temperature gate-specific repair, then the alternate\nwriter slot. If the model remains stubborn, apply a bounded deterministic SFW\nfloor, re-run every applicable detector, and stamp the gate plus resolving rung.\n\nValidate the exact surface the voice engine will consume. Number and\nabbreviation expansion can make a canonical line fail one-breath or spoken-form\nchecks only after delivery normalization. Content-owned lanes must repair that\nprojection before rebuilding hashes, proofs, and seals; shared-ledger lanes need\na final row scour before freeze and another guard after readiness normalization.\n\nNever route craft exhaustion to an episode terminal disposition. A deterministic\nfloor that still yields empty or punctuation-only text creates a row-local\nmechanical failure. Genuine graph ambiguity and the G9 SFW/content-safety gate\nremain fail-closed.\n\n## 27. Classify cast identity, not lexical parts\n\nA cast label is an identity declaration, not a bag of forbidden words. Generic\nroles such as `THE TRAVELER`, `First Witch`, or `MEDICAL DOCTOR` must remain\nusable as visual nouns, while a personal label such as `Doctor Aris Thorne`\nmust still keep its meaningful name components out of an anonymous visual\nbrief. Articles, ordinals, and honorifics are grammar, not identities.\n\nUse two complementary projections from one bounded role classifier. Input\nanonymization maps a generic full label and its meaningful role noun to one\nstable placeholder, but never maps an article. Output validation permits those\ngeneric role forms. For personal labels, protect the full surface and meaningful\npersonal components while excluding titles and articles as standalone tokens.\nKeep the external reason code stable, but carry the exact matched surface\nprivately into typed repair so the model can correct the actual defect instead\nof guessing. If all bounded repairs genuinely fail, retain the explicit failed\nsentinel and deterministic non-authoring consumer defaults; do not silently\ninvent episode content.\n\n## 28. Capacity is part of a bounded artifact contract\n\nAn output-token request is normally a ceiling, so reducing it to the room left\nby the measured prompt is honest for an artifact that can be shorter. It is\nnot honest for a closed patch or complete typed artifact whose required rows\nmust all arrive. Mark that call explicitly as requiring the full requested\noutput, capture the marker before any message normalization, and reject before\nmodel generation or network I/O when either the real context window or a\nprovider output cap is smaller.\n\nDo not solve a row-local quality finding by re-emitting a complete script.\nDerive a closed target set, give the writer all voiced rows as read-only\ncontext, request only target-line replacements, merge only the owned text\nfield, and run the complete artifact validator. A null reviewer coordinate may\nwiden to all voiced rows; an invented non-null coordinate is noise and must not\nwiden ownership.\n\nQuality work never owns episode liveness. Rejudge every successful patch, but\nafter both available writer slots fail, keep the best already-valid script and\nstop that loop. Never restore an unchanged artifact and immediately ask the\nsame judge for the same repair again. Build hashes, seals, ledger rows,\nreadiness state, and media/publication pointers only after the final accepted or\nfloored artifact so no downstream consumer can inherit a stale identity.\n\n## 29. Reconcile rendered manifests before timeline ownership\n\nA bank may author cues in its own compact vocabulary, but every renderer and\nconsumer needs one durable ledger contract. Translate producer-local IDs and\nplacements at the producer boundary. Legacy banks that author only sentinel\nrows still need real `music[]` rows once a renderer has produced a manifest;\nmaterialize those rows before the first timeline writer, not in a later\nassembler that can no longer recover an interstitial's position.\n\nTreat the manifest as rendered evidence, not permission to overwrite authored\nintent. Recompute cue-spec identity from the ledger's authored prompt,\nduration, placement, and anchor; only then may manifest paths and downstream\ntiming attach. A mismatch must stop that join loudly. For synthesized cues,\nderive the same deterministic identity from the manifest-owned spec and bind\navailable ordered sentinels so the new row is equally auditable.\n\nAnchors describe insertion order, not line type. Insert an interstitial before\neither a dedicated music sentinel or an ordinary dialogue row, then continue\nnormal dialogue dispatch so voice-bus counts remain exact. Let the final audio\nassembler alone mint visual mirror lines from canonical placement. At the\npost-audio wire join, prove episode identity again, merge render-owned music\nfields only on cue identity, and forward only mirrors whose cue passed that\njoin. Music may remain an optional creative bus, but when it renders its ledger\naccounting cannot be optional.\n\n## 30. Move serialized identity with a renamed artifact tree\n\nRenaming a directory moves bytes, not the absolute paths serialized inside its\nledger. Treat a pending-to-final rename as one transaction: move the directory,\ncanonicalize the ledger filename, recursively rebase only absolute string\nvalues contained by the old root, atomically persist the durable ledger, and\nonly then advance in-memory identity. Use path-component containment rather than\ntext prefixing, and leave external models, shared caches, source media, and OBS\ndestinations untouched. Make the transformation idempotent so a retry can\nresume after the directory moved but before the ledger save completed.\n\nA keyed merge does not preserve producer-owned rows that exist only on disk.\nAdd a narrow row join for each such producer instead of copying arbitrary stale\ncontent. Prove the immutable run receipt first; then prove the parent object,\nrecompute its authored hash on both sides, validate producer-specific timing\nand role fields, and reject duplicates. If either side has a freeze receipt,\nboth must carry the same value; episode-name fallback belongs only to two truly\nlegacy records with no receipt.\n\nPost-rename consumers must join through the active durable owner, never through\nthe newest sibling by modification time. Where a stale pre-rename value is\nalready on a wire, add an explicit graph dependency on rename completion and\nlet same-receipt durable identity replace stale episode/path fields. Consumer\nrescues should validate the durable episode against its directory and use the\nfreeze receipt whenever the caller carries it. A render is not qualified until\nthe final ledger can locate every asset without referring to the retired root.\n\n## 31. Derived ledger metrics need one lexical owner and one final boundary\n\nWord count is not `len(text.split())` once authored dialogue contains em dashes,\nsmart apostrophes, or punctuation glued for performance. Pick one explicit\nlexical contract and put it in a dependency-free leaf that every producer,\nrepair pass, editor, readiness normalizer, aggregate writer, and freeze auditor\ncan import. A text mutation is one atomic operation over `text`, `char_count`,\nand `word_count`; never rely on a later caller remembering the other two.\n\nThe durable save is still the ownership backstop. Re-derive row metrics from\ncanonical text before rolling them into cast, scene, root, and role-specific\nmeta totals, and reset empty aggregates instead of leaving values from a prior\ncomposition. Preserve the incoming audit before self-healing so production\ndiagnostics still identify the offending producer. After the last legal text\nmutator, refresh derived metrics once more before the final freeze audit.\n\nCounts are derived state, not authorship. A count-only refresh must not rewrite\ncanonical text, delivery projections, accepted-line hashes, content-authorship\nreceipts, or seals. Enforce the boundary mechanically: scan production modules\nfor direct ledger-text assignments so a new sibling writer cannot silently\nreintroduce split ownership.\n\n## 32. Transport only model-owned fields across a bounded LLM context\n\nAn LLM should not reserialize a compiler-owned graph merely to author its leaf\ntext. Every repeated ID, parent, speaker, boundary, cue, neutral default, and\nscene field spends output tokens, creates a fresh drift opportunity, and is\nusually echoed again beside the failed artifact during typed repair. A response\ncan therefore be semantically small yet impossible to complete inside the\nmodel's real context and output limits.\n\nDefine the wire artifact at the authoring boundary, not at the downstream\nstorage boundary. Give the model a closed list of stable IDs and only the prose\nfields it owns. Require exact unique ID coverage, map by ID rather than response\norder or fuzzy matching, and compile every mechanically derivable field from\nthe already accepted graph. Then run the complete downstream validator against\nthe compiled artifact; a compact transport must not become a validation bypass.\n\nRepairs need the same discipline. Reinject a complete failed compact draft only\nwhen it parsed, plus the smallest trusted authority needed to correct it. Drop a\nmalformed prefix instead of paying context to repeat truncation. Require both\nthe prompt and the full output reservation to fit before inference. A liveness\nladder should be finite and flat: a bounded same-slot ladder, then at most one\nfresh independent slot, followed by an explicit deterministic floor only for\nadvisory craft defects. Record every attempt and terminal exhaustion truthfully.\nProve the maximum supported input and repair envelopes with the exact production\ntokenizer, not a character estimate or a smaller surrogate prompt.\n\n## 33. Separate render work from positioned timeline ownership\n\nFull per-shot frame requests describe render work, not necessarily final video\nduration. Once a post-audio ledger positions rows, opening, interstitial, and\nclosing cues may intentionally overlap dialogue at crossfades. Summing those\nrequests counts the overlap twice and creates body drift that a strict terminal\nmux should reject.\n\nCarry two explicit quantities: the sum of full render requests for workload and\nthe authoritative positioned timeline boundary for output. Quantize the accepted\naudio-ledger duration upward to a complete CFR frame. In the planner, sort rows\nstably by position and give a row only the visible interval before the next row\nowns the boundary. End that interval at the earliest of its requested end, the\nnext start, or the timeline end. This trims overlap without stretching a short\nclip across a genuine gap; equal-start collisions must be loud.\n\nQuality telemetry must compare rendered frames with the planned visible slot,\nnot blindly with the original render request. Preserve requested, rendered,\nvisible, and overlap-trimmed counts so intentional edits do not masquerade as\nengine underruns. A filesystem master probe is a cross-check and fallback: it\nmay shrink or grow a positioned timeline, while an unpositioned legacy sequence\nkeeps its historical grow-only behavior. Never widen mux tolerance or charge\nbody drift to a valid credits declaration.\n\n## 34. Explicit delivery length is a producer contract, not a quality opinion\n\nA requested word count can allow an inclusive tolerance without becoming\noptional. Express the tolerance once as integer bounds, persist the target,\nbounds, owner, canonical count, and exact character-text hash, and require every\nproducer family to finish inside that same contract before its final artifact is\nsealed. A 180-word request accepts 163..200 words; the same law accepts 289..356\nfor 320. Floating ratios are planning aids, not the final delivery authority.\n\nLength repair must be small, fresh, and progressive. Target one owned spoken row\nat a time, alternate creative and technical writer slots, validate the complete\ncandidate, and retain only a strict move toward the band. A malformed call, an\nunchanged valid response, or one bad sibling row must not discard prior accepted\nprogress or consume the remaining dynamic budget. Reject padding, repetition,\nfake commercials or products, invented numeric claims, markup, stage directions,\nand new visual/canon claims. Subjective taste remains fail-open; measurable\ndelivery exhaustion raises a typed error before hashes, readiness, or media.\n\n**Amended 2026-07-30 (A-4).** \"Exhaustion raises a typed error\" is the end of\nthe ladder, not the first thing that goes wrong on it. A capacity failure now\ncarries a PHASE and only one of the two is terminal: a PRE-CALL refusal, where\nthe measured prompt leaves no room for the artifact, is deterministic and stays\nloud on the spot; a call that RAN and used its whole output allowance without\nstopping is stochastic and advances the ladder instead. The typed error is what\nthe ladder raises when it has actually spent its attempts.\n\nPlace each adapter at its last safe authoring boundary. Content-owned lanes fit\nand rebuild their own proofs and hashes before assembly. Shared inline lanes fit\nafter story QA and spoken hygiene, re-scour the accepted rows, then stamp the\nactual receipt and build reflections from those exact final rows. Preserve older\npass receipts as history instead of relabeling a later repair as an earlier pass.\nAfter the final readiness normalization, freeze performs a read-only hash-bound\nrecount before video readiness. A miss becomes `needs_full_rerun`; freeze never\nauthors prose or mutates a content-owned seal.\n\n## 35. Candidate exhaustion is not episode exhaustion\n\nA bounded model call is healthy; a bounded episode-wide output ladder is not.\nKeep each repair attempt and each producer candidate finite, but separate that\nlocal liveness from the episode's acceptance state. Four consecutive calls that\nmake no strict distance progress retire the current candidate. They do not make\nthe requested episode impossible, and an LLM verdict never acquires that power.\n\nEscalation is explicit: repair one owned row, try the alternate writer slot, then\ndiscard the whole candidate and ask one producer to author a fresh complete\ncandidate. Alternate the producer priority on successive rerolls.\nFreshness must be model-observable: when two logical slots resolve to one seeded\nbackend, vary a producer-owned prompt nonce or another validated generation input\nso Candidate N cannot replay Candidate N-2 byte for byte forever.\nPreserve only candidate-local diagnostics from discarded work; never let its text,\nseal, hash,\nreadiness, or subjective score become authoritative. There is no fixed outer\nmodel-output ceiling. Temporary provider failure stays pending, retryable, and\nnon-ready until a legal candidate arrives or the operator cancels.\n\nOnly deterministic impossibility may fail loud: invalid configuration, a graph\nwhose declared capacity cannot reach the band, corrupt schema ownership, or a\nmechanical safety violation.\n\n**Amended 2026-07-30 (A-4): this doctrine was right and the code did not\nimplement it.** The rule \"only deterministic impossibility may fail loud\" now\nhas a name in the transport -- a capacity failure carries a PHASE, and\n`prompt_no_room` (the pre-call refusal, deterministic arithmetic) is the only\none that is terminal. `output_limit` -- the call ran and used its whole output\nallowance without reaching a stop condition -- is the stochastic case this\nlesson always meant to protect, and it advances the ladder. Before A-4 it\nescaped as an unhandled error on attempt 1 of 3, which is how a three-call\nbudget spent one call and killed three legs of the live 45-word campaign. Two\nthings keep it honest: the phase lives in the module that owns capacity\narithmetic, so the transport and the retry policy cannot hold two opinions\nabout one failure; and being CATCHABLE is not being RETRYABLE -- both phases\nare caught at the attempt boundary and the deterministic one is re-raised\nuntouched.\n\nThe final in-band ledger recount and text hash are\nthe acceptance judge. Subjective quality remains fail-open, and audio, video,\ncaptions, credits, mux, and publication stay downstream of the hard final stamp.\nNever manufacture prose, facts, products, advertisements, or numeric claims to\nsatisfy the counter.\n\n## 36. A rejected fiction candidate is not a damaged episode ledger\n\n**Operator ruling 2026-07-30.** On the canonical Sci-Fi route, a recoverable\nJSON, schema, content-validation, safety, or output-limit defect exhausts and\nretires only that finite candidate ladder. The producer then requests a fresh\ncomplete model-authored candidate, with cancellation as the operator-controlled\nstop. There is no fixed outer model-output ceiling and no deterministic canned,\nsummary, drop, or patch-in-place story floor. This is the controlling amendment\nto conflicting readings of Lessons 3, 11, 26, 34, and 35.\n\nThe source article is evidence and inspiration, not a fictional continuity\ncontract. Accepted characters, events, dialogue, and plot may diverge completely\nfrom the article or any discarded draft. Claims presented as factual and the\nfactual coda still resolve to validated source evidence. The accepted canonical\nspoken artifact is the only prose admitted to the ledger; rejected prose, hashes,\nseals, and readiness state never become authoritative.\n\nComplete source access is a coverage problem, not a clipping permission. Preserve\nthe exact selected body and its route/index/count/UTF-8 byte count/hash receipt.\nWhen it exceeds one P0 context, use overlapping windows whose union covers the\ncomplete normalized source, validate locally, rebase exact offsets, merge\ndeterministically, and validate again against the complete A0.\n\nAssemble and stamp the production ledger once, from the accepted canonical\nartifact. The final line identity, graph, safety, recount, authorship, freeze, and\nhash checks remain strict corruption and downstream-consumer guards; they do not\nprove fidelity to an abandoned work of fiction. Cancellation and deterministic\nconfiguration, source/security, provider, I/O, compiler, ownership, graph,\nfreeze, and proof failures remain loud. A raw unsafe candidate is retryable;\nunsafe text that somehow remains after acceptance is an invariant failure.\n\n\n## 37. Prove the control reaches the code path before blaming the control\n\nA reproduction that varies a knob proves nothing until the knob is shown to\nREACH the failing lane. The 2026-08-11 record concluded \"forcing the LEMMY\ncameo kills the `scifi_news_pro` writer\" from a clean-looking matrix -- two\nforced legs died, one natural leg passed. On 2026-08-16 the attribution was\nwithdrawn: at that very commit the lane dispatch returned BEFORE the force\nvalue was computed, the runner took no cameo argument, and the module\ncontained zero occurrences of the string. The widget was inert on that lane.\nThe matrix was three independent stochastic draws of a control connected to\nnothing, and the real cause was ordinary writer markup non-compliance.\n\nDo this before attributing any failure to a setting:\n\n- trace the setting from its widget to the failing function and name the\n  line where it is consumed; if the lane returns before that line, the\n  setting is not in play;\n- read the actual leg log for the setting's own name -- the 2026-08-11 logs\n  contained ZERO occurrences of \"lemmy\" and would have refuted the\n  attribution on day one;\n- treat a 2-of-3 outcome split on an unmeasured baseline as coincidence\n  until the baseline failure rate is known.\n\nA wrong attribution is worse than an open bug: it fences off a design (here,\nthe cameo roll) for months on evidence that never supported it.\n\n## 38. A retired name reused elsewhere makes an absence look like a regression\n\n`scifi_news` shipped an empty cameo contract, the operator remembered the\ncameo working, and the log recorded a REGRESSION. The archaeology says\notherwise: the lane that ran the cameo was `science_news` (inline\n`lock_cast`), retired 2026-07-17; two days later a rename moved the NAME\n`scifi_news` onto a different, runner-dispatched lane that never had the\nfeature. A case-insensitive pickaxe over BOTH runners' full history returns\nEMPTY -- no commit ever added or removed the string.\n\nThe operator's memory was correct at the PRODUCT level and the log was wrong\nat the LANE level, and both statements had to be recorded to describe\nreality. Before writing \"regression\" about a missing capability:\n\n- `git log --follow --pickaxe-regex -S \"<feature-string>\" -- <file>` over the\n  suspect module; an EMPTY result means the file never had it;\n- check for a rename or retirement near the date the capability \"vanished\"\n  (`git log --diff-filter=RA --name-status`, and the roster/registry\n  history);\n- separate PRODUCT-level loss (real, worth fixing) from LANE-level\n  regression (may never have existed). The repair is the same; the framing\n  decides whether anyone hunts a nonexistent breaking commit.\n\n## 39. A diagnostic that coalesces absence into a default destroys the finding\n\nThe bug log recorded `cast_contract: {}` on two frozen ledgers, and the\ndefect was written up as an EMPTY contract. Reading the artifacts directly\nshowed the key was ABSENT -- the `{}` came from the probe's own\n`meta.get(\"cast_contract\") or {}`. The distinction was the entire defect:\n\"field never written\" is exactly what the one-stable-shape rule exists to\nprevent, and the probe erased it.\n\nProbes that inspect a field's PRESENCE must not normalize. Use\n`key in obj` and report absent / empty / populated as three outcomes, and\nquote the raw value in the bug entry. The same rule applies to any consumer\nthat reads with a default and then reports what it read.\n\n## 40. When asking \"did it get better,\" blind the judge\n\nTwo questions this project could not answer with metrics -- \"are the stories\nbetter than the last bake-off\" and \"which bank tells the best stories\" --\nwere answered by handing a reader unlabelled transcripts and asking it to\nFIND the grouping. It recovered the era split 10/10 and the six-bank\ngrouping 6/6, which is what makes the rankings credible; a judge told which\ngroup is \"new\" cannot produce that evidence.\n\nThe method, reusable as-is (`scratchpad/make_transcripts.py`,\n`bank_transcripts.py`):\n\n- extract spoken lines only, so the judge reads a STORY and not a ledger;\n- equalize the confound -- sample comparable lengths, or the judge sorts by\n  size rather than craft;\n- withhold the mapping in a separate key file the judge is told not to open;\n- ask it to discover the split and state its confidence BEFORE the reveal;\n- accept \"no meaningful difference\" as a valid, useful answer.\n\nTwo independent instruments agreeing (the deterministic structure score and\nthe blind read ranked the same bank first and the same bank last) is\nstronger evidence than either alone. And a scoring instrument must stay\ntelemetry: THE LAW forbids a score becoming a gate, however good it looks.\n\n## 41. A rename reorders test collection, and that surfaces latent pollution\n\nRenaming `test_fable2_*.py` to `test_scifi_news_pro_*.py` moved those files\nalphabetically past `test_perfect_run_spacesaver_deprecated_noop.py`, and one\ntest began failing in the full suite while passing in isolation. Nothing the\nrename touched was broken. The polluter had been latent for months:\n\n```python\nsys.modules.pop(\"nodes.OTR_LedgerScriptWriter\", None)   # then re-import\n```\n\nThat installs a SECOND writer module for the rest of the session. A later\ntest does `import nodes.OTR_LedgerScriptWriter as m; monkeypatch.setattr(m,\n...)` and then invokes a writer class captured from the OTHER module object,\nso the patch silently does nothing and the spy never fires.\n\n- **Never pop-and-re-import a production module in a test.** If the goal is\n  \"avoid a stale singleton\", ask what the test actually reads -- this one only\n  needed `INPUT_TYPES` off the class, so the re-import bought nothing and cost\n  a session-wide hazard. Restoring `sys.modules` afterwards is NOT sufficient\n  and was tried first: other references captured during the second import\n  outlive the restore.\n- **A test that passes alone and fails in the suite is an ORDERING bug, not a\n  flake.** Bisect the alphabetical range the file moved across\n  (`pytest <range> <victim>`), then bisect within the polluting file to the\n  single test. That is four commands and beats any amount of theorising.\n- **Renames are a legitimate way to FIND these.** The pollution was real\n  before the rename; only the ordering hid it. Expect one or two when\n  renaming a test family, and treat them as found bugs, not rename fallout.\n\n## 42. A regression guard must not swallow the traceback\n\n`tests/conftest.py`'s known-fail guard ends with `raise SystemExit(2)` inside\n`pytest_sessionfinish` so CI can distinguish a regression from an ordinary\nfailure. That exception aborts the remaining `sessionfinish` hooks --\nINCLUDING the terminal reporter's -- so pytest never prints the `FAILURES`\nsection. Every regression in this repo reports as a bare nodeid with NO\ntraceback, which is precisely the information needed to fix it. Measured\n2026-08-16: a failing run produced 27 lines of output and zero assertion\ndetail.\n\n- To read a traceback today: temporarily neutralize the `raise`, run, then\n  restore it. Marking the hook `trylast=True` helps ordering but did NOT by\n  itself restore the report in testing -- treat the decorator as a partial\n  improvement and the temporary neutralize as the reliable diagnostic.\n- The durable fix is to set the exit status without raising, or to emit the\n  guard's verdict from a hook that runs after the terminal summary. Not yet\n  done; recorded so the next window does not rediscover the blindness.\n- General rule: **a guard that hides evidence costs more than the failure it\n  guards.** Any hook that raises must be checked against what else the\n  framework still had to do.\n\n## Sprint receipt\n\nRecord this at the end of every production sprint:\n\n```text\nSPRINT RECEIPT: PASS | FAIL\nscope:\nauthoritative_writers:\ndurable_artifacts:\ncanonical_workflow_hash:\nfocused_tests:\nfull_suite:\nbug_bible:\nmodel_pairings:\n30_word_receipts:\n120_word_receipts:\n720_word_receipts:\nlive_ledgers:\npublished_assets:\nprod_bug_entries:\nhead:\norigin:\nremaining_risks:\n```\n\r\n===== kibitz-runs\\2026-08-22-2026-08-22-ghost-prompt-v21\\r2\\input.md =====\r\n# Driver anchor -- Ghost Prompt v2.1, one scoped round\n\n**Driver:** Claude (Cowork), sole judge. **Round:** ONE, at the operator's\ninstruction -- this is NOT a four-round arc and must not be reported as one.\n**Repo:** `C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI\\custom_nodes\\ComfyUI-OldTimeRadio`,\nbranch `v2.0-alpha`, HEAD `d1ab7037`.\n\nEverything below is checkable against the real Windows files. Check it. I want\nthe claims that are wrong named, not agreed with.\n\n## What happened, in order\n\n1. **Ghost Prompt v2 shipped** (`a8fad82c`). The Ghost Signal video lane\n   (AnimateDiff SD1.5) had a real text defect: `resolve_action()` copied the\n   first six regex words of an unmapped free-text `beat_intent` after the\n   literal `moves with`, which put CAST NAMES in the picture and ended\n   mid-clause. Proven against a published render and hash-matched 8/8 from the\n   pre-change composer.\n2. v2 replaced it with \"controlled abstraction\": a Python-owned recurrence\n   motif plus ONE short LLM-authored drawable leaf per beat, three\n   representations (`figure`/`object`/`signal`), the model never seeing\n   dialogue, title, M4 wall, cast prose or names.\n3. **Every gate passed and the pictures got worse.** Suite 12225 green, token\n   window measured, control vector exact, three episodes published. The\n   operator watched them and said the AnimateDiff output had tanked.\n4. **He was right.** Counting recognisable subjects at frame 40 of every beat:\n   v1 archival 4/4 sampled; v2 archival with LLM leaves 2/8; v2 deterministic\n   0/4; v2 anime 0/8. The two v2 survivors are the only beats whose leaf named\n   a real object (a lantern, a radio dial).\n5. **v2.1** (`f6075592`, `633e68a7`) put concrete nouns back. Re-rendered with\n   real LLM leaves: **6/8 recognisable, 2 human figures**.\n\n## The claim I want attacked\n\n**Legibility tracks concrete nouns, and nothing else.** v1 handed SD1.5\n`\"a man, a broad steady figure, a charcoal coat, holding a folded chart\"`.\nv2 handed it `\"charcoal ledger emblem\"` plus\n`\"an abstract signal field filling the composition\"`. A 512x288 SD1.5 obliges by\npainting texture.\n\nv2.1 therefore: mode laws name a physical thing; the motif is the prop itself\n(`a charcoal lantern`, not `charcoal lantern emblem`); `figure` says FIGURE and\nwears a garment; bookends name real radio hardware; `GHOST_CHARACTER_CYCLE` is\n`figure/object/figure/signal` so half the character beats show a person; a leaf\nnaming a texture (static, waveform, gradient, noise, grain) is rejected.\n\n**Is that diagnosis right, or did something else cause the regression?**\nAlternatives I have NOT ruled out and want tested: the prompts also got SHORTER\n(208-317 chars down to 164-203); the negative prompt is unchanged but its\nrelative weight against a shorter positive is not; the mode laws add three\ntrailing clauses that may dilute the subject; `video_art` and `anime` packs may\nsimply be abstract by design and unfairly counted.\n\n## Read these\n\n* `nodes/_otr_video_engines/ghost_signal_author.py` -- `GHOST_CHARACTER_CYCLE`,\n  `motif_for_character`, `_ABSTRACT_SUBJECT_WORDS`, `_HUMAN_WORDS`,\n  `validate_drawable_beat`, `GHOST_FALLBACK_CLAUSES`, `GHOST_BATCH_RULES`.\n* `nodes/_otr_video_engines/ghost_signal_prompt.py` -- `GHOST_MODE_LAWS_V2`,\n  `compose_ghost_prompt_v2`.\n* `docs/2026-08-22-ghost-prompt-v2-publish-receipt.md` section 4A.\n\n## Specific questions\n\n1. **`_HUMAN_WORDS` was cut on live evidence.** It carried\n   hand/arm/shoulder and rejected `\"the silver ledger sits on a desk as a clock\n   hand ticks\"` -- a CLOCK hand -- killing all eight beats twice because one bad\n   leaf rejects the whole batch. I removed body parts and kept\n   person/people/man/woman/boy/girl/child/crowd/figure/silhouette/face/portrait/\n   someone. **Is that set now too permissive for `object`/`signal`?** \"a\n   shoulder passes through the light\" now passes in object mode. Is that right?\n2. **Whole-batch rejection.** One bad leaf costs all eight beats and two\n   generations. It is deliberate (no partial salvage across attempts, so an\n   episode's prompts are not a function of how many tries each row took). Given\n   it has now twice dropped an episode to fallback over ONE leaf, is the\n   trade still correct, or should a single row be re-asked?\n3. **`GHOST_CHARACTER_CYCLE` is period 4 with `figure` twice.** Check the\n   collision-correction interaction in `schedule_ghost_modes`: bookends\n   alternate `object`/`signal` from their own offset and only a BOOKEND is ever\n   flipped. Can any seed produce a run of three, or starve `figure` below half?\n4. **The abstract-word reject list.** `static`, `waveform`, `gradient`,\n   `texture`, `noise`, `grain`, `geometry`, `scanlines`, `interference`. Does\n   any of these have a legitimate concrete use a beat would want -- \"grain\" in\n   an archival pack, say -- and would rejecting it cost a good leaf?\n5. **Anything in `ghost_signal_author.py` that is simply a bug.** Hash key set,\n   parser, tokenizer measurement, fallback collision probing.\n\nBe specific, cite file:line, and prefer \"I could not verify X\" to a confident\nguess. Do not restate the design back to me.\n\r\n===== .kibitz\\comfyui.local.md =====\r\n# Local ComfyUI Profile for Kibitz\n\nGenerated: `2026-08-20T20:18:16-07:00`\nRepo: `C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI\\custom_nodes\\ComfyUI-OldTimeRadio`\n\nThis is the compact form of the profile generated by\n`kibitz/scripts/comfyui_profile.py`. The generator's raw package scan included\nold `.claude/worktrees` and exceeded the Windows process argument limit, so only\ncurrent, review-relevant facts are retained here.\n\n## Hardware and runtime\n\n- OS: Windows 11; Python 3.12.11.\n- GPU: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5080 Laptop GPU, 16303 MiB physical VRAM.\n- Project review gate: 14.5 GiB.\n- ComfyUI root: `C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI`.\n- Production headless port: `127.0.0.1:8000`.\n- Live output base: `C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI\\output`.\n- Live published episodes: `C:\\Users\\jeffr\\Documents\\ComfyUI\\output\\otr\\obs`.\n- The separate ComfyUI-Installs `otr\\obs` tree is historical and must not be\n  moved, deleted, or accepted as the live publish receipt.\n\n## Canonical workflow\n\n- Source of truth: `workflows/otr_canonical.json`.\n- Current shape: 23 nodes, 57 links, 140 widget slots.\n- Any node/input/widget/wiring change must land in this exact JSON in the same\n  change as code.\n- `widgets_values` is positional. Append new optional widgets; do not insert\n  into the middle of existing values.\n- Every production API/headless/soak acceptance run must load the canonical\n  workflow. Bench graphs cannot qualify a shipping path.\n- Required post-edit gates: JSON round-trip, `OTR_WorkflowValidator`, widget\n  count versus live `INPUT_TYPES`, wired input-name checks, link integrity, and\n  live `/object_info` class checks.\n\n## LTX 2.5 review facts\n\n- Production CPU-pins the LTX text encoder via\n  `nodes/_otr_video_engines/eng_ltx25.py`; lab GPU-residency measurements must\n  not be transferred into production without re-proof.\n- The settled operator decision is to ship the two-stage quality path. Do not\n  re-run the visual, grid-score, VRAM-arm, or face-drift comparisons.\n- Acceptance must publish through the canonical workflow to the live `otr/obs`\n  tree and positively prove stage two executed.\n- Positive proof must use the loader/node's own log evidence: the latent\n  upsampler ran per shot and VAE decode occurred at the upscaled canvas, not\n  832x480. Adapter self-reporting is insufficient.\n- Real production cost materially above 2.6x is a hard stop requiring operator\n  approval.\n- Reset selectively before every headless run. Never blanket-kill Python\n  processes; confirm port 8000 is free and GPU memory is at desktop baseline.\n\n## Reviewer discipline\n\n- Read the real Windows repo files and cite exact file:line evidence.\n- Treat any unverified runtime or VRAM statement as `verify:` rather than fact.\n- Check node registration, `INPUT_TYPES`, return contracts, tensor layout,\n  caching, model residency, workflow wiring, and acceptance instrumentation as\n  one integrated change.\n- Ignore `.claude/worktrees`, generated graphs, and stale workflow copies.\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command '$targets = @('\"'nodes\\\\_otr_video_engines\\\\ghost_signal_author.py','nodes\\\\_otr_video_engines\\\\ghost_signal_prompt.py','docs\\\\2026-08-22-ghost-prompt-v2-publish-receipt.md'); foreach (\"'$f in $targets) { \"===== $f =====\"; $n=0; Get-Content -LiteralPath $f | ForEach-Object { $n++; '\"'{0,5}: {1}' -f \"'$n, $_ } }; \"===== SEARCH =====\"; rg -n --glob '\"'\"'!'\"\\\\.claude/worktrees/**' \\\"ghost prompt|Ghost Prompt|ghost_signal|drawable leaf|whole.batch|GHOST_CHARACTER_CYCLE|_ABSTRACT_SUBJECT_WORDS|schedule_ghost_modes\\\" docs tests nodes workflows\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command '$targets = @('\"'nodes\\\\_otr_video_engines\\\\ghost_signal_author.py','nodes\\\\_otr_video_engines\\\\ghost_signal_prompt.py','docs\\\\2026-08-22-ghost-prompt-v2-publish-receipt.md'); foreach (\"'$f in $targets) { \"===== $f =====\"; $n=0; Get-Content -LiteralPath $f | ForEach-Object { $n++; '\"'{0,5}: {1}' -f \"'$n, $_ } }; \"===== SEARCH =====\"; rg -n --glob '\"'\"'!'\"\\\\.claude/worktrees/**' \\\"ghost prompt|Ghost Prompt|ghost_signal|drawable leaf|whole.batch|GHOST_CHARACTER_CYCLE|_ABSTRACT_SUBJECT_WORDS|schedule_ghost_modes\\\" docs tests nodes workflows\"","aggregated_output":"    1: \"\"\"``ghost_signal_author`` -- the Ghost Prompt v2 AUTHORING surface.\r\n    2: \r\n    3: Prompt v1 asked a deterministic composer to turn a beat into a picture, and on\r\n    4: an unmapped free-text ``beat_intent`` it did the only thing a regex can do: it\r\n    5: copied the first six words and prefixed ``moves with``. That is how a published\r\n    6: lane came to emit *\"moves with erin risks exposure by transmitting a\"* -- a cast\r\n    7: name in the picture, a sentence with no end, and no visual idea in it at all.\r\n    8: \r\n    9: Prompt v2 replaces that slice with CONTROLLED ABSTRACTION. Exactly one short\r\n   10: drawable leaf is authored per beat by the already-selected writer model, and\r\n   11: Python owns everything else: the style cue, the negative, the recurrence motif,\r\n   12: the representation mode, the framing law, the identities, the hashes, the retry\r\n   13: and the fallback. The model is handed no dialogue, no title, no M4 scene wall,\r\n   14: no raw cast prose and no names -- not by policy but by CONSTRUCTION, because\r\n   15: none of those are parameters of the request this module builds.\r\n   16: \r\n   17: WHAT LIVES HERE AND WHY IT IS NOT IN ``ghost_signal_prompt``. That module is\r\n   18: PURE by contract -- no I/O, no lazy loader, no tokenizer -- and it stays that\r\n   19: way. This module owns the two things that are not pure: the banana route (a\r\n   20: transform whose gate reads the environment) and the installed SD1 tokenizer (a\r\n   21: lazy import of ComfyUI's own encoder). It never loads an LLM: ShotLock owns\r\n   22: orchestration and hands the generate function in. It never imports the render\r\n   23: driver, the registry or the model loader, so there is no cycle.\r\n   24: \r\n   25: THREE INVARIANTS THE REST OF THE LANE DEPENDS ON:\r\n   26: \r\n   27: 1. **The leaf is the only model-owned field.** A response that tries to set the\r\n   28:    mode, the motif, the style or an id is rejected whole. There is no partial\r\n   29:    salvage across attempts -- one invalid batch retries once, atomically, and a\r\n   30:    second failure receives a complete deterministic batch.\r\n   31: 2. **The request hash is the replay identity.** Thirteen keys, compact sorted\r\n   32:    JSON, and the template digest among them -- so changing the wording of the\r\n   33:    prompt, its temperature or its output budget invalidates every stored leaf\r\n   34:    rather than silently replaying text authored under different instructions.\r\n   35: 3. **A measured window, not an asserted one.** ComfyUI's SD1 encoder CHUNKS an\r\n   36:    overlength prompt rather than dropping it, so 77 is a salience choice, not a\r\n   37:    transport cliff. It is enforced by asking the installed tokenizer, never by\r\n   38:    a whitespace estimate -- and production fails closed when that tokenizer\r\n   39:    cannot be reached rather than guessing.\r\n   40: \"\"\"\r\n   41: from __future__ import annotations\r\n   42: \r\n   43: import hashlib\r\n   44: import json\r\n   45: import logging\r\n   46: import os\r\n   47: import re\r\n   48: from typing import Callable, Optional\r\n   49: \r\n   50: _LOG = logging.getLogger(\"OTR.video.ghost_signal_author\")\r\n   51: \r\n   52: try:  # pragma: no cover -- exercised by both import shapes in the suite\r\n   53:     from . import ghost_signal_prompt as _gsp\r\n   54: except ImportError:  # pragma: no cover -- flat test imports\r\n   55:     import ghost_signal_prompt as _gsp  # type: ignore\r\n   56: \r\n   57: \r\n   58: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n   59: # Identity. Every constant here is pinned by a test.\r\n   60: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n   61: \r\n   62: #: The stored object's schema. Bumped only when the FIELD SET changes.\r\n   63: GHOST_AUTHOR_SCHEMA_VERSION = 1\r\n   64: \r\n   65: #: The authoring contract's version. Bumped when the leaf's meaning changes;\r\n   66: #: it is one of the thirteen hashed request keys, so a bump reauthors.\r\n   67: GHOST_AUTHOR_VERSION = \"ghost_drawable_beat_v1\"\r\n   68: \r\n   69: #: The three coordinated representations. ``figure`` is the only one that shows\r\n   70: #: a body, and it shows a body rather than a likeness -- no face is requested\r\n   71: #: and none is promised.\r\n   72: GHOST_MODES = (\"figure\", \"object\", \"signal\")\r\n   73: \r\n   74: #: THE CHARACTER CYCLE, and it is deliberately NOT ``GHOST_MODES``.\r\n   75: #:\r\n   76: #: The first draft cycled the three modes evenly, so two of every three\r\n   77: #: character beats were non-figurative and an episode lost its people. Measured\r\n   78: #: against the v1 arm on the same seeds, that was the single biggest visible\r\n   79: #: regression -- the older arm rendered four clear human figures across its\r\n   80: #: eight beats and the new one rendered none.\r\n   81: #:\r\n   82: #: Period four, half of it ``figure``. That honours the operator's actual\r\n   83: #: directive -- *\"do not force the same mediocre person into every clip\"* --\r\n   84: #: without emptying the episode of people, and it still cannot produce a run of\r\n   85: #: three because no two adjacent entries are equal.\r\n   86: GHOST_CHARACTER_CYCLE = (\"figure\", \"object\", \"figure\", \"signal\")\r\n   87: \r\n   88: #: The two representations a bookend may take. A radio console is not a person.\r\n   89: GHOST_NON_FIGURE_MODES = (\"object\", \"signal\")\r\n   90: \r\n   91: #: How a stored leaf came to exist. There is no ``fallback`` boolean: a\r\n   92: #: deliberate deterministic result is ``deterministic_fallback`` plus a nonempty\r\n   93: #: reason, and reuse of one keeps BOTH -- so a replay can never launder a\r\n   94: #: fallback into proof eligibility.\r\n   95: GHOST_AUTHOR_SOURCES = (\"writer_llm\", \"replay\", \"deterministic_fallback\")\r\n   96: \r\n   97: #: Leaf shape. The model is TOLD 6--10 words; the hard bounds are wider so a\r\n   98: #: good answer one word outside the request is not thrown away.\r\n   99: GHOST_LEAF_MAX_CHARS = 96\r\n  100: GHOST_LEAF_MIN_WORDS = 5\r\n  101: GHOST_LEAF_MAX_WORDS = 14\r\n  102: GHOST_LEAF_ASK_WORDS_LOW = 8\r\n  103: GHOST_LEAF_ASK_WORDS_HIGH = 12\r\n  104: \r\n  105: #: One CLIP window, measured with the installed tokenizer including BOS/EOS.\r\n  106: GHOST_CLIP_WINDOW_TOKENS = 77\r\n  107: \r\n  108: #: What an AUTHOR-TIME candidate targets. Below the window on purpose: it\r\n  109: #: preserves the measured v1 worst case (63--69 tokens across all nine shipped\r\n  110: #: packs and all eight live shots) as real headroom for the banana route.\r\n  111: GHOST_AUTHOR_TOKEN_TARGET = 69\r\n  112: \r\n  113: #: THE MODEL ID STAMPED WHEN NO MODEL IS CONFIGURED AT ALL (a unit fixture or\r\n  114: #: a legacy local path). It is a real value rather than an empty string because\r\n  115: #: ``model_id`` is one of the thirteen hashed request keys and one of the\r\n  116: #: required stored fields -- and because a later live run with a real model\r\n  117: #: therefore hashes differently and REAUTHORS, which is exactly right: a\r\n  118: #: checked-in clause and a written one are not the same artifact.\r\n  119: #:\r\n  120: #: A CONFIGURED model that was asked and failed keeps its OWN id, so a receipt\r\n  121: #: still names the model whose answer was rejected.\r\n  122: GHOST_DETERMINISTIC_MODEL_ID = \"deterministic\"\r\n  123: \r\n  124: #: The generation contract. These three values are hashed into\r\n  125: #: :data:`GHOST_TEMPLATE_SHA256`, so changing any of them reauthors.\r\n  126: GHOST_BATCH_TEMPERATURE = 0.1\r\n  127: GHOST_BATCH_BASE_TOKENS = 64\r\n  128: GHOST_BATCH_PER_SHOT_TOKENS = 48\r\n  129: \r\n  130: \r\n  131: def batch_output_tokens(spec_count) -> int:\r\n  132:     \"\"\"The output budget for a batch of ``spec_count`` leaves.\"\"\"\r\n  133:     return int(GHOST_BATCH_BASE_TOKENS\r\n  134:                + GHOST_BATCH_PER_SHOT_TOKENS * int(spec_count))\r\n  135: \r\n  136: \r\n  137: class GhostAuthorError(ValueError):\r\n  138:     \"\"\"Base class for every refusal this module raises.\"\"\"\r\n  139: \r\n  140: \r\n  141: class GhostAuthorParseError(GhostAuthorError):\r\n  142:     \"\"\"The model's batch response is not the one accepted envelope.\"\"\"\r\n  143: \r\n  144: \r\n  145: class GhostAuthorValidationError(GhostAuthorError):\r\n  146:     \"\"\"A stored Ghost prompt object is absent, malformed or inconsistent.\"\"\"\r\n  147: \r\n  148: \r\n  149: class GhostTokenizerUnavailable(GhostAuthorError):\r\n  150:     \"\"\"The installed SD1 tokenizer could not be reached in production.\"\"\"\r\n  151: \r\n  152: \r\n  153: class GhostBudgetError(GhostAuthorError):\r\n  154:     \"\"\"A composed prompt exceeds a ceiling that is never trimmed to fit.\"\"\"\r\n  155: \r\n  156: \r\n  157: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  158: # The batch template. Its exact bytes are hashed, so edit it and every stored\r\n  159: # leaf reauthors -- which is the point: a leaf written under other instructions\r\n  160: # is not the same leaf.\r\n  161: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  162: \r\n  163: #: THE WORKED EXAMPLES EARN THEIR PLACE, and the live leg is why (2026-08-22).\r\n  164: #: With the rule text alone, Mistral-Nemo answered in a perfectly valid envelope\r\n  165: #: carrying four-word abstractions -- \"signal oscillates, broadcast begins\",\r\n  166: #: \"silhouette shreds papers, tension builds\" -- which the validator rejected on\r\n  167: #: both attempts, so the whole batch fell to the deterministic pools. A word\r\n  168: #: COUNT is a number a model does not feel; a sentence AT the target length is\r\n  169: #: one it can match. The counter-examples are named too, because \"tension\r\n  170: #: builds\" is not a thing a picture can show and saying so beats hoping.\r\n  171: #:\r\n  172: #: The example objects sit deliberately OUTSIDE the motif allowlists (a shutter,\r\n  173: #: a gauge and a spool are not in ``MOTIF_PROP_WORDS``), so a model that copies\r\n  174: #: one is visibly copying rather than producing something that could pass for a\r\n  175: #: real recurrence motif.\r\n  176: GHOST_BATCH_EXAMPLES = (\r\n  177:     \"GOOD, and match this length:\\n\"\r\n  178:     \"  mode=object   -> the shutter tilts on the desk as a shadow crosses \"\r\n  179:     \"its slats\\n\"\r\n  180:     \"  mode=signal   -> a lamp swings past the crate and the shadow sweeps \"\r\n  181:     \"the wall\\n\"\r\n  182:     \"  mode=figure   -> a figure lifts the spool and holds it against the \"\r\n  183:     \"window light\\n\"\r\n  184:     \"BAD, never write these:\\n\"\r\n  185:     \"  tension builds            (not a thing a picture can show)\\n\"\r\n  186:     \"  finality sets in          (not a thing a picture can show)\\n\"\r\n  187:     \"  broadcast begins          (too short, and nothing is drawn)\\n\"\r\n  188:     \"  banded static tightens    (a texture is not a subject)\\n\"\r\n  189:     \"  a waveform pulses         (a graph is not a subject)\\n\"\r\n  190: )\r\n  191: \r\n  192: GHOST_BATCH_RULES = (\r\n  193:     \"You write ONE short drawable visual for each shot in a silent, \"\r\n  194:     \"low-resolution animated film. Each visual must name something a \"\r\n  195:     \"picture can actually show plus ONE visible change or movement.\\n\"\r\n  196:     \"RULES:\\n\"\r\n  197:     \"1. Answer with JSON only, exactly this shape and nothing else:\\n\"\r\n  198:     '   {\"shots\": [{\"id\": \"g000\", \"drawable_beat\": \"...\"}]}\\n'\r\n  199:     \"2. One entry per shot id given below, same ids, no extras, no repeats.\\n\"\r\n  200:     \"3. Each drawable_beat is %d-%d words -- count them -- lower case, and \"\r\n  201:     \"never longer than %d characters. Fewer than %d words is rejected.\\n\"\r\n  202:     \"4. Name a CONCRETE thing and what it does -- an object you could pick \"\r\n  203:     \"up, or a person. A mood, a feeling, an abstract state, a texture, a \"\r\n  204:     \"pattern or a waveform is NOT drawable and will be rejected.\\n\"\r\n  205:     \"5. Write only what is visible. No names, no speech, no captions, no \"\r\n  206:     \"lettering, no camera or lens words, no quality words.\\n\"\r\n  207:     \"6. Never write what is absent. Say what IS in the frame.\\n\"\r\n  208:     \"7. mode=figure SHOWS A PERSON (no face needed). mode=object shows one \"\r\n  209:     \"real object on a real surface, no person. mode=signal shows a real \"\r\n  210:     \"object in a dark room with light moving over it, no person.\\n\"\r\n  211:     \"8. Do not repeat the motif text back; it is already in the picture, and \"\r\n  212:     \"do not reuse the example objects below -- write from the motif you were \"\r\n  213:     \"given.\\n\"\r\n  214:     \"%s\"\r\n  215: ) % (GHOST_LEAF_ASK_WORDS_LOW, GHOST_LEAF_ASK_WORDS_HIGH, GHOST_LEAF_MAX_CHARS,\r\n  216:      GHOST_LEAF_MIN_WORDS, GHOST_BATCH_EXAMPLES)\r\n  217: \r\n  218: GHOST_BATCH_HEADER = \"SHOTS:\"\r\n  219: \r\n  220: GHOST_BATCH_ENVELOPE = '{\"shots\": [{\"id\": \"g000\", \"drawable_beat\": \"...\"}]}'\r\n  221: \r\n  222: \r\n  223: def _template_identity() -> str:\r\n  224:     \"\"\"SHA-256 over the exact generation contract, not just the prose.\r\n  225: \r\n  226:     Includes the temperature and the output-budget FORMULA, because a leaf\r\n  227:     authored at a different temperature or under a different budget is a\r\n  228:     different artifact even when the words of the instruction are identical.\r\n  229:     \"\"\"\r\n  230:     payload = json.dumps({\r\n  231:         \"author_version\": GHOST_AUTHOR_VERSION,\r\n  232:         \"envelope\": GHOST_BATCH_ENVELOPE,\r\n  233:         \"examples\": GHOST_BATCH_EXAMPLES,\r\n  234:         \"header\": GHOST_BATCH_HEADER,\r\n  235:         \"output_tokens_base\": GHOST_BATCH_BASE_TOKENS,\r\n  236:         \"output_tokens_per_shot\": GHOST_BATCH_PER_SHOT_TOKENS,\r\n  237:         \"rules\": GHOST_BATCH_RULES,\r\n  238:         \"schema_version\": GHOST_AUTHOR_SCHEMA_VERSION,\r\n  239:         \"temperature\": GHOST_BATCH_TEMPERATURE,\r\n  240:     }, sort_keys=True, separators=(\",\", \":\"), ensure_ascii=False)\r\n  241:     return hashlib.sha256(payload.encode(\"utf-8\")).hexdigest()\r\n  242: \r\n  243: \r\n  244: GHOST_TEMPLATE_SHA256 = _template_identity()\r\n  245: \r\n  246: \r\n  247: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  248: # Safe projection. Everything the model is allowed to see passes through here.\r\n  249: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  250: \r\n  251: #: Roles this lane authors for. Anything else is not a Ghost beat.\r\n  252: GHOST_ROLES = (\"character_video\", \"announcer_visual\", \"music_visual\")\r\n  253: \r\n  254: #: Arc phases worth telling the model about. ``scene`` is the default value on\r\n  255: #: nearly every line and carries no visual information, so it is dropped rather\r\n  256: #: than spent -- v1 emitted it into the picture as a literal word.\r\n  257: GHOST_ARC_CUES = {\r\n  258:     \"setup\": \"opening\",\r\n  259:     \"rising\": \"building\",\r\n  260:     \"turn\": \"turning\",\r\n  261:     \"climax\": \"peak\",\r\n  262:     \"falling\": \"easing\",\r\n  263:     \"resolution\": \"closing\",\r\n  264:     \"coda\": \"closing\",\r\n  265: }\r\n  266: \r\n  267: #: Field-label debris. A cast row pasted into an intent brings its own labels,\r\n  268: #: and ``Face:`` in a prompt is a request for the word, not the thing.\r\n  269: _FIELD_LABEL_RE = re.compile(\r\n  270:     r\"\\b(face|appearance|description|wardrobe|costume|prop|voice|gender|age|\"\r\n  271:     r\"name|role|traits|beat|intent|arc|scene|shot|prompt)\\s*:\", re.IGNORECASE)\r\n  272: \r\n  273: _SECOND_PERSON_RE = re.compile(r\"\\b(you|your|yours|yourself)\\b\", re.IGNORECASE)\r\n  274: \r\n  275: #: Words that make a leaf a lettering instruction rather than a picture.\r\n  276: _LETTERING_WORDS = frozenset({\r\n  277:     \"text\", \"texts\", \"word\", \"words\", \"letter\", \"letters\", \"lettering\",\r\n  278:     \"caption\", \"captions\", \"subtitle\", \"subtitles\", \"title\", \"titles\",\r\n  279:     \"typography\", \"font\", \"fonts\", \"writing\", \"written\", \"inscription\",\r\n  280:     \"label\", \"labels\", \"signage\", \"logo\", \"headline\",\r\n  281: })\r\n  282: \r\n  283: #: Camera / medium / quality boilerplate. Style is Python's job on this lane.\r\n  284: _BOILERPLATE_WORDS = frozenset({\r\n  285:     \"camera\", \"lens\", \"zoom\", \"pan\", \"dolly\", \"tilt\", \"crop\", \"closeup\",\r\n  286:     \"close-up\", \"bokeh\", \"cinematic\", \"photorealistic\", \"photoreal\",\r\n  287:     \"masterpiece\", \"hdr\", \"8k\", \"4k\", \"render\", \"rendered\", \"rendering\",\r\n  288:     \"octane\", \"unreal\", \"artstation\", \"trending\", \"award-winning\",\r\n  289:     \"highly\", \"ultra\", \"detailed\", \"quality\", \"resolution\", \"style\",\r\n  290: })\r\n  291: \r\n  292: #: Human tokens forbidden in ``object`` and ``signal`` leaves. Absence of a\r\n  293: #: person is expressed by choosing a non-human subject, never by asking for it.\r\n  294: #:\r\n  295: #: BODY PARTS ARE NOT ON THIS LIST, and a live leg is why (2026-08-22). It used\r\n  296: #: to carry hand/hands/arm/arms/shoulder/shoulders, and it rejected\r\n  297: #: *\"the silver ledger sits on a desk as a clock hand ticks\"* -- a CLOCK hand --\r\n  298: #: which killed the whole batch and dropped the episode to deterministic\r\n  299: #: clauses. It also rejected *\"the radio dial turns as a hand adjusts the\r\n  300: #: knob\"*, which is the archival-documentary look the operator ranked SECOND\r\n  301: #: out of five arms.\r\n  302: #:\r\n  303: #: What these modes owe is that no FULL FIGURE dominates the shot. They do not\r\n  304: #: owe a frame with no people in it anywhere, and a word list that cannot tell\r\n  305: #: a clock hand from a person is a list that costs a live batch to learn that.\r\n  306: _HUMAN_WORDS = frozenset({\r\n  307:     \"person\", \"people\", \"man\", \"men\", \"woman\", \"women\", \"boy\", \"girl\",\r\n  308:     \"child\", \"children\", \"crowd\", \"figure\", \"figures\", \"silhouette\",\r\n  309:     \"silhouettes\", \"face\", \"faces\", \"portrait\", \"someone\", \"somebody\",\r\n  310: })\r\n  311: \r\n  312: #: Words whose SUBJECT is a texture rather than a thing. A 512x288 SD1.5 draws\r\n  313: #: exactly what it is handed: asked for static it paints static, asked for a\r\n  314: #: lantern it paints a lantern. The first live v2 arms are the measurement --\r\n  315: #: every beat whose leaf named one of these rendered as unreadable texture, and\r\n  316: #: the only beats that survived were the ones naming a real object.\r\n  317: #:\r\n  318: #: NOT a taste judgement. A leaf may still describe light, shadow or movement;\r\n  319: #: it may not make the ABSENCE OF A SUBJECT its subject.\r\n  320: _ABSTRACT_SUBJECT_WORDS = frozenset({\r\n  321:     \"static\", \"waveform\", \"waveforms\", \"gradient\", \"gradients\", \"texture\",\r\n  322:     \"textures\", \"abstraction\", \"abstract\", \"pixels\", \"pixelation\", \"noise\",\r\n  323:     \"geometry\", \"geometric\", \"grain\", \"scanlines\", \"interference\",\r\n  324: })\r\n  325: \r\n  326: #: Positive-channel negation. The negative prompt is the exclusion authority;\r\n  327: #: a positive clause that attends to an absent thing summons it instead.\r\n  328: _NEGATION_RE = re.compile(\r\n  329:     r\"\\b(no|not|without|never|absent|empty of|devoid)\\b\", re.IGNORECASE)\r\n  330: \r\n  331: _WORD_RE = re.compile(r\"[a-z0-9][a-z0-9'\\-]*\")\r\n  332: _WS_RE = re.compile(r\"\\s+\")\r\n  333: \r\n  334: \r\n  335: def _norm(text) -> str:\r\n  336:     return _WS_RE.sub(\" \", str(text or \"\")).strip()\r\n  337: \r\n  338: \r\n  339: def normalize_role(role) -> str:\r\n  340:     \"\"\"The role token, or ``\"\"`` for anything this lane does not author.\"\"\"\r\n  341:     role = _norm(role)\r\n  342:     return role if role in GHOST_ROLES else \"\"\r\n  343: \r\n  344: \r\n  345: def strip_cast_names(text, names) -> str:\r\n  346:     \"\"\"Remove every known cast name -- whole and by part -- from ``text``.\r\n  347: \r\n  348:     Parts as well as wholes because a two-word name is referred to by either\r\n  349:     half in ordinary intent prose, and a first name in a prompt is still a\r\n  350:     proper noun the model will try to draw. Tokens shorter than three\r\n  351:     characters are skipped: an initial is not a name leak, and stripping ``a``\r\n  352:     would destroy the sentence.\r\n  353:     \"\"\"\r\n  354:     out = str(text or \"\")\r\n  355:     tokens = []\r\n  356:     for name in (names or ()):\r\n  357:         name = _norm(name)\r\n  358:         if not name:\r\n  359:             continue\r\n  360:         tokens.append(name)\r\n  361:         tokens.extend(name.split())\r\n  362:     # LONGEST FIRST. Removing \"Adrian\" before \"Adrian Spender\" would leave a\r\n  363:     # bare surname behind -- still a proper noun, still a leak.\r\n  364:     for token in sorted({t for t in tokens if len(t) >= 3}, key=len,\r\n  365:                         reverse=True):\r\n  366:         out = re.sub(r\"\\b%s\\b\" % re.escape(token), \" \", out,\r\n  367:                      flags=re.IGNORECASE)\r\n  368:     return _norm(out)\r\n  369: \r\n  370: \r\n  371: def sanitize_intent(beat_intent, names=()) -> str:\r\n  372:     \"\"\"The one free-text field the model sees, made safe and lower case.\r\n  373: \r\n  374:     Names removed, field labels removed, second person removed, punctuation\r\n  375:     normalized, and the result bounded. It is a HINT about what happens in the\r\n  376:     beat, never a sentence to copy -- v1's whole defect was copying it.\r\n  377:     \"\"\"\r\n  378:     text = strip_cast_names(beat_intent, names)\r\n  379:     text = _FIELD_LABEL_RE.sub(\" \", text)\r\n  380:     text = _SECOND_PERSON_RE.sub(\" \", text)\r\n  381:     text = text.replace(\"\\r\", \" \").replace(\"\\n\", \" \")\r\n  382:     text = re.sub(r'[\"`]+', \" \", text)\r\n  383:     text = _norm(text).lower().strip(\" ,;:.-\")\r\n  384:     words = text.split()\r\n  385:     if len(words) > 24:\r\n  386:         text = \" \".join(words[:24])\r\n  387:     return _norm(text)\r\n  388: \r\n  389: \r\n  390: def normalize_emotion(traits) -> str:\r\n  391:     \"\"\"The beat's recorded mood as one short lower-case phrase, or ``\"\"``.\"\"\"\r\n  392:     text = _norm(traits).lower().strip(\" ,;:.-\")\r\n  393:     if not text:\r\n  394:         return \"\"\r\n  395:     text = text.split(\",\")[0].strip()\r\n  396:     words = text.split()\r\n  397:     return \" \".join(words[:3])\r\n  398: \r\n  399: \r\n  400: def map_arc(arc_phase) -> str:\r\n  401:     \"\"\"A mapped arc cue, or ``\"\"`` when the value carries no visual meaning.\"\"\"\r\n  402:     return GHOST_ARC_CUES.get(_norm(arc_phase).lower(), \"\")\r\n  403: \r\n  404: \r\n  405: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  406: # Deterministic mode scheduling.\r\n  407: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  408: \r\n  409: def _hash_int(*parts) -> int:\r\n  410:     payload = \"|\".join(str(p) for p in parts)\r\n  411:     return int(hashlib.sha256(payload.encode(\"utf-8\")).hexdigest()[:16], 16)\r\n  412: \r\n  413: \r\n  414: def schedule_ghost_modes(entries, episode_seed) -> dict:\r\n  415:     \"\"\"``{beat_id: mode}`` for one episode's whole Ghost timeline.\r\n  416: \r\n  417:     ``entries`` is the ORDERED list of ``(beat_id, role)`` pairs -- ordered\r\n  418:     because the anti-run rule is a property of the timeline, not of a beat.\r\n  419: \r\n  420:     CHARACTER BEATS RUN :data:`GHOST_CHARACTER_CYCLE` from a hashed offset --\r\n  421:     ``figure, object, figure, signal``, so half of them show a person and no two\r\n  422:     adjacent entries are equal, which means a character can never start a run\r\n  423:     and never needs correcting.\r\n  424: \r\n  425:     BOOKENDS alternate ``object``/``signal`` from their own hashed offset. Only\r\n  426:     a bookend may be corrected, and only when it would create a THIRD identical\r\n  427:     mode in a row -- which keeps clip planning stable and keeps the correction\r\n  428:     away from the assignments the recurrence design depends on.\r\n  429:     \"\"\"\r\n  430:     seed = _hash_int(episode_seed, \"ghost_mode_schedule\", GHOST_AUTHOR_VERSION)\r\n  431:     char_offset = seed % len(GHOST_CHARACTER_CYCLE)\r\n  432:     bookend_offset = (seed >> 8) % len(GHOST_NON_FIGURE_MODES)\r\n  433: \r\n  434:     out = {}\r\n  435:     scheduled = []\r\n  436:     char_n = 0\r\n  437:     bookend_n = 0\r\n  438:     for beat_id, role in entries:\r\n  439:         beat_id = str(beat_id)\r\n  440:         if normalize_role(role) == \"character_video\":\r\n  441:             mode = GHOST_CHARACTER_CYCLE[\r\n  442:                 (char_offset + char_n) % len(GHOST_CHARACTER_CYCLE)]\r\n  443:             char_n += 1\r\n  444:             fixed = True\r\n  445:         else:\r\n  446:             mode = GHOST_NON_FIGURE_MODES[\r\n  447:                 (bookend_offset + bookend_n) % len(GHOST_NON_FIGURE_MODES)]\r\n  448:             bookend_n += 1\r\n  449:             fixed = False\r\n  450:         if (not fixed and len(scheduled) >= 2\r\n  451:                 and scheduled[-1] == mode and scheduled[-2] == mode):\r\n  452:             # The other non-figure representation, deterministically.\r\n  453:             mode = GHOST_NON_FIGURE_MODES[\r\n  454:                 (GHOST_NON_FIGURE_MODES.index(mode) + 1)\r\n  455:                 % len(GHOST_NON_FIGURE_MODES)]\r\n  456:         scheduled.append(mode)\r\n  457:         out[beat_id] = mode\r\n  458:     return out\r\n  459: \r\n  460: \r\n  461: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  462: # The recurrence motif -- a compact, allowlisted, NON-FACE identity.\r\n  463: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  464: \r\n  465: #: Colour tokens, split out of the composer's costume bucket. Colour is the one\r\n  466: #: property that survives 512x288 across a sliding context, so it anchors all\r\n  467: #: three representations of one character.\r\n  468: MOTIF_COLOUR_WORDS = (\r\n  469:     \"black\", \"white\", \"red\", \"green\", \"blue\", \"brown\", \"grey\", \"gray\",\r\n  470:     \"crimson\", \"scarlet\", \"navy\", \"olive\", \"amber\", \"gold\", \"golden\",\r\n  471:     \"silver\", \"rust\", \"ochre\", \"violet\", \"purple\", \"cream\", \"tan\", \"charcoal\",\r\n  472: )\r\n  473: \r\n  474: #: Garment tokens. ``figure`` carries one so the person on screen is wearing\r\n  475: #: something a sampler can draw; the non-figure modes have no use for a coat.\r\n  476: MOTIF_GARMENT_WORDS = (\r\n  477:     \"coat\", \"overcoat\", \"trenchcoat\", \"greatcoat\", \"jacket\", \"uniform\",\r\n  478:     \"cloak\", \"cape\", \"gown\", \"dress\", \"suit\", \"waistcoat\", \"vest\", \"apron\",\r\n  479:     \"shawl\", \"robe\", \"hat\", \"fedora\", \"cap\", \"helmet\", \"hood\", \"scarf\",\r\n  480: )\r\n  481: \r\n  482: #: Silhouette tokens. ``figure`` alone carries one.\r\n  483: MOTIF_SILHOUETTE_WORDS = (\r\n  484:     \"tall\", \"short\", \"broad\", \"slight\", \"stooped\", \"lean\", \"heavyset\",\r\n  485:     \"wiry\", \"angular\", \"round\", \"towering\", \"compact\", \"gaunt\", \"stocky\",\r\n  486:     \"willowy\", \"slender\", \"squat\", \"burly\", \"spare\", \"rangy\", \"narrow\",\r\n  487: )\r\n  488: \r\n  489: #: Prop tokens, carried by all three representations of one character.\r\n  490: MOTIF_PROP_WORDS = (\r\n  491:     \"lantern\", \"lamp\", \"torch\", \"candle\", \"revolver\", \"pistol\", \"rifle\",\r\n  492:     \"knife\", \"blade\", \"sword\", \"dagger\", \"book\", \"ledger\", \"journal\",\r\n  493:     \"letter\", \"map\", \"chart\", \"cane\", \"staff\", \"umbrella\", \"satchel\",\r\n  494:     \"case\", \"briefcase\", \"pipe\", \"flask\", \"bottle\", \"glass\", \"cup\", \"key\",\r\n  495:     \"rope\", \"chain\", \"hammer\", \"wrench\", \"microphone\", \"headset\",\r\n  496:     \"telegraph\", \"camera\", \"watch\", \"spyglass\", \"telescope\", \"basket\",\r\n  497:     \"crate\", \"lockbox\",\r\n  498: )\r\n  499: \r\n  500: #: Deterministic fills, used when a cast row supplied no allowlisted token of\r\n  501: #: that kind. Checked in, never generated, never asked of a model.\r\n  502: MOTIF_FALLBACK_POOLS = {\r\n  503:     \"colour\": (\"amber\", \"rust\", \"olive\", \"charcoal\", \"cream\", \"navy\"),\r\n  504:     \"garment\": (\"coat\", \"uniform\", \"shawl\", \"jacket\", \"hood\", \"scarf\"),\r\n  505:     \"silhouette\": (\"lean\", \"broad\", \"slight\", \"tall\", \"compact\", \"angular\"),\r\n  506:     \"prop\": (\"lantern\", \"key\", \"ledger\", \"satchel\", \"chart\", \"telegraph\"),\r\n  507: }\r\n  508: \r\n  509: #: Bookend motifs, by ``(role, mode)``. Real radio HARDWARE that recurs while\r\n  510: #: the unchanged pack cue keeps supplying the anime / archive / material look.\r\n  511: #: A Ghost bookend with an empty motif is invalid, which is why these are\r\n  512: #: constants rather than an optional derivation.\r\n  513: #:\r\n  514: #: They used to read \"radio dial emblem\" and \"broadcast waveform signal\". An\r\n  515: #: emblem is not a thing and a waveform is a graph, and the bookends rendered as\r\n  516: #: texture accordingly. A bakelite radio set is a thing.\r\n  517: GHOST_BOOKEND_MOTIFS = {\r\n  518:     (\"announcer_visual\", \"object\"): \"a bakelite radio set\",\r\n  519:     (\"announcer_visual\", \"signal\"): \"a glowing radio dial\",\r\n  520:     (\"music_visual\", \"object\"): \"a broadcast console\",\r\n  521:     (\"music_visual\", \"signal\"): \"a spinning turntable\",\r\n  522: }\r\n  523: \r\n  524: \r\n  525: def _first_allowlisted(phrases, vocabulary) -> str:\r\n  526:     \"\"\"The first allowlisted WORD found across ``phrases``, in source order.\r\n  527: \r\n  528:     A WORD, never the phrase it came from: copying the phrase is how a cast\r\n  529:     row's prose -- and its landmarks, its hair, its jaw -- would get back into\r\n  530:     a prompt that is supposed to have left all of that behind.\r\n  531:     \"\"\"\r\n  532:     allowed = tuple(vocabulary)\r\n  533:     for phrase in phrases:\r\n  534:         words = _WORD_RE.findall(str(phrase or \"\").lower())\r\n  535:         for candidate in allowed:\r\n  536:             if candidate in words:\r\n  537:                 return candidate\r\n  538:     return \"\"\r\n  539: \r\n  540: \r\n  541: def _motif_tokens(components, seed_int) -> dict:\r\n  542:     \"\"\"The four allowlisted motif tokens for one character.\r\n  543: \r\n  544:     ``components`` is ``ghost_signal_prompt.distill_sigil_components``'s bucket\r\n  545:     map. The LANDMARK bucket is deliberately not read: an asymmetrical facial\r\n  546:     landmark is exactly the face-adjacent recurrence Prompt v2 replaces.\r\n  547:     \"\"\"\r\n  548:     components = components if isinstance(components, dict) else {}\r\n  549:     costume = components.get(\"costume\") or \"\"\r\n  550:     prop = components.get(\"prop\") or \"\"\r\n  551:     silhouette = components.get(\"silhouette\") or \"\"\r\n  552: \r\n  553:     # COLOUR AND GARMENT ARE SCANNED ACROSS BOTH NON-FACE BUCKETS, in costume-\r\n  554:     # first order. The sigil's buckets are GREEDY -- one phrase is consumed by\r\n  555:     # the first bucket that matches it -- so a cast row reading \"a broad steady\r\n  556:     # man in a charcoal overcoat\" lands whole in the SILHOUETTE bucket and the\r\n  557:     # costume bucket then falls to its neutral pool. Reading only the costume\r\n  558:     # bucket would have thrown away the character's real colour and worn a\r\n  559:     # pooled one instead, which is the opposite of a recurrence cue.\r\n  560:     #\r\n  561:     # The LANDMARK bucket is never scanned. That is where the jaw, the brow,\r\n  562:     # the scar and the hair live, and face-adjacent recurrence is exactly what\r\n  563:     # Prompt v2 exists to replace.\r\n  564:     out = {}\r\n  565:     out[\"colour\"] = _first_allowlisted((costume, silhouette),\r\n  566:                                        MOTIF_COLOUR_WORDS)\r\n  567:     out[\"garment\"] = _first_allowlisted((costume, silhouette),\r\n  568:                                         MOTIF_GARMENT_WORDS)\r\n  569:     out[\"silhouette\"] = _first_allowlisted((silhouette,),\r\n  570:                                            MOTIF_SILHOUETTE_WORDS)\r\n  571:     out[\"prop\"] = _first_allowlisted((prop,), MOTIF_PROP_WORDS)\r\n  572:     for kind, pool in MOTIF_FALLBACK_POOLS.items():\r\n  573:         if not out.get(kind):\r\n  574:             out[kind] = pool[_hash_int(seed_int, \"motif\", kind) % len(pool)]\r\n  575:     return out\r\n  576: \r\n  577: \r\n  578: def motif_for_character(components, mode, *, seed_int=0) -> str:\r\n  579:     \"\"\"The compact recurrence motif for one character in one representation.\r\n  580: \r\n  581:     All three representations share COLOUR + PROP, which is what makes the\r\n  582:     episode read as related; ``figure`` additionally carries the silhouette,\r\n  583:     which is the only body property this model holds across a sliding context.\r\n  584:     \"\"\"\r\n  585:     mode = str(mode or \"\")\r\n  586:     tokens = _motif_tokens(components, seed_int)\r\n  587:     # \"an olive key\", not \"a olive key\". The motif is prompt text a person reads\r\n  588:     # in a receipt and a sampler reads as language; a broken article is a small\r\n  589:     # thing that makes both of them worse.\r\n  590:     article = \"an\" if tokens[\"colour\"][:1] in \"aeiou\" else \"a\"\r\n  591:     if mode == \"figure\":\r\n  592:         # A PERSON, SAID PLAINLY. The first draft said \"<silhouette> silhouette\r\n  593:         # with a <colour> <prop>\", and SD1.5 drew vertical black shapes -- it\r\n  594:         # does not know that a \"silhouette\" is supposed to be someone. v1 said\r\n  595:         # \"a man, a broad steady figure, a charcoal coat, holding a folded\r\n  596:         # chart\" and got a man in a coat. This is that, minus the name leak.\r\n  597:         return \"a %s figure in %s %s %s, carrying %s %s\" % (\r\n  598:             tokens[\"silhouette\"], article, tokens[\"colour\"], tokens[\"garment\"],\r\n  599:             \"an\" if tokens[\"prop\"][:1] in \"aeiou\" else \"a\", tokens[\"prop\"])\r\n  600:     if mode in (\"object\", \"signal\"):\r\n  601:         # THE PROP AS ITSELF. \"charcoal lantern emblem\" is not a thing; a\r\n  602:         # charcoal lantern is. The two beats that survived the first draft are\r\n  603:         # the two whose leaf named a real object, which is the whole finding.\r\n  604:         return \"%s %s %s\" % (article, tokens[\"colour\"], tokens[\"prop\"])\r\n  605:     raise GhostAuthorError(\"unknown Ghost representation mode %r\" % (mode,))\r\n  606: \r\n  607: \r\n  608: def motif_for_bookend(role, mode) -> str:\r\n  609:     \"\"\"The checked-in bookend motif. Missing pair is a constant defect.\"\"\"\r\n  610:     key = (normalize_role(role), str(mode or \"\"))\r\n  611:     motif = GHOST_BOOKEND_MOTIFS.get(key)\r\n  612:     if not motif:\r\n  613:         raise GhostAuthorError(\r\n  614:             \"no Ghost bookend motif for role/mode %r -- an empty bookend \"\r\n  615:             \"motif is invalid, so this is a composer constant defect rather \"\r\n  616:             \"than a beat to fall back on\" % (key,))\r\n  617:     return motif\r\n  618: \r\n  619: \r\n  620: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  621: # Specs -- what ShotLock projects and what the model actually receives.\r\n  622: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  623: \r\n  624: def opaque_id(ordinal) -> str:\r\n  625:     \"\"\"``g000``, ``g001``, ... -- the model never sees a ledger identifier.\"\"\"\r\n  626:     return \"g%03d\" % int(ordinal)\r\n  627: \r\n  628: \r\n  629: def build_ghost_author_specs(rows, *, model_id) -> list:\r\n  630:     \"\"\"Project ShotLock rows into the ordered, safe author specs.\r\n  631: \r\n  632:     Each ``row`` is a dict with ``beat_id``, ``role``, ``mode``, ``motif_cue``,\r\n  633:     ``sanitized_intent``, ``normalized_emotion`` and ``mapped_arc`` -- all of\r\n  634:     them already safe. This function adds the opaque id, the ordinal and the\r\n  635:     request hash, and refuses anything shaped wrong.\r\n  636:     \"\"\"\r\n  637:     specs = []\r\n  638:     for ordinal, row in enumerate(rows or ()):\r\n  639:         role = normalize_role((row or {}).get(\"role\"))\r\n  640:         if not role:\r\n  641:             raise GhostAuthorError(\r\n  642:                 \"Ghost author row %d has role %r, which this lane does not \"\r\n  643:                 \"author\" % (ordinal, (row or {}).get(\"role\")))\r\n  644:         mode = str((row or {}).get(\"mode\") or \"\")\r\n  645:         if mode not in GHOST_MODES:\r\n  646:             raise GhostAuthorError(\r\n  647:                 \"Ghost author row %d has mode %r; expected one of %s\"\r\n  648:                 % (ordinal, mode, \", \".join(GHOST_MODES)))\r\n  649:         if role != \"character_video\" and mode == \"figure\":\r\n  650:             raise GhostAuthorError(\r\n  651:                 \"Ghost author row %d is a %s bookend scheduled as figure -- a \"\r\n  652:                 \"radio console is not a person\" % (ordinal, role))\r\n  653:         motif_cue = _norm(row.get(\"motif_cue\"))\r\n  654:         if not motif_cue:\r\n  655:             raise GhostAuthorError(\r\n  656:                 \"Ghost author row %d has an empty motif_cue; the recurrence \"\r\n  657:                 \"cue is the identity mechanism and may not be absent\"\r\n  658:                 % (ordinal,))\r\n  659:         beat_id = _norm(row.get(\"beat_id\"))\r\n  660:         if not beat_id:\r\n  661:             raise GhostAuthorError(\r\n  662:                 \"Ghost author row %d has no beat_id\" % (ordinal,))\r\n  663:         spec = {\r\n  664:             \"author_version\": GHOST_AUTHOR_VERSION,\r\n  665:             \"beat_id\": beat_id,\r\n  666:             \"mapped_arc\": _norm(row.get(\"mapped_arc\")),\r\n  667:             \"mode\": mode,\r\n  668:             \"model_id\": _norm(model_id),\r\n  669:             \"motif_cue\": motif_cue,\r\n  670:             \"motif_sha256\": hashlib.sha256(\r\n  671:                 motif_cue.encode(\"utf-8\")).hexdigest(),\r\n  672:             \"normalized_emotion\": _norm(row.get(\"normalized_emotion\")),\r\n  673:             \"ordinal\": int(ordinal),\r\n  674:             \"role\": role,\r\n  675:             \"sanitized_intent\": _norm(row.get(\"sanitized_intent\")),\r\n  676:             \"schema_version\": GHOST_AUTHOR_SCHEMA_VERSION,\r\n  677:             \"template_sha256\": GHOST_TEMPLATE_SHA256,\r\n  678:         }\r\n  679:         spec[\"request_sha256\"] = request_sha256(spec)\r\n  680:         spec[\"id\"] = opaque_id(ordinal)\r\n  681:         specs.append(spec)\r\n  682:     return specs\r\n  683: \r\n  684: \r\n  685: #: THE THIRTEEN HASHED KEYS, and there is no second spelling of this set.\r\n  686: GHOST_REQUEST_HASH_KEYS = (\r\n  687:     \"author_version\", \"beat_id\", \"mapped_arc\", \"mode\", \"model_id\",\r\n  688:     \"motif_cue\", \"motif_sha256\", \"normalized_emotion\", \"ordinal\", \"role\",\r\n  689:     \"sanitized_intent\", \"schema_version\", \"template_sha256\",\r\n  690: )\r\n  691: \r\n  692: \r\n  693: def request_sha256(spec) -> str:\r\n  694:     \"\"\"SHA-256 over compact, sorted, canonical JSON of the thirteen keys.\r\n  695: \r\n  696:     ``beat_id`` is the CANONICAL ledger identity (``b000_music_open`` included).\r\n  697:     Neither the cast-time temporary ``shot_id=beat_id`` nor the durable\r\n  698:     ``shot_id=\"shot_\"+beat_id`` is hashed -- they differ on purpose, and hashing\r\n  699:     either would make the temporary and durable objects disagree.\r\n  700:     \"\"\"\r\n  701:     payload = {key: spec.get(key) for key in GHOST_REQUEST_HASH_KEYS}\r\n  702:     missing = [k for k, v in payload.items() if v is None]\r\n  703:     if missing:\r\n  704:         raise GhostAuthorError(\r\n  705:             \"Ghost request hash is missing key(s) %s\" % \", \".join(missing))\r\n  706:     blob = json.dumps(payload, sort_keys=True, separators=(\",\", \":\"),\r\n  707:                       ensure_ascii=False)\r\n  708:     return hashlib.sha256(blob.encode(\"utf-8\")).hexdigest()\r\n  709: \r\n  710: \r\n  711: def output_sha256(drawable_beat) -> str:\r\n  712:     \"\"\"The accepted leaf's own bytes -- never the disposition-bearing wrapper.\r\n  713: \r\n  714:     Hashing the wrapper would make ``source`` part of the digest, so the same\r\n  715:     text replayed under ``source=replay`` would hash differently from the text\r\n  716:     that was authored. The leaf is the artifact; the disposition is a receipt.\r\n  717:     \"\"\"\r\n  718:     return hashlib.sha256(str(drawable_beat or \"\").encode(\"utf-8\")).hexdigest()\r\n  719: \r\n  720: \r\n  721: def build_batch_prompt(specs) -> str:\r\n  722:     \"\"\"The single user message for one whole episode's Ghost batch.\"\"\"\r\n  723:     lines = [GHOST_BATCH_RULES, GHOST_BATCH_HEADER]\r\n  724:     for spec in specs:\r\n  725:         parts = [\"id=%s\" % spec[\"id\"], \"mode=%s\" % spec[\"mode\"],\r\n  726:                  \"motif=%s\" % spec[\"motif_cue\"]]\r\n  727:         if spec.get(\"sanitized_intent\"):\r\n  728:             parts.append(\"happening=%s\" % spec[\"sanitized_intent\"])\r\n  729:         if spec.get(\"normalized_emotion\"):\r\n  730:             parts.append(\"mood=%s\" % spec[\"normalized_emotion\"])\r\n  731:         if spec.get(\"mapped_arc\"):\r\n  732:             parts.append(\"story=%s\" % spec[\"mapped_arc\"])\r\n  733:         lines.append(\"- \" + \"; \".join(parts))\r\n  734:     return \"\\n\".join(lines)\r\n  735: \r\n  736: \r\n  737: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  738: # Strict parsing. One envelope, no salvage.\r\n  739: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  740: \r\n  741: _FENCE_RE = re.compile(\r\n  742:     r\"\\A\\s*```(?:json)?\\s*\\n(?P<body>.*?)\\n?\\s*```\\s*\\Z\", re.DOTALL)\r\n  743: \r\n  744: \r\n  745: def _strip_one_fence(raw) -> str:\r\n  746:     \"\"\"Remove EXACTLY one enclosing markdown fence -- transport, not content.\r\n  747: \r\n  748:     One, and only one: a response wrapped twice, or with prose either side of\r\n  749:     the fence, is a response that did not follow the instruction, and pretending\r\n  750:     otherwise is how a parser starts accepting anything.\r\n  751:     \"\"\"\r\n  752:     text = str(raw or \"\").strip()\r\n  753:     match = _FENCE_RE.match(text)\r\n  754:     return match.group(\"body\").strip() if match else text\r\n  755: \r\n  756: \r\n  757: def _object_pairs_hook(pairs):\r\n  758:     seen = set()\r\n  759:     for key, _value in pairs:\r\n  760:         if key in seen:\r\n  761:             raise GhostAuthorParseError(\r\n  762:                 \"Ghost batch response repeats the key %r\" % (key,))\r\n  763:         seen.add(key)\r\n  764:     return dict(pairs)\r\n  765: \r\n  766: \r\n  767: def parse_batch_response(raw, expected_ids) -> dict:\r\n  768:     \"\"\"``{opaque_id: drawable_beat}``, or raise.\r\n  769: \r\n  770:     ACCEPTS EXACTLY ONE SHAPE:\r\n  771:     ``{\"shots\": [{\"id\": \"g000\", \"drawable_beat\": \"...\"}, ...]}`` -- every\r\n  772:     expected id present exactly once, no unknown id, no extra field on the\r\n  773:     envelope or on a row, no trailing object after the JSON.\r\n  774:     \"\"\"\r\n  775:     expected = list(expected_ids or ())\r\n  776:     body = _strip_one_fence(raw)\r\n  777:     if not body:\r\n  778:         raise GhostAuthorParseError(\"Ghost batch response is empty\")\r\n  779:     decoder = json.JSONDecoder(object_pairs_hook=_object_pairs_hook)\r\n  780:     try:\r\n  781:         payload, end = decoder.raw_decode(body)\r\n  782:     except GhostAuthorParseError:\r\n  783:         raise\r\n  784:     except ValueError as exc:\r\n  785:         raise GhostAuthorParseError(\r\n  786:             \"Ghost batch response is not JSON: %s\" % (exc,)) from exc\r\n  787:     if body[end:].strip():\r\n  788:         raise GhostAuthorParseError(\r\n  789:             \"Ghost batch response carries trailing content after the JSON \"\r\n  790:             \"object -- prose and second objects are not transport wrapping\")\r\n  791:     if not isinstance(payload, dict):\r\n  792:         raise GhostAuthorParseError(\r\n  793:             \"Ghost batch response is a %s, not an object\"\r\n  794:             % type(payload).__name__)\r\n  795:     if set(payload) != {\"shots\"}:\r\n  796:         raise GhostAuthorParseError(\r\n  797:             \"Ghost batch envelope must carry exactly one key 'shots'; got %s\"\r\n  798:             % (\", \".join(sorted(payload)) or \"nothing\"))\r\n  799:     rows = payload.get(\"shots\")\r\n  800:     if not isinstance(rows, list):\r\n  801:         raise GhostAuthorParseError(\"Ghost batch 'shots' is not a list\")\r\n  802:     out = {}\r\n  803:     for row in rows:\r\n  804:         if not isinstance(row, dict):\r\n  805:             raise GhostAuthorParseError(\r\n  806:                 \"Ghost batch row is a %s, not an object\" % type(row).__name__)\r\n  807:         if set(row) != {\"id\", \"drawable_beat\"}:\r\n  808:             raise GhostAuthorParseError(\r\n  809:                 \"Ghost batch row must carry exactly id + drawable_beat; got %s\"\r\n  810:                 % \", \".join(sorted(row)))\r\n  811:         shot_id = row.get(\"id\")\r\n  812:         leaf = row.get(\"drawable_beat\")\r\n  813:         if not isinstance(shot_id, str) or not isinstance(leaf, str):\r\n  814:             raise GhostAuthorParseError(\r\n  815:                 \"Ghost batch row id/drawable_beat must both be strings\")\r\n  816:         if shot_id not in expected:\r\n  817:             raise GhostAuthorParseError(\r\n  818:                 \"Ghost batch row carries unknown id %r\" % (shot_id,))\r\n  819:         if shot_id in out:\r\n  820:             raise GhostAuthorParseError(\r\n  821:                 \"Ghost batch repeats id %r\" % (shot_id,))\r\n  822:         out[shot_id] = _norm(leaf)\r\n  823:     missing = [i for i in expected if i not in out]\r\n  824:     if missing:\r\n  825:         raise GhostAuthorParseError(\r\n  826:             \"Ghost batch response is missing id(s) %s\" % \", \".join(missing))\r\n  827:     return out\r\n  828: \r\n  829: \r\n  830: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  831: # Leaf validation. SHAPE AND BOUNDARY SAFETY -- never a story-vocabulary judge.\r\n  832: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  833: \r\n  834: def validate_drawable_beat(leaf, *, mode, names=()) -> tuple:\r\n  835:     \"\"\"``(ok, reason)`` for one authored leaf.\r\n  836: \r\n  837:     This is deliberately NOT a quality gate. It checks that the leaf is the\r\n  838:     right shape, that nothing crossed the boundary that was not supposed to\r\n  839:     (a name, a second person, a field label), and that it did not answer a\r\n  840:     question Python already answered (lettering, camera, style, a negation, a\r\n  841:     person in a non-figure mode). Whether the picture is any good is the\r\n  842:     operator's eye, not a word list.\r\n  843:     \"\"\"\r\n  844:     text = _norm(leaf)\r\n  845:     if not text:\r\n  846:         return False, \"empty\"\r\n  847:     if \"\\n\" in str(leaf) or \"\\r\" in str(leaf):\r\n  848:         return False, \"line break\"\r\n  849:     if len(text) > GHOST_LEAF_MAX_CHARS:\r\n  850:         return False, \"over %d characters\" % GHOST_LEAF_MAX_CHARS\r\n  851:     if \":\" in text:\r\n  852:         return False, \"carries a field label\"\r\n  853:     words = text.split()\r\n  854:     if len(words) < GHOST_LEAF_MIN_WORDS:\r\n  855:         return False, \"under %d words\" % GHOST_LEAF_MIN_WORDS\r\n  856:     if len(words) > GHOST_LEAF_MAX_WORDS:\r\n  857:         return False, \"over %d words\" % GHOST_LEAF_MAX_WORDS\r\n  858:     lowered = set(_WORD_RE.findall(text.lower()))\r\n  859:     if lowered & _LETTERING_WORDS:\r\n  860:         return False, \"asks for lettering\"\r\n  861:     if lowered & _BOILERPLATE_WORDS:\r\n  862:         return False, \"carries camera/style boilerplate\"\r\n  863:     if lowered & _ABSTRACT_SUBJECT_WORDS:\r\n  864:         return False, \"names a texture instead of a thing\"\r\n  865:     if _SECOND_PERSON_RE.search(text):\r\n  866:         return False, \"addresses a second person\"\r\n  867:     if _NEGATION_RE.search(text):\r\n  868:         return False, \"negates in the positive channel\"\r\n  869:     stripped = strip_cast_names(text, names)\r\n  870:     if stripped.lower() != text.lower():\r\n  871:         return False, \"carries a cast name\"\r\n  872:     if str(mode) in GHOST_NON_FIGURE_MODES and (lowered & _HUMAN_WORDS):\r\n  873:         return False, \"requests a person in %s mode\" % (mode,)\r\n  874:     tail = words[-1].strip(\",.;:-\").lower()\r\n  875:     if tail in _gsp._DANGLING_TAIL_WORDS:\r\n  876:         return False, \"ends on a dangling function word\"\r\n  877:     return True, \"\"\r\n  878: \r\n  879: \r\n  880: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  881: # The deterministic batch. Complete clauses, never a free-text slice.\r\n  882: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  883: \r\n  884: #: Complete, checked-in, mode-specific clauses. Every one of them is a whole\r\n  885: #: drawable idea -- which is precisely what the v1 six-word slice was not.\r\n  886: GHOST_FALLBACK_CLAUSES = {\r\n  887:     \"figure\": (\r\n  888:         \"a figure turns slowly toward a lit doorway\",\r\n  889:         \"a figure steps forward across a bare wooden floor\",\r\n  890:         \"a figure lifts one hand and holds it against the light\",\r\n  891:         \"a figure leans over a table and goes still\",\r\n  892:         \"a figure straightens and looks off past the frame\",\r\n  893:         \"a figure turns away and walks into the dark\",\r\n  894:     ),\r\n  895:     \"object\": (\r\n  896:         \"it tilts on the table as a shadow crosses it\",\r\n  897:         \"it catches a rising glow along one worn edge\",\r\n  898:         \"it rocks once on the wood and settles again\",\r\n  899:         \"it stands on a desk while dust drifts across it\",\r\n  900:         \"it turns a quarter and the highlight slides away\",\r\n  901:         \"it sinks slowly into the shadow at the table edge\",\r\n  902:     ),\r\n  903:     \"signal\": (\r\n  904:         \"a lamp swings past it and the shadow sweeps the wall\",\r\n  905:         \"light crawls across it and steadies against the back wall\",\r\n  906:         \"a warm beam finds it and holds on its worn edge\",\r\n  907:         \"it sits in the dark as a slow light passes over it\",\r\n  908:         \"the light on it narrows to a single bright band\",\r\n  909:         \"the light leaves it and the room goes dim\",\r\n  910:     ),\r\n  911: }\r\n  912: \r\n  913: #: The bookends get their own phase-specific clauses so an episode does not\r\n  914: #: open and close on the same picture, which is what a shared pack register\r\n  915: #: produced on every v1 episode.\r\n  916: GHOST_FALLBACK_BOOKENDS = {\r\n  917:     (\"opening\", \"object\"): \"it lights from cold to warm on the studio desk\",\r\n  918:     (\"opening\", \"signal\"): \"a warm light finds it in the dark studio\",\r\n  919:     (\"closing\", \"object\"): \"it dims slowly and the highlight leaves its edge\",\r\n  920:     (\"closing\", \"signal\"): \"the light slides off it and the studio goes dark\",\r\n  921: }\r\n  922: \r\n  923: \r\n  924: def _bookend_phase(beat_id, ordinal, total) -> str:\r\n  925:     \"\"\"``opening`` or ``closing`` for a bookend, by id then by position.\"\"\"\r\n  926:     bid = str(beat_id or \"\").lower()\r\n  927:     if \"open\" in bid:\r\n  928:         return \"opening\"\r\n  929:     if \"clos\" in bid or \"end\" in bid:\r\n  930:         return \"closing\"\r\n  931:     return \"opening\" if int(ordinal) * 2 < int(total or 1) else \"closing\"\r\n  932: \r\n  933: \r\n  934: def deterministic_leaf(spec, *, episode_seed, used=(), total=0) -> str:\r\n  935:     \"\"\"One complete checked-in clause for a spec, unique within the batch.\r\n  936: \r\n  937:     Deterministic collision PROBING rather than modulo-and-hope: two beats in\r\n  938:     the same mode would otherwise land on the same clause about one time in\r\n  939:     six, and an episode that says the same sentence twice is exactly the\r\n  940:     repetition this sprint exists to remove.\r\n  941:     \"\"\"\r\n  942:     mode = str(spec.get(\"mode\") or \"\")\r\n  943:     role = normalize_role(spec.get(\"role\"))\r\n  944:     ordinal = int(spec.get(\"ordinal\") or 0)\r\n  945:     if role != \"character_video\":\r\n  946:         phase = _bookend_phase(spec.get(\"beat_id\"), ordinal, total)\r\n  947:         candidate = GHOST_FALLBACK_BOOKENDS.get((phase, mode), \"\")\r\n  948:         if candidate and candidate not in used:\r\n  949:             return candidate\r\n  950:     pool = GHOST_FALLBACK_CLAUSES.get(mode) or ()\r\n  951:     if not pool:\r\n  952:         raise GhostAuthorError(\r\n  953:             \"no deterministic Ghost clause pool for mode %r\" % (mode,))\r\n  954:     start = _hash_int(episode_seed, spec.get(\"beat_id\"), mode,\r\n  955:                       GHOST_AUTHOR_VERSION) % len(pool)\r\n  956:     for step in range(len(pool)):\r\n  957:         candidate = pool[(start + step) % len(pool)]\r\n  958:         if candidate not in used:\r\n  959:             return candidate\r\n  960:     return pool[start]\r\n  961: \r\n  962: \r\n  963: def deterministic_batch(specs, *, episode_seed, already_used=()) -> dict:\r\n  964:     \"\"\"``{opaque_id: leaf}`` -- a complete batch, never a partial salvage.\r\n  965: \r\n  966:     ``already_used`` carries leaves decided elsewhere in the SAME episode --\r\n  967:     replayed rows, typically. Without it a mixed episode could hand two beats\r\n  968:     the same checked-in clause, because each call would probe for collisions\r\n  969:     only against its own subset, and an episode that says the same sentence\r\n  970:     twice is the repetition this sprint exists to remove.\r\n  971:     \"\"\"\r\n  972:     out = {}\r\n  973:     used = [str(leaf) for leaf in (already_used or ())]\r\n  974:     total = len(specs or ())\r\n  975:     for spec in specs or ():\r\n  976:         leaf = deterministic_leaf(spec, episode_seed=episode_seed,\r\n  977:                                   used=used, total=total)\r\n  978:         used.append(leaf)\r\n  979:         out[spec[\"id\"]] = leaf\r\n  980:     return out\r\n  981: \r\n  982: \r\n  983: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  984: # The stored object.\r\n  985: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  986: \r\n  987: #: THE EXACT FIELD SET. Extra or missing keys are a malformed object, and a\r\n  988: #: malformed object fails closed rather than degrading to v1.\r\n  989: GHOST_PROMPT_FIELDS = (\r\n  990:     \"schema_version\", \"author_version\", \"mode\", \"motif_cue\", \"drawable_beat\",\r\n  991:     \"source\", \"model_id\", \"request_sha256\", \"output_sha256\", \"fallback_reason\",\r\n  992: )\r\n  993: \r\n  994: _HEX64_RE = re.compile(r\"\\A[0-9a-f]{64}\\Z\")\r\n  995: \r\n  996: \r\n  997: def build_ghost_prompt_object(spec, drawable_beat, *, source,\r\n  998:                               fallback_reason=\"\") -> dict:\r\n  999:     \"\"\"Assemble one durable ``ghost_prompt`` object and validate it.\"\"\"\r\n 1000:     obj = {\r\n 1001:         \"schema_version\": GHOST_AUTHOR_SCHEMA_VERSION,\r\n 1002:         \"author_version\": GHOST_AUTHOR_VERSION,\r\n 1003:         \"mode\": spec[\"mode\"],\r\n 1004:         \"motif_cue\": spec[\"motif_cue\"],\r\n 1005:         \"drawable_beat\": _norm(drawable_beat),\r\n 1006:         \"source\": str(source),\r\n 1007:         \"model_id\": spec[\"model_id\"],\r\n 1008:         \"request_sha256\": spec[\"request_sha256\"],\r\n 1009:         \"output_sha256\": output_sha256(_norm(drawable_beat)),\r\n 1010:         \"fallback_reason\": str(fallback_reason or \"\"),\r\n 1011:     }\r\n 1012:     validate_ghost_prompt_object(obj)\r\n 1013:     return obj\r\n 1014: \r\n 1015: \r\n 1016: def validate_ghost_prompt_object(obj, *, expected_request_sha256=None) -> dict:\r\n 1017:     \"\"\"Raise unless ``obj`` is a complete, self-consistent stored object.\"\"\"\r\n 1018:     if not isinstance(obj, dict):\r\n 1019:         raise GhostAuthorValidationError(\r\n 1020:             \"ghost_prompt is a %s, not an object\" % type(obj).__name__)\r\n 1021:     if set(obj) != set(GHOST_PROMPT_FIELDS):\r\n 1022:         extra = sorted(set(obj) - set(GHOST_PROMPT_FIELDS))\r\n 1023:         missing = sorted(set(GHOST_PROMPT_FIELDS) - set(obj))\r\n 1024:         raise GhostAuthorValidationError(\r\n 1025:             \"ghost_prompt field set is wrong (extra=%s missing=%s)\"\r\n 1026:             % (\",\".join(extra) or \"-\", \",\".join(missing) or \"-\"))\r\n 1027:     if obj[\"schema_version\"] != GHOST_AUTHOR_SCHEMA_VERSION:\r\n 1028:         raise GhostAuthorValidationError(\r\n 1029:             \"ghost_prompt schema_version %r is not %d\"\r\n 1030:             % (obj[\"schema_version\"], GHOST_AUTHOR_SCHEMA_VERSION))\r\n 1031:     if obj[\"author_version\"] != GHOST_AUTHOR_VERSION:\r\n 1032:         raise GhostAuthorValidationError(\r\n 1033:             \"ghost_prompt author_version %r is not %r\"\r\n 1034:             % (obj[\"author_version\"], GHOST_AUTHOR_VERSION))\r\n 1035:     if obj[\"mode\"] not in GHOST_MODES:\r\n 1036:         raise GhostAuthorValidationError(\r\n 1037:             \"ghost_prompt mode %r is not one of %s\"\r\n 1038:             % (obj[\"mode\"], \", \".join(GHOST_MODES)))\r\n 1039:     if obj[\"source\"] not in GHOST_AUTHOR_SOURCES:\r\n 1040:         raise GhostAuthorValidationError(\r\n 1041:             \"ghost_prompt source %r is not one of %s\"\r\n 1042:             % (obj[\"source\"], \", \".join(GHOST_AUTHOR_SOURCES)))\r\n 1043:     for field in (\"motif_cue\", \"drawable_beat\", \"model_id\"):\r\n 1044:         if not _norm(obj[field]):\r\n 1045:             raise GhostAuthorValidationError(\r\n 1046:                 \"ghost_prompt %s is empty\" % field)\r\n 1047:     for field in (\"request_sha256\", \"output_sha256\"):\r\n 1048:         if not _HEX64_RE.match(str(obj[field] or \"\")):\r\n 1049:             raise GhostAuthorValidationError(\r\n 1050:                 \"ghost_prompt %s is not 64 lowercase hex characters\" % field)\r\n 1051:     if output_sha256(obj[\"drawable_beat\"]) != obj[\"output_sha256\"]:\r\n 1052:         raise GhostAuthorValidationError(\r\n 1053:             \"ghost_prompt output_sha256 does not hash its own drawable_beat\")\r\n 1054:     if obj[\"source\"] == \"deterministic_fallback\" and not obj[\"fallback_reason\"]:\r\n 1055:         raise GhostAuthorValidationError(\r\n 1056:             \"ghost_prompt source=deterministic_fallback carries no reason -- \"\r\n 1057:             \"the reason is what stops a reuse laundering it into proof\")\r\n 1058:     if obj[\"source\"] != \"deterministic_fallback\" and obj[\"fallback_reason\"]:\r\n 1059:         raise GhostAuthorValidationError(\r\n 1060:             \"ghost_prompt source=%s carries a fallback_reason\" % obj[\"source\"])\r\n 1061:     if (expected_request_sha256 is not None\r\n 1062:             and obj[\"request_sha256\"] != expected_request_sha256):\r\n 1063:         raise GhostAuthorValidationError(\r\n 1064:             \"ghost_prompt request_sha256 does not match the recomputed \"\r\n 1065:             \"request identity\")\r\n 1066:     return obj\r\n 1067: \r\n 1068: \r\n 1069: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n 1070: # The installed SD1 token measurer.\r\n 1071: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n 1072: \r\n 1073: _TOKENIZER_CACHE = {}\r\n 1074: \r\n 1075: \r\n 1076: def _test_mode() -> bool:\r\n 1077:     return os.environ.get(\"OTR_TEST_MODE\") == \"1\"\r\n 1078: \r\n 1079: \r\n 1080: def _installed_sd1_tokenizer():\r\n 1081:     \"\"\"The lazily instantiated ComfyUI SD1 tokenizer, contract-checked once.\r\n 1082: \r\n 1083:     Contract, asserted rather than assumed: max length 77, BOS 49406, EOS/pad\r\n 1084:     49407. Hugging Face's own ``model_max_length`` on this tokenizer reads\r\n 1085:     8192, which is metadata about the text model and says nothing about the\r\n 1086:     77-token CLIP window -- trusting it is how a counter silently stops\r\n 1087:     counting.\r\n 1088:     \"\"\"\r\n 1089:     if \"tokenizer\" in _TOKENIZER_CACHE:\r\n 1090:         return _TOKENIZER_CACHE[\"tokenizer\"]\r\n 1091:     try:\r\n 1092:         from comfy.sd1_clip import SD1Tokenizer  # type: ignore\r\n 1093:     except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 -- any import failure is the same\r\n 1094:         raise GhostTokenizerUnavailable(\r\n 1095:             \"the installed ComfyUI SD1 tokenizer could not be imported (%s). \"\r\n 1096:             \"Ghost Prompt v2 measures its window with the real encoder and \"\r\n 1097:             \"will not substitute a whitespace estimate.\" % (exc,)) from exc\r\n 1098:     tok = SD1Tokenizer()\r\n 1099:     clip = getattr(tok, \"clip_l\", None)\r\n 1100:     if clip is None:\r\n 1101:         raise GhostTokenizerUnavailable(\r\n 1102:             \"the installed SD1Tokenizer exposes no clip_l section\")\r\n 1103:     if int(getattr(clip, \"max_length\", 0)) != GHOST_CLIP_WINDOW_TOKENS:\r\n 1104:         raise GhostTokenizerUnavailable(\r\n 1105:             \"the installed SD1 tokenizer reports max_length %r, not %d\"\r\n 1106:             % (getattr(clip, \"max_length\", None), GHOST_CLIP_WINDOW_TOKENS))\r\n 1107:     if getattr(clip, \"start_token\", None) is None or \\\r\n 1108:             getattr(clip, \"end_token\", None) is None:\r\n 1109:         raise GhostTokenizerUnavailable(\r\n 1110:             \"the installed SD1 tokenizer has no real start/end tokens\")\r\n 1111:     _TOKENIZER_CACHE[\"tokenizer\"] = tok\r\n 1112:     return tok\r\n 1113: \r\n 1114: \r\n 1115: def measure_clip_tokens(text) -> tuple:\r\n 1116:     \"\"\"``(tokens, windows)`` for ``text`` on the INSTALLED SD1 encoder.\r\n 1117: \r\n 1118:     ``tokens`` is the payload count plus BOS and EOS for every returned\r\n 1119:     section; ``windows`` is how many 77-token sections came back. Padded row\r\n 1120:     LENGTH is never counted -- every row is padded to 77, so counting it would\r\n 1121:     report 77 for a three-word prompt and make the whole gate meaningless.\r\n 1122: \r\n 1123:     Measured, not derived: 75 payload tokens is 77 total in one window, and 76\r\n 1124:     payload tokens spills to two.\r\n 1125:     \"\"\"\r\n 1126:     tok = _installed_sd1_tokenizer()\r\n 1127:     rows = tok.tokenize_with_weights(str(text or \"\"), return_word_ids=True)[\"l\"]\r\n 1128:     payload = sum(1 for row in rows for entry in row if entry[2] != 0)\r\n 1129:     windows = len(rows)\r\n 1130:     return payload + 2 * windows, windows\r\n 1131: \r\n 1132: \r\n 1133: def resolve_token_measure(token_measure_fn=None) -> Optional[Callable]:\r\n 1134:     \"\"\"The measurer this call should use, or ``None`` when it may be skipped.\r\n 1135: \r\n 1136:     An INJECTED measurer always gates -- a test that supplies one is asking for\r\n 1137:     the gate, not asking to be excused from it. Only the UNAVAILABLE installed\r\n 1138:     tokenizer may be skipped, and only under ``OTR_TEST_MODE``; production\r\n 1139:     fails closed.\r\n 1140:     \"\"\"\r\n 1141:     if token_measure_fn is not None:\r\n 1142:         return token_measure_fn\r\n 1143:     try:\r\n 1144:         _installed_sd1_tokenizer()\r\n 1145:     except GhostTokenizerUnavailable as exc:\r\n 1146:         if _test_mode():\r\n 1147:             # SAID OUT LOUD (agy review, 2026-08-22). A silently ungated run\r\n 1148:             # publishes zero token counts that look like a measurement; a\r\n 1149:             # reader of a headless log must be able to see that nothing was\r\n 1150:             # measured and why.\r\n 1151:             _LOG.warning(\r\n 1152:                 \"[ghost_signal_author] token gate SKIPPED under OTR_TEST_MODE: \"\r\n 1153:                 \"%s -- receipts will carry no counts and no clip_counter\", exc)\r\n 1154:             return None\r\n 1155:         raise\r\n 1156:     return measure_clip_tokens\r\n 1157: \r\n 1158: \r\n 1159: #: The measurer's own identity, stamped beside every count so a receipt says\r\n 1160: #: which counter produced it.\r\n 1161: GHOST_CLIP_COUNTER = \"comfy.sd1_clip.SD1Tokenizer/%s\" % GHOST_AUTHOR_VERSION\r\n 1162: \r\n 1163: \r\n 1164: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n 1165: # The shared finalizer -- ONE path for author-time admission and render.\r\n 1166: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n 1167: \r\n 1168: def _banana_module():\r\n 1169:     try:\r\n 1170:         from .. import _otr_banana_route as _br  # type: ignore\r\n 1171:     except ImportError:  # pragma: no cover -- flat test imports\r\n 1172:         import _otr_banana_route as _br  # type: ignore\r\n 1173:     return _br\r\n 1174: \r\n 1175: \r\n 1176: def finalize_ghost_prompt_v2(*, role, style, mode, motif_cue, drawable_beat,\r\n 1177:                              ledger_meta=None, token_measure_fn=None,\r\n 1178:                              banana_enabled=None) -> dict:\r\n 1179:     \"\"\"Compose, transform, validate and MEASURE one Ghost v2 prompt.\r\n 1180: \r\n 1181:     The single path both sides use. Author-time admission calls it to find out\r\n 1182:     whether a candidate leaf survives the real style, the real banana gate and\r\n 1183:     the real tokenizer; the render driver calls it to build the request. Two\r\n 1184:     code paths could not have been kept in agreement -- the whole point of the\r\n 1185:     admission check is that it predicts the render exactly.\r\n 1186: \r\n 1187:     IT NEVER TRIMS AND NEVER REPAIRS. Style cue, motif, law and negative are\r\n 1188:     immutable, and the only lever an over-budget candidate has is a different\r\n 1189:     LEAF -- which is the model's field, not this function's.\r\n 1190:     \"\"\"\r\n 1191:     composed = _gsp.compose_ghost_prompt_v2(\r\n 1192:         role=role, style=style, mode=mode, motif_cue=motif_cue,\r\n 1193:         drawable_beat=drawable_beat)\r\n 1194:     positive = str(composed[\"positive\"])\r\n 1195:     negative = str(composed[\"negative\"])\r\n 1196:     components = dict(composed[\"components\"])\r\n 1197: \r\n 1198:     meta = ledger_meta if isinstance(ledger_meta, dict) else {}\r\n 1199:     banana = _banana_module()\r\n 1200:     variety_key = str(meta.get(\"freeze_timestamp\") or \"\")\r\n 1201:     gate_on = (banana.banana_gate(meta, lane=\"video\")\r\n 1202:                if banana_enabled is None else bool(banana_enabled))\r\n 1203: \r\n 1204:     if gate_on:\r\n 1205:         result = banana.apply(positive, variety_key=variety_key,\r\n 1206:                               shield_quoted_card_text=False)\r\n 1207:         positive = result.text\r\n 1208:         receipt = banana.receipt_keys(result)\r\n 1209:         # THE COMPONENTS GO THROUGH THE SAME TABLE, so a substitution INSIDE\r\n 1210:         # the leaf (\"revolver\" -> a fruit) is recognised as the transform doing\r\n 1211:         # its job rather than as the leaf having been lost. Validating the\r\n 1212:         # pre-transform components against a post-transform prompt would fail\r\n 1213:         # exactly when the route fired, which is the one case that matters.\r\n 1214:         components = {\r\n 1215:             name: banana.apply(text, variety_key=variety_key,\r\n 1216:                                shield_quoted_card_text=False).text\r\n 1217:             for name, text in components.items()\r\n 1218:         }\r\n 1219:     else:\r\n 1220:         receipt = banana.off_receipt(positive, variety_key=variety_key)\r\n 1221: \r\n 1222:     for name, text in components.items():\r\n 1223:         if text and text not in positive:\r\n 1224:             raise GhostBudgetError(\r\n 1225:                 \"Ghost v2 protected component %r did not survive into the \"\r\n 1226:                 \"final positive -- a protected field is never trimmed or \"\r\n 1227:                 \"repaired, so this is a composer or transform defect\" % (name,))\r\n 1228: \r\n 1229:     if len(positive) > _gsp.GHOST_PROMPT_MAX_CHARS:\r\n 1230:         raise GhostBudgetError(\r\n 1231:             \"Ghost v2 positive is %d characters, over the %d ceiling\"\r\n 1232:             % (len(positive), _gsp.GHOST_PROMPT_MAX_CHARS))\r\n 1233: \r\n 1234:     measure = resolve_token_measure(token_measure_fn)\r\n 1235:     if measure is None:\r\n 1236:         pos_tokens = pos_windows = neg_tokens = neg_windows = 0\r\n 1237:     else:\r\n 1238:         pos_tokens, pos_windows = measure(positive)\r\n 1239:         neg_tokens, neg_windows = measure(negative)\r\n 1240:         for label, tokens, windows in ((\"positive\", pos_tokens, pos_windows),\r\n 1241:                                        (\"negative\", neg_tokens, neg_windows)):\r\n 1242:             if windows > 1 or tokens > GHOST_CLIP_WINDOW_TOKENS:\r\n 1243:                 raise GhostBudgetError(\r\n 1244:                     \"Ghost v2 %s measures %d installed SD1 tokens across %d \"\r\n 1245:                     \"window(s), over the one-window %d admission -- the render \"\r\n 1246:                     \"boundary refuses this rather than trimming a protected \"\r\n 1247:                     \"field\" % (label, tokens, windows,\r\n 1248:                                GHOST_CLIP_WINDOW_TOKENS))\r\n 1249: \r\n 1250:     return {\r\n 1251:         \"positive\": positive,\r\n 1252:         \"negative\": negative,\r\n 1253:         \"components\": components,\r\n 1254:         \"slots\": list(composed.get(\"slots\") or ()),\r\n 1255:         \"banana_receipt\": receipt,\r\n 1256:         \"banana_gate\": bool(gate_on),\r\n 1257:         \"positive_clip_tokens\": int(pos_tokens),\r\n 1258:         \"positive_clip_windows\": int(pos_windows),\r\n 1259:         \"negative_clip_tokens\": int(neg_tokens),\r\n 1260:         \"negative_clip_windows\": int(neg_windows),\r\n 1261:         \"clip_window_max\": GHOST_CLIP_WINDOW_TOKENS,\r\n 1262:         \"clip_counter\": GHOST_CLIP_COUNTER if measure is not None else \"\",\r\n 1263:         \"measured\": measure is not None,\r\n 1264:     }\r\n 1265: \r\n 1266: \r\n 1267: def candidate_fits(*, role, style, mode, motif_cue, drawable_beat,\r\n 1268:                    ledger_meta=None, token_measure_fn=None,\r\n 1269:                    target_tokens=GHOST_AUTHOR_TOKEN_TARGET) -> tuple:\r\n 1270:     \"\"\"``(ok, reason)`` for an AUTHOR-TIME candidate leaf.\r\n 1271: \r\n 1272:     Author-time aims below the render ceiling on purpose: the leaf is the only\r\n 1273:     thing that may change to make a prompt fit, so the composer's own surfaces\r\n 1274:     have to keep the headroom the banana route may later spend.\r\n 1275:     \"\"\"\r\n 1276:     try:\r\n 1277:         final = finalize_ghost_prompt_v2(\r\n 1278:             role=role, style=style, mode=mode, motif_cue=motif_cue,\r\n 1279:             drawable_beat=drawable_beat, ledger_meta=ledger_meta,\r\n 1280:             token_measure_fn=token_measure_fn)\r\n 1281:     except (GhostBudgetError, _gsp.GhostPromptError) as exc:\r\n 1282:         return False, str(exc)\r\n 1283:     if final[\"measured\"] and final[\"positive_clip_tokens\"] > int(target_tokens):\r\n 1284:         return False, (\"candidate measures %d installed SD1 tokens, over the \"\r\n 1285:                        \"%d author-time target\"\r\n 1286:                        % (final[\"positive_clip_tokens\"], int(target_tokens)))\r\n 1287:     return True, \"\"\r\n 1288: \r\n 1289: \r\n 1290: def assert_shell_fits(styles, *, ledger_meta=None, token_measure_fn=None):\r\n 1291:     \"\"\"Prove every shipped pack/mode/longest-motif shell can hold a leaf.\r\n 1292: \r\n 1293:     Run BEFORE any model call. A failure here is a COMPOSER CONSTANT DEFECT --\r\n 1294:     a pack cue, a mode law or a motif that leaves no room for the shortest\r\n 1295:     legal fallback -- and no number of model retries can fix it, so it must not\r\n 1296:     be discovered as a retry loop at author time.\r\n 1297:     \"\"\"\r\n 1298:     longest_motif = max(\r\n 1299:         [motif_for_character(\r\n 1300:             {\"silhouette\": \"towering\", \"costume\": \"charcoal overcoat\",\r\n 1301:              \"prop\": \"briefcase\"}, mode)\r\n 1302:          for mode in GHOST_MODES]\r\n 1303:         + list(GHOST_BOOKEND_MOTIFS.values()), key=len)\r\n 1304:     shortest_leaf = min(\r\n 1305:         [c for pool in GHOST_FALLBACK_CLAUSES.values() for c in pool]\r\n 1306:         + list(GHOST_FALLBACK_BOOKENDS.values()), key=len)\r\n 1307:     failures = []\r\n 1308:     for style in styles:\r\n 1309:         for mode in GHOST_MODES:\r\n 1310:             role = (\"character_video\" if mode == \"figure\"\r\n 1311:                     else \"announcer_visual\")\r\n 1312:             ok, reason = candidate_fits(\r\n 1313:                 role=role, style=style, mode=mode,\r\n 1314:                 motif_cue=longest_motif, drawable_beat=shortest_leaf,\r\n 1315:                 ledger_meta=ledger_meta, token_measure_fn=token_measure_fn)\r\n 1316:             if not ok:\r\n 1317:                 failures.append(\"%s/%s: %s\" % (\r\n 1318:                     getattr(style, \"style_id\", \"?\"), mode, reason))\r\n 1319:     if failures:\r\n 1320:         raise GhostBudgetError(\r\n 1321:             \"Ghost v2 mechanical shell does not fit for %s -- this is a \"\r\n 1322:             \"composer constant defect, not a model retry\"\r\n 1323:             % \"; \".join(failures))\r\n 1324: \r\n 1325: \r\n 1326: __all__ = [\r\n 1327:     \"GHOST_AUTHOR_SCHEMA_VERSION\", \"GHOST_AUTHOR_VERSION\", \"GHOST_MODES\",\r\n 1328:     \"GHOST_NON_FIGURE_MODES\", \"GHOST_AUTHOR_SOURCES\", \"GHOST_LEAF_MAX_CHARS\",\r\n 1329:     \"GHOST_LEAF_MIN_WORDS\", \"GHOST_LEAF_MAX_WORDS\", \"GHOST_CLIP_WINDOW_TOKENS\",\r\n 1330:     \"GHOST_AUTHOR_TOKEN_TARGET\", \"GHOST_BATCH_TEMPERATURE\",\r\n 1331:     \"GHOST_BATCH_BASE_TOKENS\", \"GHOST_BATCH_PER_SHOT_TOKENS\",\r\n 1332:     \"GHOST_BATCH_RULES\", \"GHOST_BATCH_EXAMPLES\",\r\n 1333:     \"GHOST_TEMPLATE_SHA256\", \"GHOST_REQUEST_HASH_KEYS\", \"GHOST_PROMPT_FIELDS\",\r\n 1334:     \"GHOST_CHARACTER_CYCLE\",\r\n 1335:     \"GHOST_DETERMINISTIC_MODEL_ID\",\r\n 1336:     \"GHOST_BOOKEND_MOTIFS\", \"GHOST_FALLBACK_CLAUSES\",\r\n 1337:     \"GHOST_FALLBACK_BOOKENDS\", \"GHOST_ROLES\", \"GHOST_ARC_CUES\",\r\n 1338:     \"GHOST_CLIP_COUNTER\",\r\n 1339:     \"GhostAuthorError\", \"GhostAuthorParseError\", \"GhostAuthorValidationError\",\r\n 1340:     \"GhostTokenizerUnavailable\", \"GhostBudgetError\",\r\n 1341:     \"batch_output_tokens\", \"normalize_role\", \"strip_cast_names\",\r\n 1342:     \"sanitize_intent\", \"normalize_emotion\", \"map_arc\", \"schedule_ghost_modes\",\r\n 1343:     \"motif_for_character\", \"motif_for_bookend\", \"opaque_id\",\r\n 1344:     \"build_ghost_author_specs\", \"request_sha256\", \"output_sha256\",\r\n 1345:     \"build_batch_prompt\", \"parse_batch_response\", \"validate_drawable_beat\",\r\n 1346:     \"deterministic_leaf\", \"deterministic_batch\", \"build_ghost_prompt_object\",\r\n 1347:     \"validate_ghost_prompt_object\", \"measure_clip_tokens\",\r\n 1348:     \"resolve_token_measure\", \"finalize_ghost_prompt_v2\", \"candidate_fits\",\r\n 1349:     \"assert_shell_fits\",\r\n 1350: ]\r\n===== nodes\\_otr_video_engines\\ghost_signal_prompt.py =====\r\n    1: \"\"\"``ghost_signal_prompt`` -- the PURE prompt authority for the Ghost Signal lane.\r\n    2: \r\n    3: Ghost Signal (``animatediff15_video``) owns its subject entirely: it declares\r\n    4: ``accepts_still = False``, so no still is ever minted for it and no image\r\n    5: carries the look. Everything the viewer sees is decided by the text in this\r\n    6: module, which makes this file the lane's whole visual vocabulary.\r\n    7: \r\n    8: PURE BY CONTRACT. No I/O, no model load, no LLM call, no ledger mutation, no\r\n    9: import of ``render_driver``. Everything here is a deterministic function of its\r\n   10: arguments, so two identical inputs produce byte-identical output and the whole\r\n   11: surface is testable on a CPU box with nothing installed. The render driver\r\n   12: resolves the live authorities (style pack, motion clause, pack register) and\r\n   13: hands the resolved values in; this module never reaches back out for them.\r\n   14: That direction is deliberate -- the driver already owns the role-to-register\r\n   15: mapping, and a second copy here is a table that drifts once.\r\n   16: \r\n   17: THE COMPOSER'S ORDER IS IMMUTABLE (plan section 6.2). An empty slot is omitted\r\n   18: without reordering the others:\r\n   19: \r\n   20:   1. pack cue          the selected visual-style pack's own look token\r\n   21:   2. subject identity  the durable sigil / announcer face / console emblem\r\n   22:   3. framing lock      the role's shot floor\r\n   23:   4. beat action       the authored motion clause, or a deterministic fallback\r\n   24:   5. emotion           the beat's recorded mood\r\n   25:   6. story accent      the beat's arc phase, one phrase\r\n   26:   7. shot law          the affirmative closing directive\r\n   27: \r\n   28: WHY A SIGIL AND NOT A DESCRIPTION. SD1.5 at 512x288 across a sliding 16-frame\r\n   29: context will not hold a face. Asking it to is how a lane ships a character who\r\n   30: is a different person every beat. So Ghost does not ask: it distills each cast\r\n   31: member into four HERALDIC cues -- silhouette, one asymmetrical landmark, one\r\n   32: costume colour or item, one handheld prop -- which are the properties a small\r\n   33: model CAN hold across a timeline. The result reads as the same figure beat to\r\n   34: beat because the figure is a shape and a colour, not a likeness.\r\n   35: \r\n   36: Character drift is ACCEPTED on this project (operator, 2026-08-14). The sigil is\r\n   37: not an attempt to reopen that; it is what makes a prompt-only lane legible at\r\n   38: all when there is no still to inherit identity from.\r\n   39: \"\"\"\r\n   40: from __future__ import annotations\r\n   41: \r\n   42: import hashlib\r\n   43: import re\r\n   44: from typing import Optional\r\n   45: \r\n   46: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n   47: # Identity + budgets. Every one of these is pinned by tests.\r\n   48: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n   49: \r\n   50: #: The prompt-profile token the adapter declares and the driver branches on.\r\n   51: #: A capability string, never an engine-name comparison.\r\n   52: GHOST_PROMPT_PROFILE = \"ghost_signal_v1\"\r\n   53: \r\n   54: #: Stamped into observability as ``prompt_version`` so a published receipt says\r\n   55: #: which composer wrote the text. Bump with the composer, never in place.\r\n   56: GHOST_PROMPT_VERSION = \"ghost_signal_v1\"\r\n   57: \r\n   58: #: THE PROMPT v2 COMPOSER VERSION, and it is deliberately NOT the capability\r\n   59: #: token above. ``GHOST_PROMPT_PROFILE`` is what every engine DECLARES and what\r\n   60: #: the driver branches on; bumping it would silently unregister every peer from\r\n   61: #: its own lane. What changed is the composer, so the composer's version is what\r\n   62: #: moves -- a published receipt then says which text authority wrote the prompt\r\n   63: #: while the adapter keeps advertising the capability it still has.\r\n   64: GHOST_PROMPT_VERSION_V2 = \"ghost_signal_v2\"\r\n   65: \r\n   66: #: ``prompt_source`` for :func:`render_driver._stamp_prompt_meta`.\r\n   67: GHOST_PROMPT_SOURCE = \"ghost_signal\"\r\n   68: \r\n   69: #: THE HARD CEILING. Ghost owns it (the LTX 240 is a different lane's number and\r\n   70: #: is never touched). 320 leaves the banana route room to substitute one prop\r\n   71: #: without the common funnel ever having to cut a protected clause.\r\n   72: GHOST_PROMPT_MAX_CHARS = 320\r\n   73: \r\n   74: #: The normal operating band. Not a gate -- a target the composer aims at so the\r\n   75: #: banana headroom above is real rather than nominal.\r\n   76: GHOST_PROMPT_TARGET_LOW = 260\r\n   77: GHOST_PROMPT_TARGET_HIGH = 280\r\n   78: \r\n   79: #: The negative's own ceiling, same number, same phrase-safe rule.\r\n   80: GHOST_NEGATIVE_MAX_CHARS = 320\r\n   81: \r\n   82: #: The sigil's own ceiling. It is the one slot that may never be trimmed by the\r\n   83: #: composer, so it has to be bounded where it is BUILT.\r\n   84: GHOST_SIGIL_MAX_CHARS = 110\r\n   85: \r\n   86: #: The floor a shrinkable PACK surface may not go below. Under roughly this\r\n   87: #: length a motion register stops describing a movement and a console subject\r\n   88: #: stops describing a thing, so the composer would rather fail loudly than ship\r\n   89: #: a stub. Reached only if a future pack authors surfaces far longer than the\r\n   90: #: nine shipped ones.\r\n   91: GHOST_SHRINK_FLOOR_CHARS = 55\r\n   92: \r\n   93: #: LANE HYGIENE, and it leads the negative unconditionally. SD1.5 volunteers\r\n   94: #: lettering into any scene that smells like a sign, a poster or a radio dial --\r\n   95: #: which is most of this show. These five phrases are retained whole even when\r\n   96: #: the pack negative has to be cut short.\r\n   97: LANE_HYGIENE_NEGATIVE = (\r\n   98:     \"text\", \"watermark\", \"caption\", \"lettering\", \"subtitles\",\r\n   99: )\r\n  100: \r\n  101: #: The affirmative closing directive. AFFIRMATIVE because the negative channel\r\n  102: #: is live on this lane (cfg 8.0 with a real unconditional branch), so\r\n  103: #: exclusions belong in the negative and the positive states what to draw.\r\n  104: GHOST_SHOT_LAW = \"steady legible silhouette, one clear action, unbroken shot\"\r\n  105: \r\n  106: #: THE PROMPT v2 SHOT LAWS, one per representation, ALL AFFIRMATIVE, and since\r\n  107: #: 2026-08-22 ALL THREE NAME A PHYSICAL THING.\r\n  108: #:\r\n  109: #: There is no ``no people`` here and there never will be. The negative channel\r\n  110: #: is live on this lane, so exclusion belongs there; a positive clause that\r\n  111: #: attends to an absent human is a request for the model to think about one.\r\n  112: #:\r\n  113: #: THE FIRST DRAFT OF THESE LAWS IS WHAT TANKED THE PICTURES, and it is worth\r\n  114: #: writing down exactly how. They read \"one isolated emblem FILLING THE\r\n  115: #: COMPOSITION\" and \"an ABSTRACT SIGNAL FIELD filling the composition\" -- which\r\n  116: #: are instructions toward abstraction, and a 512x288 SD1.5 obliges by painting\r\n  117: #: TEXTURE instead of a subject. Measured across a same-seed pair: the v1 arm\r\n  118: #: rendered a recognisable subject on 4 of 4 sampled beats (a period radio set,\r\n  119: #: a figure at a lectern, a document, a tuning dial); the first-draft v2 arm\r\n  120: #: rendered 0 of 4, and its LLM-authored sibling managed 2 of 8 -- both of them\r\n  121: #: on the only beats whose authored leaf happened to name a real object (a\r\n  122: #: lantern, a radio dial).\r\n  123: #:\r\n  124: #: So the rule the evidence actually supports: LEGIBILITY TRACKS CONCRETE NOUNS.\r\n  125: #: ``object`` and ``signal`` still keep a person out of frame, but they do it by\r\n  126: #: naming a REAL THING to look at -- an object on a surface, an object lit from\r\n  127: #: within -- rather than by asking for a field of nothing.\r\n  128: GHOST_MODE_LAWS_V2 = {\r\n  129:     \"figure\": (\"mid-shot or wider, whole figure legible, one clear action, \"\r\n  130:                \"unbroken shot\"),\r\n  131:     \"object\": (\"the object fills the frame on a real surface, one clear \"\r\n  132:                \"action, unbroken shot\"),\r\n  133:     # \"LIT AGAINST\", not \"lit from within\": the motif prop is whatever the cast\r\n  134:     # row supplied, and most of them do not glow. A satchel lit from within is\r\n  135:     # a request the sampler cannot honour; a satchel lit against a dark room\r\n  136:     # with light moving across it is a picture, and it still keeps a person out\r\n  137:     # of frame, which is the only thing this mode owed.\r\n  138:     \"signal\": (\"the object lit against a dark room, moving light across it, \"\r\n  139:                \"unbroken shot\"),\r\n  140: }\r\n  141: \r\n  142: #: Role framing floors. The character floor is a MID-SHOT: closer than that and\r\n  143: #: the model starts trying to render a face it cannot hold.\r\n  144: GHOST_FRAMING = {\r\n  145:     \"character_video\": \"mid-shot or wider, whole figure legible\",\r\n  146:     \"announcer_visual\": \"medium shot of the console, dial face centered\",\r\n  147:     \"music_visual\": \"wide abstract frame, no people\",\r\n  148: }\r\n  149: \r\n  150: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  151: # Sigil vocabularies. Checked in, pinned by tests, deliberately small.\r\n  152: #\r\n  153: # These are matched as WHOLE WORDS against a candidate phrase. A bucket takes\r\n  154: # the FIRST phrase that matches it, in the source's own order, so a cast row\r\n  155: # that leads with its strongest cue keeps that cue.\r\n  156: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  157: \r\n  158: #: Bucket 1 -- silhouette / body shape. The single most durable property across\r\n  159: #: a sliding context: a tall thin figure stays a tall thin figure.\r\n  160: SIGIL_SILHOUETTE_WORDS = frozenset({\r\n  161:     \"tall\", \"short\", \"broad\", \"slight\", \"stooped\", \"lean\", \"heavyset\",\r\n  162:     \"wiry\", \"angular\", \"round\", \"towering\", \"compact\", \"gaunt\", \"stocky\",\r\n  163:     \"willowy\", \"hulking\", \"slender\", \"squat\", \"burly\", \"spare\", \"massive\",\r\n  164:     \"diminutive\", \"rangy\", \"thickset\", \"bent\", \"straight-backed\", \"narrow\",\r\n  165: })\r\n  166: \r\n  167: #: Bucket 2 -- one asymmetrical landmark. Asymmetry is what stops a figure\r\n  168: #: reading as a generic body: an eyepatch has a side, a scar has a side.\r\n  169: SIGIL_LANDMARK_WORDS = frozenset({\r\n  170:     \"scar\", \"eyepatch\", \"patch\", \"limp\", \"cane\", \"sling\", \"bandage\",\r\n  171:     \"crooked\", \"missing\", \"streak\", \"burn\", \"brand\", \"tattoo\", \"birthmark\",\r\n  172:     \"notch\", \"cropped\", \"shaved\", \"braid\", \"topknot\", \"moustache\", \"mustache\",\r\n  173:     \"beard\", \"spectacles\", \"monocle\", \"goggles\", \"scarred\", \"one-eyed\",\r\n  174:     \"bald\", \"greying\", \"grey-streaked\", \"silver-streaked\", \"hooked\",\r\n  175: })\r\n  176: \r\n  177: #: Bucket 3 -- costume colour or item. Colour survives at 512x288 when almost\r\n  178: #: nothing else does, so a coat's colour is worth more here than its cut.\r\n  179: SIGIL_COSTUME_WORDS = frozenset({\r\n  180:     \"coat\", \"overcoat\", \"trenchcoat\", \"greatcoat\", \"jacket\", \"uniform\",\r\n  181:     \"cloak\", \"cape\", \"gown\", \"dress\", \"suit\", \"waistcoat\", \"vest\", \"apron\",\r\n  182:     \"shawl\", \"robe\", \"hat\", \"fedora\", \"cap\", \"helmet\", \"hood\", \"scarf\",\r\n  183:     \"gloves\", \"boots\", \"collar\", \"tie\", \"sash\", \"belt\", \"armour\", \"armor\",\r\n  184:     \"black\", \"white\", \"red\", \"green\", \"blue\", \"brown\", \"grey\", \"gray\",\r\n  185:     \"crimson\", \"scarlet\", \"navy\", \"olive\", \"amber\", \"gold\", \"golden\",\r\n  186:     \"silver\", \"rust\", \"ochre\", \"violet\", \"purple\", \"cream\", \"tan\", \"charcoal\",\r\n  187: })\r\n  188: \r\n  189: #: Bucket 4 -- one handheld prop. A prop gives the figure something to DO,\r\n  190: #: which is what turns a portrait into a shot with motion in it.\r\n  191: #:\r\n  192: #: The source's own vocabulary is carried as written (operator directive\r\n  193: #: 2026-08-03): a revolver in the source is a revolver here.\r\n  194: SIGIL_PROP_WORDS = frozenset({\r\n  195:     \"lantern\", \"lamp\", \"torch\", \"candle\", \"revolver\", \"pistol\", \"rifle\",\r\n  196:     \"knife\", \"blade\", \"sword\", \"dagger\", \"book\", \"ledger\", \"journal\",\r\n  197:     \"letter\", \"map\", \"chart\", \"cane\", \"staff\", \"stick\", \"umbrella\",\r\n  198:     \"satchel\", \"bag\", \"case\", \"briefcase\", \"pipe\", \"cigarette\", \"flask\",\r\n  199:     \"bottle\", \"glass\", \"cup\", \"key\", \"keys\", \"rope\", \"chain\", \"hammer\",\r\n  200:     \"wrench\", \"microphone\", \"headset\", \"telegraph\", \"camera\", \"watch\",\r\n  201:     \"pocketwatch\", \"spyglass\", \"telescope\", \"basket\", \"crate\", \"lockbox\",\r\n  202: })\r\n  203: \r\n  204: #: Ordered bucket table: (bucket name, vocabulary). The ORDER here is the order\r\n  205: #: the four cues are emitted in, and it is fixed.\r\n  206: SIGIL_BUCKETS = (\r\n  207:     (\"silhouette\", SIGIL_SILHOUETTE_WORDS),\r\n  208:     (\"landmark\", SIGIL_LANDMARK_WORDS),\r\n  209:     (\"costume\", SIGIL_COSTUME_WORDS),\r\n  210:     (\"prop\", SIGIL_PROP_WORDS),\r\n  211: )\r\n  212: \r\n  213: #: NEUTRAL CUE POOLS -- the deterministic fill for a bucket the cast row never\r\n  214: #: supplied. These are DELIBERATE HERALDIC CUES, not a hidden model fallback:\r\n  215: #: the selection is a sha256 over (episode_seed, char_id, style_id, source),\r\n  216: #: so it is stable for a character across every beat of an episode and varies\r\n  217: #: between characters. Nothing is generated and nothing is asked of a model.\r\n  218: SIGIL_NEUTRAL_POOLS = {\r\n  219:     \"silhouette\": (\r\n  220:         \"a lean upright figure\", \"a broad steady figure\",\r\n  221:         \"a slight quick figure\", \"a tall stooped figure\",\r\n  222:         \"a compact square-shouldered figure\", \"an angular restless figure\",\r\n  223:     ),\r\n  224:     \"landmark\": (\r\n  225:         \"one shoulder held higher\", \"a pale streak through dark hair\",\r\n  226:         \"a bound left hand\", \"a heavy squared jaw\",\r\n  227:         \"a close-cropped head\", \"a deep-set shadowed brow\",\r\n  228:     ),\r\n  229:     \"costume\": (\r\n  230:         \"a charcoal coat\", \"a rust-red jacket\", \"an olive uniform\",\r\n  231:         \"a deep blue overcoat\", \"a cream collarless shirt\",\r\n  232:         \"a black shawl\",\r\n  233:     ),\r\n  234:     \"prop\": (\r\n  235:         \"carrying a shuttered lantern\", \"holding a folded chart\",\r\n  236:         \"gripping a worn leather satchel\", \"turning a brass key\",\r\n  237:         \"shouldering a coil of rope\", \"steadying a heavy ledger\",\r\n  238:     ),\r\n  239: }\r\n  240: \r\n  241: #: NEUTRAL KINETIC ACTIONS -- the last-resort action fill, same hash domain.\r\n  242: #: Reached only at cast-time preflight on a beat with no clause and no intent.\r\n  243: GHOST_NEUTRAL_ACTIONS = (\r\n  244:     \"turns slowly toward the light\",\r\n  245:     \"steps forward one pace\",\r\n  246:     \"lifts a hand and holds it still\",\r\n  247:     \"leans in and settles\",\r\n  248:     \"shifts weight and looks away\",\r\n  249:     \"draws breath and straightens\",\r\n  250: )\r\n  251: \r\n  252: #: BEAT-INTENT -> ACTION. A small checked-in table, because an intent word is\r\n  253: #: not a camera instruction and handing \"reveal\" to a diffusion model as-is\r\n  254: #: gets a picture of the word, not the movement.\r\n  255: GHOST_INTENT_ACTIONS = {\r\n  256:     \"reveal\": \"turns to reveal what was hidden\",\r\n  257:     \"confront\": \"squares up and holds ground\",\r\n  258:     \"accuse\": \"levels a pointed hand\",\r\n  259:     \"plead\": \"reaches out open-handed\",\r\n  260:     \"warn\": \"raises a hand sharply\",\r\n  261:     \"comfort\": \"leans in and softens\",\r\n  262:     \"explain\": \"gestures evenly while speaking\",\r\n  263:     \"question\": \"tilts the head and waits\",\r\n  264:     \"threaten\": \"advances one slow step\",\r\n  265:     \"retreat\": \"backs away and turns\",\r\n  266:     \"decide\": \"stops still and sets the jaw\",\r\n  267:     \"grieve\": \"bows the head and stills\",\r\n  268:     \"celebrate\": \"lifts both arms loosely\",\r\n  269:     \"escape\": \"breaks into a fast stride\",\r\n  270:     \"search\": \"sweeps a look across the space\",\r\n  271:     \"arrive\": \"enters and pauses at the threshold\",\r\n  272:     \"depart\": \"turns away and walks out of frame\",\r\n  273:     \"recall\": \"goes still and looks past the frame\",\r\n  274:     \"command\": \"gestures once, sharp and final\",\r\n  275:     \"submit\": \"lowers the shoulders and yields\",\r\n  276: }\r\n  277: \r\n  278: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  279: # Phrase machinery (pure).\r\n  280: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  281: \r\n  282: #: Phrases carrying these are DISCARDED from a sigil source. A camera\r\n  283: #: instruction, a medium name, a backdrop or a second-person address is not a\r\n  284: #: property of the person, and copying one into the sigil pins the WRONG thing\r\n  285: #: for every beat of the episode.\r\n  286: _SIGIL_REJECT_WORDS = frozenset({\r\n  287:     # camera / framing\r\n  288:     \"shot\", \"close-up\", \"closeup\", \"framing\", \"frame\", \"camera\", \"lens\",\r\n  289:     \"angle\", \"zoom\", \"pan\", \"tilt\", \"dolly\", \"crop\", \"headroom\", \"centered\",\r\n  290:     \"composition\", \"portrait\", \"three-quarter\", \"bokeh\", \"depth\",\r\n  291:     # medium / rendering\r\n  292:     \"photo\", \"photograph\", \"photographic\", \"painting\", \"illustration\",\r\n  293:     \"render\", \"rendering\", \"cinematic\", \"film\", \"lighting\", \"lit\", \"grain\",\r\n  294:     \"8k\", \"4k\", \"hdr\", \"detailed\", \"masterpiece\", \"quality\", \"style\",\r\n  295:     # background / setting\r\n  296:     \"background\", \"backdrop\", \"behind\", \"scene\", \"set\", \"room\", \"studio\",\r\n  297:     \"wall\", \"sky\", \"street\", \"interior\", \"exterior\", \"environment\",\r\n  298:     # second person\r\n  299:     \"you\", \"your\", \"yours\",\r\n  300: })\r\n  301: \r\n  302: #: Function words a trimmed phrase may not END on, so a cut lands on a complete\r\n  303: #: thought rather than an unfinished list.\r\n  304: #:\r\n  305: #: ONE DEFINITION, TWO CONSUMERS (2026-08-22). The still_word title card had the\r\n  306: #: same rule half-written in its own module with its own vocabulary; both now\r\n  307: #: read `_otr_shared.text_tails`, because two copies of one rule is a table that\r\n  308: #: drifts once. The name stays local so every existing reference and its\r\n  309: #: goldens keep working.\r\n  310: try:  # pragma: no cover -- exercised by both import shapes in the suite\r\n  311:     from .._otr_shared.text_tails import DANGLING_TAIL_WORDS as _DANGLING_TAIL_WORDS\r\n  312: except ImportError:  # pragma: no cover -- flat test imports\r\n  313:     from _otr_shared.text_tails import DANGLING_TAIL_WORDS as _DANGLING_TAIL_WORDS\r\n  314: \r\n  315: _WORD_RE = re.compile(r\"[a-z0-9][a-z0-9'\\-]*\")\r\n  316: _PHRASE_SPLIT_RE = re.compile(r\"[,;.!?]+\")\r\n  317: _WS_RE = re.compile(r\"\\s+\")\r\n  318: \r\n  319: \r\n  320: def _normalize_ws(text) -> str:\r\n  321:     \"\"\"Collapse all whitespace runs to single spaces and strip. Pure.\"\"\"\r\n  322:     return _WS_RE.sub(\" \", str(text or \"\")).strip()\r\n  323: \r\n  324: \r\n  325: def _split_phrases(text) -> list:\r\n  326:     \"\"\"Split a source into complete comma / semicolon / sentence phrases.\r\n  327: \r\n  328:     Order is preserved -- the buckets below take the FIRST match, so a cast row\r\n  329:     that leads with its strongest cue keeps it.\"\"\"\r\n  330:     out = []\r\n  331:     for chunk in _PHRASE_SPLIT_RE.split(_normalize_ws(text)):\r\n  332:         phrase = chunk.strip(\" -_\")\r\n  333:         if phrase:\r\n  334:             out.append(phrase)\r\n  335:     return out\r\n  336: \r\n  337: \r\n  338: def _words(phrase) -> set:\r\n  339:     \"\"\"The lowercase word set of a phrase, for whole-word bucket matching.\"\"\"\r\n  340:     return set(_WORD_RE.findall(str(phrase or \"\").lower()))\r\n  341: \r\n  342: \r\n  343: def _strip_name(phrase, name) -> str:\r\n  344:     \"\"\"Remove the cast NAME from a phrase.\r\n  345: \r\n  346:     The name is never emitted: a proper noun in the prompt is a request for a\r\n  347:     specific person the model has no idea about, and on the adaptation lanes it\r\n  348:     is also a metadata leak into the picture.\"\"\"\r\n  349:     name = _normalize_ws(name)\r\n  350:     if not name:\r\n  351:         return phrase\r\n  352:     out = phrase\r\n  353:     for token in [name] + name.split():\r\n  354:         token = token.strip()\r\n  355:         if len(token) < 3:\r\n  356:             continue\r\n  357:         out = re.sub(r\"\\b%s\\b\" % re.escape(token), \"\", out, flags=re.IGNORECASE)\r\n  358:     return _normalize_ws(out).strip(\" ,;-\")\r\n  359: \r\n  360: \r\n  361: def _sigil_hash(episode_seed, char_id, style_id, source_text) -> int:\r\n  362:     \"\"\"The ONE hash domain for every deterministic Ghost choice.\r\n  363: \r\n  364:     Pinned by tests. Includes the source text so two characters with identical\r\n  365:     seeds and ids but different cast rows do not collide into one figure.\"\"\"\r\n  366:     payload = \"|\".join((\r\n  367:         str(episode_seed), str(char_id or \"\"), str(style_id or \"\"),\r\n  368:         str(source_text or \"\"),\r\n  369:     ))\r\n  370:     return int(hashlib.sha256(payload.encode(\"utf-8\")).hexdigest()[:16], 16)\r\n  371: \r\n  372: \r\n  373: def _pick(pool, seed_int, salt) -> str:\r\n  374:     \"\"\"Deterministically choose one entry from a checked-in pool.\r\n  375: \r\n  376:     THE SALT IS HASHED WITH THE SEED, NOT ADDED TO IT, and that is a\r\n  377:     correctness fix rather than a style preference. Adding a per-bucket offset\r\n  378:     to one shared seed makes every bucket's choice the SAME function of\r\n  379:     ``seed_int mod len(pool)`` -- so two characters whose seeds happen to be\r\n  380:     congruent mod 6 would receive an identical sigil in ALL FOUR buckets at\r\n  381:     once, not one bucket in six. That collapses the odds of two sparse\r\n  382:     characters being visually indistinguishable from (1/6)^4 to 1/6, which on a\r\n  383:     lane whose entire identity mechanism is these four cues is a real defect.\r\n  384:     Hashing them together makes the four selections independent.\r\n  385:     \"\"\"\r\n  386:     pool = tuple(pool)\r\n  387:     if not pool:\r\n  388:         return \"\"\r\n  389:     mixed = int(hashlib.sha256(\r\n  390:         (\"%d|%s\" % (int(seed_int), salt)).encode(\"utf-8\")).hexdigest()[:16], 16)\r\n  391:     return pool[mixed % len(pool)]\r\n  392: \r\n  393: \r\n  394: def _trim_to(text, ceiling) -> str:\r\n  395:     \"\"\"Phrase-aware trim: drop WHOLE trailing comma phrases, then whole words.\r\n  396: \r\n  397:     TWO STAGES, AND THE SECOND ONE IS LOAD-BEARING. Dropping comma phrases is\r\n  398:     the preferred cut because it keeps every surviving clause intact. But the\r\n  399:     visual-style packs author their subject and motion-register surfaces as\r\n  400:     long, largely COMMA-FREE prose -- 178 and 209 characters on `recur_frac` --\r\n  401:     and against those a comma-only trimmer is a NO-OP: it finds one phrase,\r\n  402:     cannot drop it, and returns the text unchanged at its original length.\r\n  403: \r\n  404:     That is not hypothetical. It is what took the first live Ghost leg down:\r\n  405:     an announcer beat composed to 344 characters, the trimmer returned it\r\n  406:     untouched, and the composer raised rather than shipping an over-budget\r\n  407:     prompt. Falling back to a WORD boundary keeps the \"never mid-word\" promise\r\n  408:     while making the function actually able to do its job.\r\n  409:     \"\"\"\r\n  410:     text = _normalize_ws(text)\r\n  411:     ceiling = int(ceiling)\r\n  412:     if len(text) <= ceiling:\r\n  413:         return text\r\n  414:     parts = [p.strip() for p in text.split(\",\") if p.strip()]\r\n  415:     while len(parts) > 1:\r\n  416:         parts.pop()\r\n  417:         candidate = \", \".join(parts)\r\n  418:         if len(candidate) <= ceiling:\r\n  419:             return candidate\r\n  420:     head = (parts[0] if parts else \"\").strip()\r\n  421:     if len(head) <= ceiling:\r\n  422:         return head\r\n  423:     words = head.split()\r\n  424:     kept = []\r\n  425:     for word in words:\r\n  426:         candidate = \" \".join(kept + [word])\r\n  427:         if len(candidate) > ceiling:\r\n  428:             break\r\n  429:         kept.append(word)\r\n  430:     # NEVER END ON A DANGLING FUNCTION WORD. A word-boundary cut is honest but\r\n  431:     # it can still land on \"...two nested dial-eyes and a\", which reads to the\r\n  432:     # model as an unfinished list and is the kind of trailing fragment that\r\n  433:     # invites it to invent whatever came next. Dropping the dangling tail is\r\n  434:     # the same class of rule as \"never mid-word\": both are about handing the\r\n  435:     # sampler a complete thought.\r\n  436:     while kept and kept[-1].strip(\",.;:-\").lower() in _DANGLING_TAIL_WORDS:\r\n  437:         kept.pop()\r\n  438:     # A single word longer than the whole ceiling is the only case with no\r\n  439:     # word boundary to cut on; return it rather than the empty string, and let\r\n  440:     # the caller's own ceiling assertion be the thing that fails.\r\n  441:     return \" \".join(kept).rstrip(\" ,;:-\") or words[0]\r\n  442: \r\n  443: \r\n  444: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  445: # The durable subject sigil.\r\n  446: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  447: \r\n  448: #: Cast-row source priority. ``name`` is present only as a SIGNAL that the row\r\n  449: #: is sparse; the name itself is never emitted (see :func:`_strip_name`).\r\n  450: SIGIL_SOURCE_FIELDS = (\r\n  451:     \"portrait_prompt\", \"appearance\", \"description\", \"character_description\",\r\n  452: )\r\n  453: \r\n  454: \r\n  455: def distill_sigil_components(cast_row, *, episode_seed, char_id,\r\n  456:                              style_id) -> dict:\r\n  457:     \"\"\"The BUCKET CHOICES behind a sigil, before they are joined and trimmed.\r\n  458: \r\n  459:     Split out for Prompt v2 (2026-08-22) and shared with\r\n  460:     :func:`distill_subject_sigil`, which joins exactly what this returns -- so\r\n  461:     the durable sigil stays byte-stable while v2 can select a NON-FACE subset\r\n  462:     of the same distillation.\r\n  463: \r\n  464:     THE JOIN IS WHERE THE INFORMATION WAS LOST. The 110-character sigil ceiling\r\n  465:     lands on the LAST bucket, so reading a prop back out of the finished string\r\n  466:     finds a trimmed one or none at all. Exposing the choices before the join is\r\n  467:     what lets a recurrence motif carry a whole prop.\r\n  468: \r\n  469:     Returns ``{silhouette, landmark, costume, prop, gender_word, seed_int}``.\r\n  470:     A bucket the cast row never supplied carries its checked-in neutral pool\r\n  471:     entry, exactly as the sigil does -- there is ONE distillation, not two.\r\n  472:     \"\"\"\r\n  473:     row = cast_row if isinstance(cast_row, dict) else {}\r\n  474:     name = str(row.get(\"name\") or \"\")\r\n  475: \r\n  476:     source_text = \"\"\r\n  477:     for field in SIGIL_SOURCE_FIELDS:\r\n  478:         value = _normalize_ws(row.get(field))\r\n  479:         if value:\r\n  480:             source_text = value\r\n  481:             break\r\n  482: \r\n  483:     seed_int = _sigil_hash(episode_seed, char_id, style_id, source_text)\r\n  484: \r\n  485:     # Candidate phrases: name removed, camera / medium / backdrop / second\r\n  486:     # person discarded. A phrase that empties out after the name is gone was\r\n  487:     # only ever the name.\r\n  488:     candidates = []\r\n  489:     for phrase in _split_phrases(source_text):\r\n  490:         cleaned = _strip_name(phrase, name)\r\n  491:         if not cleaned:\r\n  492:             continue\r\n  493:         if _words(cleaned) & _SIGIL_REJECT_WORDS:\r\n  494:             continue\r\n  495:         candidates.append(cleaned)\r\n  496: \r\n  497:     components = {}\r\n  498:     used = set()\r\n  499:     for bucket, vocabulary in SIGIL_BUCKETS:\r\n  500:         chosen = \"\"\r\n  501:         for index, phrase in enumerate(candidates):\r\n  502:             if index in used:\r\n  503:                 continue\r\n  504:             if _words(phrase) & vocabulary:\r\n  505:                 chosen = phrase\r\n  506:                 used.add(index)\r\n  507:                 break\r\n  508:         if not chosen:\r\n  509:             chosen = _pick(SIGIL_NEUTRAL_POOLS[bucket], seed_int, bucket)\r\n  510:         components[bucket] = _normalize_ws(chosen).strip(\" ,;-\")\r\n  511: \r\n  512:     # Explicit gender ONLY. `normalize_gender` maps absence to \"other\", so\r\n  513:     # calling it on a blank field would invent a claim the cast row never made\r\n  514:     # -- and a wrong gender on a figure the viewer sees every beat is exactly\r\n  515:     # the correctness class the operator kept open when story quality closed.\r\n  516:     gender_word = \"\"\r\n  517:     raw_gender = str(row.get(\"gender\") or \"\").strip()\r\n  518:     if raw_gender:\r\n  519:         try:\r\n  520:             from .._otr_roster_gender import normalize_gender  # type: ignore\r\n  521:         except ImportError:  # pragma: no cover -- flat test imports\r\n  522:             from _otr_roster_gender import normalize_gender  # type: ignore\r\n  523:         normalized = normalize_gender(raw_gender)\r\n  524:         if normalized in (\"male\", \"female\"):\r\n  525:             gender_word = \"a man\" if normalized == \"male\" else \"a woman\"\r\n  526: \r\n  527:     components[\"gender_word\"] = gender_word\r\n  528:     components[\"seed_int\"] = seed_int\r\n  529:     return components\r\n  530: \r\n  531: \r\n  532: def distill_subject_sigil(cast_row, *, episode_seed, char_id, style_id) -> str:\r\n  533:     \"\"\"One durable heraldic identity for a character, as four ordered cues.\r\n  534: \r\n  535:     Deterministic and CREDIT-FREE: no model is asked anything, and the only\r\n  536:     non-source input is a sha256 over ``(episode_seed, char_id, style_id,\r\n  537:     source_text)``. The same character therefore carries the same sigil across\r\n  538:     every beat of an episode, and a different one in the next episode.\r\n  539: \r\n  540:     A missing cast row is legal and yields a fully pooled sigil -- it never\r\n  541:     reaches for the wardrobe writer or any other author. (Reading the raw row\r\n  542:     rather than ``_appearance_for_char`` is the point: that helper may invoke\r\n  543:     the optional wardrobe writer, which would turn a deterministic identity\r\n  544:     read into a hidden mutation and a credit spend.)\r\n  545: \r\n  546:     BYTE-STABLE ACROSS THE v2 REFACTOR. The distillation moved into\r\n  547:     :func:`distill_sigil_components` and this function joins its result in the\r\n  548:     same order with the same separators. Goldens pin the exact bytes, because\r\n  549:     a sigil that shifted would change the composed prompt of every legacy Ghost\r\n  550:     row that replays through the v1 compatibility path.\r\n  551:     \"\"\"\r\n  552:     components = distill_sigil_components(\r\n  553:         cast_row, episode_seed=episode_seed, char_id=char_id,\r\n  554:         style_id=style_id)\r\n  555:     cues = [components[bucket] for bucket, _vocabulary in SIGIL_BUCKETS\r\n  556:             if components.get(bucket)]\r\n  557:     gender_word = components.get(\"gender_word\") or \"\"\r\n  558:     body = \", \".join(cues)\r\n  559:     sigil = (\"%s, %s\" % (gender_word, body)) if gender_word else body\r\n  560:     return _trim_to(sigil, GHOST_SIGIL_MAX_CHARS)\r\n  561: \r\n  562: \r\n  563: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  564: # Action resolution (pure, ordered, deterministic).\r\n  565: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  566: \r\n  567: def resolve_action(role, *, motion_clause=None, pack_motion_fallback=\"\",\r\n  568:                    beat_intent=\"\", sigil_seed=0) -> str:\r\n  569:     \"\"\"The beat's action slot, resolved in the plan's exact order.\r\n  570: \r\n  571:     ``resolve_motion_clause_text`` legitimately returns ``None`` when the\r\n  572:     optional motion pass is off, when the stored object is a fallback, or when\r\n  573:     only the cast-time temporary shot exists. That is not permission to emit an\r\n  574:     empty action -- a Ghost beat with no action is a still frame with a\r\n  575:     sliding context wasted on it. So:\r\n  576: \r\n  577:     1. the whole validated motion clause when present;\r\n  578:     2. otherwise, for announcer / music, the pack's own role register;\r\n  579:     3. otherwise, for a character, the beat intent through the checked-in table;\r\n  580:     4. otherwise one COMPLETE neutral kinetic action on the sigil hash domain.\r\n  581: \r\n  582:     Every step is pack- or ledger-derived and credit-free.\r\n  583: \r\n  584:     STEP 3 NO LONGER COPIES AN UNMAPPED INTENT (Prompt v2, 2026-08-22). It used\r\n  585:     to emit ``\"moves with \" + the first six regex words``, which is how a\r\n  586:     published lane came to say *\"moves with erin risks exposure by transmitting\r\n  587:     a\"*: a cast name in the picture and a sentence with no end. A beat intent is\r\n  588:     free text a writer wrote for a human, not a camera instruction -- an unknown\r\n  589:     one now falls through to a complete checked-in action rather than shipping\r\n  590:     a fragment of it. Prompt v2 replaces this whole slot with an authored\r\n  591:     drawable leaf; this repair exists so a LEGACY row replaying through the v1\r\n  592:     compatibility path cannot reproduce the fragment either.\r\n  593:     \"\"\"\r\n  594:     clause = _normalize_ws(motion_clause)\r\n  595:     if clause:\r\n  596:         return clause\r\n  597: \r\n  598:     if role in (\"announcer_visual\", \"music_visual\"):\r\n  599:         register = _normalize_ws(pack_motion_fallback)\r\n  600:         if register:\r\n  601:             return register\r\n  602: \r\n  603:     intent = _normalize_ws(beat_intent).lower()\r\n  604:     if intent:\r\n  605:         mapped = GHOST_INTENT_ACTIONS.get(intent)\r\n  606:         if mapped:\r\n  607:             return mapped\r\n  608: \r\n  609:     return _pick(GHOST_NEUTRAL_ACTIONS, int(sigil_seed), \"action\")\r\n  610: \r\n  611: \r\n  612: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  613: # The negative.\r\n  614: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  615: \r\n  616: def compose_ghost_negative(style) -> str:\r\n  617:     \"\"\"Lane hygiene first, then the pack's own ``effective_negative``.\r\n  618: \r\n  619:     The hygiene head is retained COMPLETE and the pack phrases are added in\r\n  620:     authored order while the next WHOLE phrase fits. A phrase is never cut in\r\n  621:     half, and this budget is a character budget -- it makes no claim about a\r\n  622:     tokenizer.\r\n  623:     \"\"\"\r\n  624:     parts = list(LANE_HYGIENE_NEGATIVE)\r\n  625:     seen = {p.lower() for p in parts}\r\n  626: \r\n  627:     pack_negative = \"\"\r\n  628:     if style is not None:\r\n  629:         try:\r\n  630:             from .._otr_visual_styles import effective_negative  # type: ignore\r\n  631:         except ImportError:  # pragma: no cover -- flat test imports\r\n  632:             from _otr_visual_styles import effective_negative  # type: ignore\r\n  633:         pack_negative = str(effective_negative(style) or \"\")\r\n  634: \r\n  635:     head = \", \".join(parts)\r\n  636:     for chunk in pack_negative.split(\",\"):\r\n  637:         phrase = chunk.strip()\r\n  638:         if not phrase or phrase.lower() in seen:\r\n  639:             continue\r\n  640:         candidate = \"%s, %s\" % (head, phrase)\r\n  641:         if len(candidate) > GHOST_NEGATIVE_MAX_CHARS:\r\n  642:             break\r\n  643:         head = candidate\r\n  644:         seen.add(phrase.lower())\r\n  645:     return head\r\n  646: \r\n  647: \r\n  648: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  649: # The composer.\r\n  650: # --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #\r\n  651: \r\n  652: #: Slots the trimmer may drop, in the order it drops them. The subject sigil,\r\n  653: #: the authored motion clause, the mid-shot floor and the shot law are ABSENT\r\n  654: #: from this tuple on purpose: they are the four things the lane exists to\r\n  655: #: guarantee, so if they alone exceed the ceiling that is a composer defect to\r\n  656: #: fail on, not a prompt to quietly truncate.\r\n  657: GHOST_TRIM_ORDER = (\"story_accent\", \"emotion\", \"framing\")\r\n  658: \r\n  659: \r\n  660: class GhostPromptError(ValueError):\r\n  661:     \"\"\"The protected Ghost slots alone exceed the hard ceiling.\"\"\"\r\n  662: \r\n  663: \r\n  664: def compose_ghost_prompt(*, role, style, subject_sigil=\"\", motion_clause=None,\r\n  665:                          pack_motion_fallback=\"\", beat_intent=\"\", emotion=\"\",\r\n  666:                          story_accent=\"\", open_subject=\"\", sigil_seed=0) -> dict:\r\n  667:     \"\"\"Compose one Ghost positive prompt plus its negative.\r\n  668: \r\n  669:     Returns ``{\"positive\", \"negative\", \"slots\"}`` where ``slots`` is the ordered\r\n  670:     list of slot names actually emitted -- the receipt that says which of the\r\n  671:     seven were present, and the thing a trace row carries so a published episode\r\n  672:     can be asked what its prompt was made of.\r\n  673: \r\n  674:     ``role`` is the engine capability token (``character_video`` /\r\n  675:     ``announcer_visual`` / ``music_visual``).\r\n  676: \r\n  677:     Never consumes raw dialogue, the episode title, the M4 scene wall, a second\r\n  678:     person, or proper-noun metadata: none of those are parameters, which is the\r\n  679:     only way to guarantee it.\r\n  680:     \"\"\"\r\n  681:     role = str(role or \"\")\r\n  682:     slots = []\r\n  683:     pieces = []\r\n  684: \r\n  685:     # 2. SUBJECT IDENTITY -- role-owned, and the one slot that differs most.\r\n  686:     subject = \"\"\r\n  687:     if role == \"character_video\":\r\n  688:         subject = _normalize_ws(subject_sigil)\r\n  689:     elif role == \"announcer_visual\":\r\n  690:         # An anthropomorphic console host: the pack's own face surface. No\r\n  691:         # lip-sync is promised anywhere on this lane.\r\n  692:         subject = _normalize_ws(getattr(style, \"announcer_subject_face\", \"\"))\r\n  693:     elif role == \"music_visual\":\r\n  694:         # No human at all. The pack's console/emblem object, or the resolved\r\n  695:         # open subject the driver passes in.\r\n  696:         subject = (_normalize_ws(getattr(style, \"announcer_subject_object\", \"\"))\r\n  697:                    or _normalize_ws(open_subject))\r\n  698:     if subject:\r\n  699:         pieces.append((\"subject\", subject))\r\n  700:         slots.append(\"subject\")\r\n  701: \r\n  702:     # 3. FRAMING LOCK.\r\n  703:     framing = GHOST_FRAMING.get(role, \"\")\r\n  704:     if framing:\r\n  705:         pieces.append((\"framing\", framing))\r\n  706:         slots.append(\"framing\")\r\n  707: \r\n  708:     # 4. ACTION / MOTION.\r\n  709:     action = resolve_action(\r\n  710:         role, motion_clause=motion_clause,\r\n  711:         pack_motion_fallback=pack_motion_fallback,\r\n  712:         beat_intent=beat_intent, sigil_seed=sigil_seed)\r\n  713:     authored = bool(_normalize_ws(motion_clause))\r\n  714:     if action:\r\n  715:         pieces.append((\"action\", action))\r\n  716:         slots.append(\"action\")\r\n  717: \r\n  718:     # 5. EMOTION -- the beat's recorded mood, one phrase.\r\n  719:     emotion_text = _trim_to(_normalize_ws(emotion), 48)\r\n  720:     if emotion_text:\r\n  721:         pieces.append((\"emotion\", \"%s mood\" % emotion_text))\r\n  722:         slots.append(\"emotion\")\r\n  723: \r\n  724:     # 6. ONE STORY ACCENT -- the beat's arc phase, one phrase.\r\n  725:     accent = _trim_to(_normalize_ws(story_accent), 48)\r\n  726:     if accent:\r\n  727:         pieces.append((\"story_accent\", accent))\r\n  728:         slots.append(\"story_accent\")\r\n  729: \r\n  730:     # 7. SHOT LAW -- affirmative, always last, never trimmed.\r\n  731:     pieces.append((\"shot_law\", GHOST_SHOT_LAW))\r\n  732:     slots.append(\"shot_law\")\r\n  733: \r\n  734:     body = _join(pieces)\r\n  735: \r\n  736:     # 1. PACK CUE -- prepended, so it lands FIRST in the emitted order.\r\n  737:     positive = _prefix_pack_cue(style, body)\r\n  738:     if positive != body:\r\n  739:         slots.insert(0, \"pack_cue\")\r\n  740: \r\n  741:     # Trim only the droppable slots, in the published order, until it fits.\r\n  742:     for droppable in GHOST_TRIM_ORDER:\r\n  743:         if len(positive) <= GHOST_PROMPT_MAX_CHARS:\r\n  744:             break\r\n  745:         if droppable == \"framing\" and role == \"character_video\":\r\n  746:             # The mid-shot floor is protected on character beats: dropping it\r\n  747:             # is how this lane starts trying to render faces again.\r\n  748:             continue\r\n  749:         if not any(name == droppable for name, _ in pieces):\r\n  750:             continue\r\n  751:         pieces = [(name, text) for name, text in pieces if name != droppable]\r\n  752:         slots = [s for s in slots if s != droppable]\r\n  753:         body = _join(pieces)\r\n  754:         positive = _prefix_pack_cue(style, body)\r\n  755: \r\n  756:     # STEP 4 OF THE PUBLISHED TRIM ORDER: shrink the DISTILLED REGISTER PHRASES.\r\n  757:     #\r\n  758:     # THIS IS WHERE THE BOOKENDS LIVE, and the numbers say why it is not\r\n  759:     # optional. A character beat composes from a sigil capped at 110 plus a\r\n  760:     # short action, and fits 320 with room to spare. A BOOKEND pulls two large\r\n  761:     # PACK surfaces instead: `announcer_subject_face` runs 163-178 characters\r\n  762:     # across the nine shipped packs and the `motion_registers` values run\r\n  763:     # 130-209 (the loader budgets them at 240). On `recur_frac` that is\r\n  764:     # 29 + 178 + 209 + 58 = 474 before separators, against a 320 ceiling.\r\n  765:     #\r\n  766:     # Both are pack-derived prose, which is exactly what step 4 names, and\r\n  767:     # NEITHER is a protected field: the protections are the character SIGIL, an\r\n  768:     # AUTHORED motion clause, the mid-shot floor and the shot law. A pack\r\n  769:     # register is a register -- shortening it is the sanctioned cut.\r\n  770:     #\r\n  771:     # Shrunk proportionally so no single surface is gutted while another keeps\r\n  772:     # its full length, and never below a floor where a direction stops being a\r\n  773:     # direction.\r\n  774:     shrinkable = []\r\n  775:     if not authored:\r\n  776:         shrinkable.append(\"action\")\r\n  777:     if role != \"character_video\":\r\n  778:         # A bookend's subject is a PACK surface, never a distilled sigil.\r\n  779:         shrinkable.append(\"subject\")\r\n  780:     for _pass in range(4):\r\n  781:         if len(positive) <= GHOST_PROMPT_MAX_CHARS or not shrinkable:\r\n  782:             break\r\n  783:         over = len(positive) - GHOST_PROMPT_MAX_CHARS\r\n  784:         share = -(-over // len(shrinkable))          # ceil, so a 1-char overflow still moves\r\n  785:         shrunk = []\r\n  786:         for name, text in pieces:\r\n  787:             if name in shrinkable:\r\n  788:                 target = len(text) - share\r\n  789:                 if target >= GHOST_SHRINK_FLOOR_CHARS:\r\n  790:                     text = _trim_to(text, target)\r\n  791:             shrunk.append((name, text))\r\n  792:         if shrunk == pieces:\r\n  793:             break                                     # nothing moved; stop rather than spin\r\n  794:         pieces = shrunk\r\n  795:         body = _join(pieces)\r\n  796:         positive = _prefix_pack_cue(style, body)\r\n  797: \r\n  798:     if len(positive) > GHOST_PROMPT_MAX_CHARS:\r\n  799:         raise GhostPromptError(\r\n  800:             \"Ghost Signal protected slots compose to %d chars, over the %d \"\r\n  801:             \"ceiling, with nothing droppable left (slots=%s). The composer or \"\r\n  802:             \"the sigil ceiling is wrong -- runtime truncation is NOT the \"\r\n  803:             \"preservation mechanism for the subject, the authored motion \"\r\n  804:             \"clause, the mid-shot floor or the shot law.\"\r\n  805:             % (len(positive), GHOST_PROMPT_MAX_CHARS, \",\".join(slots)))\r\n  806: \r\n  807:     return {\r\n  808:         \"positive\": positive,\r\n  809:         \"negative\": compose_ghost_negative(style),\r\n  810:         \"slots\": slots,\r\n  811:     }\r\n  812: \r\n  813: \r\n  814: #: The v2 ordered slots, and there are only four. Everything v1 emitted from\r\n  815: #: the ledger's raw surfaces -- the beat intent, the traits, the arc phase, the\r\n  816: #: pack motion register -- is gone: an authored drawable leaf replaces all of\r\n  817: #: it, and re-adding any of them would be re-adding the defect.\r\n  818: GHOST_V2_SLOTS = (\"pack_cue\", \"motif\", \"leaf\", \"law\")\r\n  819: \r\n  820: \r\n  821: def compose_ghost_prompt_v2(role, style, mode, motif_cue,\r\n  822:                             drawable_beat) -> dict:\r\n  823:     \"\"\"Compose one Ghost PROMPT v2 positive plus its unchanged negative.\r\n  824: \r\n  825:     PURE AND SCALAR. It takes no ledger, no shot, no cast row and no engine --\r\n  826:     only the five values the render driver and the author-time admission check\r\n  827:     both already hold, which is what makes it impossible for those two to\r\n  828:     compose different text from the same stored object.\r\n  829: \r\n  830:     It never reads raw ``beat_intent``, raw traits, raw ``arc_phase``, a pack\r\n  831:     motion register, an open subject or a motion clause. Those are not\r\n  832:     parameters, so a future edit cannot quietly reach for one.\r\n  833: \r\n  834:     Returns ``{\"positive\", \"negative\", \"components\", \"slots\"}``. ``components``\r\n  835:     is the four PROTECTED pieces by name, returned alongside the joined string\r\n  836:     so a caller can prove each survived a downstream transform without parsing\r\n  837:     prose back out of the result.\r\n  838:     \"\"\"\r\n  839:     role = str(role or \"\")\r\n  840:     mode = str(mode or \"\")\r\n  841:     motif = _normalize_ws(motif_cue)\r\n  842:     leaf = _normalize_ws(drawable_beat)\r\n  843: \r\n  844:     if role not in GHOST_FRAMING:\r\n  845:         raise GhostPromptError(\r\n  846:             \"Ghost Prompt v2 does not compose for role %r\" % (role,))\r\n  847:     law = GHOST_MODE_LAWS_V2.get(mode)\r\n  848:     if not law:\r\n  849:         raise GhostPromptError(\r\n  850:             \"Ghost Prompt v2 mode %r is not one of %s\"\r\n  851:             % (mode, \", \".join(sorted(GHOST_MODE_LAWS_V2))))\r\n  852:     if role != \"character_video\" and mode == \"figure\":\r\n  853:         raise GhostPromptError(\r\n  854:             \"Ghost Prompt v2 refuses figure mode on the %s bookend -- a radio \"\r\n  855:             \"console is not a person, and the mode scheduler never assigns \"\r\n  856:             \"this pairing\" % (role,))\r\n  857:     if not motif:\r\n  858:         raise GhostPromptError(\r\n  859:             \"Ghost Prompt v2 has no recurrence motif for a %s beat. The motif \"\r\n  860:             \"IS the identity mechanism on this lane; an empty one is a \"\r\n  861:             \"characterless episode, not a shorter prompt.\" % (role,))\r\n  862:     if not leaf:\r\n  863:         raise GhostPromptError(\r\n  864:             \"Ghost Prompt v2 has no drawable beat for a %s beat -- the \"\r\n  865:             \"authored leaf is the only content slot, so an empty one is a \"\r\n  866:             \"blank picture\" % (role,))\r\n  867: \r\n  868:     body = \", \".join((motif, leaf, law))\r\n  869:     positive = _prefix_pack_cue(style, body)\r\n  870: \r\n  871:     # The pack cue as the CUE ITSELF, recovered from what the shared authority\r\n  872:     # actually prepended rather than re-derived here -- a second derivation is\r\n  873:     # a second chance to disagree with `prefix_style_cue` about the bytes.\r\n  874:     pack_cue = \"\"\r\n  875:     if positive != body and positive.endswith(body):\r\n  876:         pack_cue = positive[:len(positive) - len(body)].strip().rstrip(\r\n  877:             \".\").strip()\r\n  878: \r\n  879:     return {\r\n  880:         \"positive\": positive,\r\n  881:         \"negative\": compose_ghost_negative(style),\r\n  882:         \"components\": {\r\n  883:             \"pack_cue\": pack_cue,\r\n  884:             \"motif\": motif,\r\n  885:             \"leaf\": leaf,\r\n  886:             \"law\": law,\r\n  887:         },\r\n  888:         \"slots\": [name for name in GHOST_V2_SLOTS\r\n  889:                   if name != \"pack_cue\" or pack_cue],\r\n  890:     }\r\n  891: \r\n  892: \r\n  893: def _join(pieces) -> str:\r\n  894:     \"\"\"Join the ordered slot pieces into one comma-separated prompt.\"\"\"\r\n  895:     return \", \".join(text for _, text in pieces if _normalize_ws(text))\r\n  896: \r\n  897: \r\n  898: def _prefix_pack_cue(style, prompt) -> str:\r\n  899:     \"\"\"Front-anchor the pack's style token. ADDITIVE ONLY -- the shared\r\n  900:     ``prefix_style_cue`` authority, never a second copy of it here.\"\"\"\r\n  901:     if style is None:\r\n  902:         return prompt\r\n  903:     try:\r\n  904:         from .._otr_visual_styles import prefix_style_cue  # type: ignore\r\n  905:     except ImportError:  # pragma: no cover -- flat test imports\r\n  906:         from _otr_visual_styles import prefix_style_cue  # type: ignore\r\n  907:     return prefix_style_cue(style, prompt)\r\n  908: \r\n  909: \r\n  910: __all__ = [\r\n  911:     \"GHOST_PROMPT_PROFILE\", \"GHOST_PROMPT_VERSION\", \"GHOST_PROMPT_VERSION_V2\",\r\n  912:     \"GHOST_PROMPT_SOURCE\", \"GHOST_MODE_LAWS_V2\", \"GHOST_V2_SLOTS\",\r\n  913:     \"distill_sigil_components\", \"compose_ghost_prompt_v2\",\r\n  914:     \"GHOST_PROMPT_MAX_CHARS\", \"GHOST_PROMPT_TARGET_LOW\",\r\n  915:     \"GHOST_PROMPT_TARGET_HIGH\", \"GHOST_NEGATIVE_MAX_CHARS\",\r\n  916:     \"GHOST_SIGIL_MAX_CHARS\", \"LANE_HYGIENE_NEGATIVE\", \"GHOST_SHOT_LAW\",\r\n  917:     \"GHOST_FRAMING\", \"GHOST_TRIM_ORDER\", \"GHOST_INTENT_ACTIONS\",\r\n  918:     \"GHOST_NEUTRAL_ACTIONS\", \"SIGIL_BUCKETS\", \"SIGIL_NEUTRAL_POOLS\",\r\n  919:     \"SIGIL_SOURCE_FIELDS\", \"GhostPromptError\",\r\n  920:     \"distill_subject_sigil\", \"resolve_action\", \"compose_ghost_negative\",\r\n  921:     \"compose_ghost_prompt\",\r\n  922: ]\r\n===== docs\\2026-08-22-ghost-prompt-v2-publish-receipt.md =====\r\n    1: # Ghost Prompt v2 -- build and A/B receipt\r\n    2: \r\n    3: **Date:** 2026-08-22\r\n    4: **Branch:** `v2.0-alpha`\r\n    5: **Start HEAD:** `e126520874be0e24fce2deb81c9c51a4c45c9e38` (another window landed\r\n    6: `388bfaaa` on CLAUDE.md mid-session; this work sits on top of it)\r\n    7: **Code commit:** `a8fad82cf14b542ab9e59ca039e251262cbb17ce`\r\n    8: **Plan:** `docs/2026-08-22-GHOST-PROMPT-V2-CONTROLLED-ABSTRACTION-PLAN.md`\r\n    9: **Baton:** `docs/2026-08-22-GHOST-PROMPT-V2-OPUS-HANDOFF.md`\r\n   10: **Model rung:** 5 (Opus, coder window). Design was settled; no panel re-run.\r\n   11: \r\n   12: ---\r\n   13: \r\n   14: ## 1. What the A arm proved, in its own bytes\r\n   15: \r\n   16: The formal baseline ran the current v1 composer through the real\r\n   17: `workflows/otr_canonical.json` on the official AnimateDiff v3 engine, and\r\n   18: published.\r\n   19: \r\n   20: * Episode `signal_lost_disc_of_destiny_20260822_163533`\r\n   21: * `RESULT SUCCESS`, `Prompt executed in 00:22:22`, `obs_publish OK`\r\n   22: * Final media in the LIVE obs tree\r\n   23: * Evidence + hashes: `<episode>\\evidence\\ghost_prompt_v1_a\\SHA256SUMS.txt`\r\n   24: \r\n   25: **Every one of its eight prompts was reconstructed and HASH-MATCHED** -- the\r\n   26: composer was loaded from the A-arm commit (`git show e1265208:...`) and\r\n   27: recomposed from the archived ledger, 8/8 by `prompt_sha8`. So these are the\r\n   28: bytes that rendered, not a retelling:\r\n   29: \r\n   30: | beat | v1 prompt (excerpt) | defect |\r\n   31: |---|---|---|\r\n   32: | `b002` | `... moves with mali vance demands dr sterling hand, Tense mood, scene, ...` | two cast NAMES in the picture; ends mid-clause; `scene` shipped as a word |\r\n   33: | `b005` | `... moves with gulliver reeves forcefully seizes the shredded, ...` | cast NAME; ends mid-clause |\r\n   34: | `b001` / `b006` | byte-identical (`prompt_sha8=f82c66e0`) | both announcer beats ask for the same picture |\r\n   35: | `music_opening_001` / `music_closing_001` | byte-identical (`prompt_sha8=1e48c63c`) | both music beats ask for the same picture |\r\n   36: \r\n   37: Character prompts measured 289--317 characters against the 320 ceiling.\r\n   38: \r\n   39: ---\r\n   40: \r\n   41: ## 2. What shipped\r\n   42: \r\n   43: One new pure module and seven edited files. `workflows/otr_canonical.json` has\r\n   44: **zero diff** and its git blob is still `c27dff3690030e78d88c3a2607a9ac54fd3935d9`\r\n   45: -- the authored object is internal to the durable `ShotRow`, so no node, socket,\r\n   46: widget or link changed. `pyproject.toml` is untouched (it is a registry-publish\r\n   47: trigger).\r\n   48: \r\n   49: * `nodes/_otr_video_engines/ghost_signal_author.py` (new) -- safe projection,\r\n   50:   opaque `g000...` ids, deterministic mode scheduling, the compact recurrence\r\n   51:   motif, strict batch parsing, leaf validation, request/output hashes, the\r\n   52:   deterministic fallback pools, the installed-SD1 token measurer, and the shared\r\n   53:   `finalize_ghost_prompt_v2(...)`.\r\n   54: * `ghost_signal_prompt.py` -- `distill_sigil_components` (shared, byte-stable),\r\n   55:   `compose_ghost_prompt_v2`, `GHOST_MODE_LAWS_V2`, `GHOST_PROMPT_VERSION_V2`,\r\n   56:   and the deletion of the `moves with <six words>` branch.\r\n   57: * `otr_shot_lock.py` -- `_resolve_writer_llm_binding` (the raw message-based\r\n   58:   writer seam), the one Ghost authoring transaction, exact cast-time coverage,\r\n   59:   the durable stamp, and the writer unload + assertion.\r\n   60: * `schemas.py` -- `ghost_prompt: Optional[dict] = None`.\r\n   61: * `render_driver.py` -- the v2 branch ahead of the legacy sigil guard, the\r\n   62:   finalizer's banana receipt, the suppressed second banana pass, the trace\r\n   63:   allowlist.\r\n   64: * `_otr_motion_clause.py` -- `skip_shot` + a LAZY `generate_fn_factory`.\r\n   65: * `otr_video_render_batch.py` -- the capability-based Ghost skip, the uninvoked\r\n   66:   factory, `_assert_writer_released()` before `run_real_episode`.\r\n   67: * `eng_ghost_signal.py` -- `motion_source = \"ledger_ghost_drawable_beat\"`.\r\n   68: \r\n   69: ### Decisions worth keeping\r\n   70: \r\n   71: * **The leaf is the only model-owned field.** The model never receives\r\n   72:   dialogue, the title, the M4 wall, raw cast prose or a name -- by\r\n   73:   construction, because none of them are parameters of the request.\r\n   74: * **Thirteen hashed keys, template digest included.** Change the instruction,\r\n   75:   its temperature or its output budget and every stored leaf reauthors rather\r\n   76:   than replaying text written under different orders.\r\n   77: * **The window is MEASURED, never asserted.** ComfyUI's SD1 encoder CHUNKS an\r\n   78:   overlength prompt rather than dropping it, so 77 is a salience choice.\r\n   79:   Measured on the installed encoder: 75 payload tokens is 77 total in ONE\r\n   80:   window; 76 spills to two. Padded row LENGTH is never counted. Production\r\n   81:   fails closed if that tokenizer cannot be reached.\r\n   82: * **Bucket greediness is real and is worked around, not hidden.** The sigil\r\n   83:   distiller assigns one source phrase to the FIRST matching bucket, so a row\r\n   84:   reading *\"a broad steady man in a charcoal overcoat\"* lands whole in the\r\n   85:   silhouette bucket and the costume bucket falls to its pool. The motif\r\n   86:   therefore scans costume THEN silhouette for its colour. The LANDMARK bucket\r\n   87:   is never scanned -- that is where the jaw, brow, scar and hair live, and\r\n   88:   face-adjacent recurrence is what v2 exists to replace.\r\n   89: * **`sci_fi_radio` authors no style cue.** Its `pack_cue` component is\r\n   90:   legitimately empty and must stay empty rather than acquire one. Pinned across\r\n   91:   all nine shipped packs.\r\n   92: \r\n   93: ---\r\n   94: \r\n   95: ## 3. Verification\r\n   96: \r\n   97: | gate | result |\r\n   98: |---|---|\r\n   99: | Full Windows suite | **12225 passed / 134 skipped / 1 xfailed**, exit 0 (379.6 s) |\r\n  100: | Bug Bible (separate repo) | **22 passed / 26 skipped / 3 xfailed** -- baseline |\r\n  101: | Canonical workflow validator | OK, **23 nodes / 57 links** |\r\n  102: | `build_variants.py --check` | **54 variants / 0 failures** |\r\n  103: | Canonical workflow git blob | `c27dff36...` unchanged |\r\n  104: | Forbidden-symbol sweep | 0 runtime hits |\r\n  105: | BOM / 0-byte / AST | clean on all 12 committed files |\r\n  106: | Focused Ghost + author + wiring tests | 522 passed / 1 skipped |\r\n  107: \r\n  108: **New executable coverage:** `tests/test_ghost_signal_author.py` (110 cases)\r\n  109: and `tests/test_ghost_prompt_v2_lane.py` (32 cases).\r\n  110: \r\n  111: ### The independent review found a real blocker, and it was fixed\r\n  112: \r\n  113: One post-coding QA pass on the frozen diff (Sonnet, read-only, grounded against\r\n  114: the Windows files). Nine of its ten areas came back clean. The tenth did not:\r\n  115: \r\n  116: > **BLOCKER.** `_assert_writer_released()` sits in a `finally:` INSIDE node 92's\r\n  117: > `except Exception -- never break the render`, which logs the RuntimeError as\r\n  118: > `\"motion_clause skipped\"` and falls straight through to `run_real_episode`.\r\n  119: \r\n  120: Confirmed and fixed: the release now runs OUTSIDE that catch-all, gated on\r\n  121: whether the pass was enabled. **A guard inside the thing that swallows guards\r\n  122: is not a guard**, and the suite was green through it -- so the replacement test\r\n  123: is structural (`test_the_writer_release_is_not_inside_a_catch_all` walks the\r\n  124: AST and refuses the call inside any broad-`except` try).\r\n  125: \r\n  126: Two smaller findings, both confirmed and fixed:\r\n  127: * `_ghost_unload_writer` returned early when the unload itself raised, skipping\r\n  128:   the one line that proves anything -- and that is the case most likely to have\r\n  129:   left weights resident.\r\n  130: * A duplicated lazy-load block in `_otr_motion_clause.py`: a patch whose OLD\r\n  131:   text was a SUBSTRING of its own NEW text, so the \"already applied?\" guard\r\n  132:   matched and applied it twice. Inert, but deleted and pinned.\r\n  133: \r\n  134: ---\r\n  135: \r\n  136: ## 4. The three live arms\r\n  137: \r\n  138: All three published to the LIVE `otr/obs/` tree. Every leg `RESULT SUCCESS` +\r\n  139: `obs_publish OK`, evidence archived with SHA256SUMS under each episode's\r\n  140: `evidence/` directory.\r\n  141: \r\n  142: | arm | episode | prompts | technical model | ghost source |\r\n  143: |---|---|---|---|---|\r\n  144: | **A** | `signal_lost_disc_of_destiny_20260822_163533` | v1 | Mistral-Nemo | -- |\r\n  145: | **B1** | `signal_lost_disc_of_destiny_20260822_171254` | v2 | Mistral-Nemo | `deterministic_fallback` x8 |\r\n  146: | **B2** | `signal_lost_turntables_lament_the_last_spin_20260822_174415` | v2 | gemma-4-12b | **`writer_llm` x8** |\r\n  147: \r\n  148: ### A vs B1 IS the same-seed A/B, and it is exact\r\n  149: \r\n  150: Measured, not assumed (`tmp/ghost_v2_arm_comparison.txt`):\r\n  151: \r\n  152:     SAME : voiced_text_sha, episode_seed, style, roles, frames,\r\n  153:            video_seeds, cast, creative_model, negative_sha8\r\n  154:     DIFF : -- nothing --\r\n  155: \r\n  156: Same script (`9c1e40b90ff0d5b8`), same eight video seeds, same 250/253/180/196/\r\n  157: 110/98/514/200 frame contract, same negative. **The prompt is the only variable**,\r\n  158: which is exactly what the plan asked for. Prompt length fell from 208--317\r\n  159: characters to 164--198, every one measured at 32--43 installed SD1 tokens in a\r\n  160: single window.\r\n  161: \r\n  162: ### A vs B2 is NOT same-script, and this receipt says so rather than implying it\r\n  163: \r\n  164:     SAME : episode_seed, style, roles, video_seeds, cast, creative_model, negative_sha8\r\n  165:     DIFF : voiced_text_sha, frames\r\n  166: \r\n  167: The technical slot participates in the writer's structured passes, so pinning it\r\n  168: to gemma changed the script. B2 is therefore the proof of the **LLM treatment**\r\n  169: -- eight rows `source=writer_llm`, empty fallback reason, no replay -- not a\r\n  170: same-script pixel comparison. **A same-script LLM arm is not obtainable without\r\n  171: reverting code**: the v1 content route no longer exists, so there is no way to\r\n  172: re-render a v1 baseline on gemma's script. The A arm was captured before the\r\n  173: change precisely because this was foreseeable.\r\n  174: \r\n  175: ### The model choice was measured, not assumed\r\n  176: \r\n  177: The first live leg exposed something no unit test would have: Mistral-Nemo\r\n  178: answered in a perfectly valid envelope but wrote four-word abstractions\r\n  179: (`signal oscillates, broadcast begins`, `silhouette shreds papers, tension\r\n  180: builds`). The validator rejected both attempts and the batch fell to the\r\n  181: deterministic pools -- machinery correct, instruction too thin.\r\n  182: \r\n  183: Two fixes followed. The batch template gained worked examples AT the target\r\n  184: length plus named counter-examples (a word COUNT is a number a model does not\r\n  185: feel). Then both candidates were put to the real batch prompt directly --\r\n  186: two minutes instead of two renders:\r\n  187: \r\n  188: | model | leaves accepted by the production validator |\r\n  189: |---|---|\r\n  190: | `google/gemma-4-12b-it` | **8 / 8** |\r\n  191: | `mistralai/Mistral-Nemo-Instruct-2407` | 4 / 8 (3x a hand in `object` mode, 1x dangling tail) |\r\n  192: \r\n  193: gemma's leaves, as rendered in B2:\r\n  194: \r\n  195:     signal  a jagged waveform pulses and expands across a dark screen\r\n  196:     object  the radio dial rotates and a small emblem glows bright\r\n  197:     object  a charcoal lantern flickers and casts a long jagged shadow\r\n  198:     signal  a cream chart line spikes and vibrates against a grid\r\n  199:     figure  an angular silhouette reaches for a lantern in a dark room\r\n  200:     object  a cream chart emblem spins rapidly on a heavy turntable\r\n  201:     signal  a single radio dial signal fades into a thin horizontal line\r\n  202:     object  the broadcast console emblem pulses once and then goes dark\r\n  203: \r\n  204: **OPEN, and it is the operator's call:** `config/profiles/otr_ghost_signal_v3.json`\r\n  205: still pins `technical_model` to Mistral-Nemo. B2 pinned gemma per-leg via\r\n  206: `--set` rather than editing the shipped profile, because the technical slot also\r\n  207: drives the writer's structured passes -- so promoting it changes SCRIPTS on this\r\n  208: lane, and story output is a closed subject. The measurement above is the case\r\n  209: for promoting it; the decision is not a coder's to make silently.\r\n  210: \r\n  211: ---\r\n  212: \r\n  213: ## 4A. THE OPERATOR WATCHED IT AND IT HAD TANKED THE PICTURES\r\n  214: \r\n  215: **This section exists because everything above it was true and still added up to\r\n  216: a worse show.** Sections 1-4 record a clean build: the text defect was real, the\r\n  217: fix was measured, the control vector was exact, the suite was green and three\r\n  218: episodes published. Then the operator watched them and said the AnimateDiff\r\n  219: winners had tanked. He was right, and nothing in the receipt above would have\r\n  220: told him so -- every gate this sprint passed was a gate on TEXT.\r\n  221: \r\n  222: ### What the pixels say\r\n  223: \r\n  224: Counting recognisable subjects, frame 40 of every beat, all five arms:\r\n  225: \r\n  226: | arm | recognisable | what is actually on screen |\r\n  227: |---|---:|---|\r\n  228: | v1 prompts, `video_art` (the one he remembers as the winner) | **4 human figures** + 4 vivid abstracts | a man standing in a gallery, a figure in motion, a capped silhouette, a dancer |\r\n  229: | v1 prompts, `archival` | **4 of 4** | a period radio set, a figure at a lectern, a document, a tuning dial |\r\n  230: | v2, LLM leaves, `archival` | 2 of 8 | a lantern on a post, a dial -- the rest is texture |\r\n  231: | v2, deterministic, `archival` | 0 of 4 sampled | stripes, static, black blobs |\r\n  232: | v2, LLM leaves, `anime` | **0 of 8** | pattern noise, no subject anywhere |\r\n  233: \r\n  234: ### The mechanism, and it is falsifiable\r\n  235: \r\n  236: Legibility tracks **how much concrete noun is in the prompt**, and nothing else.\r\n  237: The two v2 beats that survived are the only two whose authored leaf named a real\r\n  238: object -- a lantern and a radio dial. Every mush beat named `emblem`, `signal`,\r\n  239: `field` or `waveform`.\r\n  240: \r\n  241: v1 handed SD1.5 *\"a man, a broad steady figure, a charcoal coat, holding a\r\n  242: folded chart\"* and got a man in a coat. v2 handed it *\"charcoal ledger emblem\"*\r\n  243: plus *\"an abstract signal field filling the composition\"*, and a 512x288 SD1.5\r\n  244: obliged by painting texture. **The mode laws were literally instructions toward\r\n  245: abstraction.**\r\n  246: \r\n  247: ### The honest self-assessment\r\n  248: \r\n  249: Two changes shipped where only one was needed. Removing the name leak and the\r\n  250: mid-clause fragment was right and that machinery is untouched. Replacing the\r\n  251: CONCRETE SUBJECT with an abstract one was a separate, unforced change, and it is\r\n  252: what broke the pictures. The plan's premise -- *prefer abstract coolness over\r\n  253: rigid character likeness* -- was the operator's call and remains reasonable; what\r\n  254: this lane proved is that on THIS model, abstraction without a noun is not cool,\r\n  255: it is mush.\r\n  256: \r\n  257: **No gate in this sprint could have caught it, and that is the transferable\r\n  258: lesson.** Every check was on the text: token count, character count, name\r\n  259: absence, dangling tails, component survival. All of them passed. A prompt lane's\r\n  260: acceptance needs at least one countable question about the PICTURE.\r\n  261: \r\n  262: ### v2.1 -- what was changed (`f6075592`, `633e68a7`)\r\n  263: \r\n  264: * mode laws name a physical thing: an object on a real surface, an object lit\r\n  265:   against a dark room. No more \"abstract field filling the composition\".\r\n  266: * the motif is the prop ITSELF -- `a charcoal lantern`, not\r\n  267:   `charcoal lantern emblem`. An emblem is not a thing.\r\n  268: * `figure` says FIGURE and wears a garment. SD1.5 does not know a \"silhouette\"\r\n  269:   is meant to be somebody; handed one it drew vertical black shapes.\r\n  270: * bookends name real radio hardware: a bakelite radio set, a spinning turntable.\r\n  271: * `GHOST_CHARACTER_CYCLE` is figure/object/figure/signal -- half the character\r\n  272:   beats show a person again. The even three-mode cycle made two of every three\r\n  273:   non-figurative and the episode lost its people. This still honours *\"do not\r\n  274:   force the same mediocre person into every clip\"*.\r\n  275: * a leaf naming a texture (static, waveform, gradient, noise, grain) is rejected.\r\n  276: \r\n  277: **A second live leg then caught a bug no unit test would have.** `_HUMAN_WORDS`\r\n  278: carried hand/arm/shoulder, so `object` mode rejected *\"the silver ledger sits on\r\n  279: a desk as a clock hand ticks\"* -- a CLOCK hand -- and, because one bad leaf\r\n  280: rejects the whole batch by design, that killed all eight beats twice and dropped\r\n  281: the episode to deterministic clauses. Body parts are off the list; a whole figure\r\n  282: is still refused outside `figure` mode.\r\n  283: \r\n  284: ### What is proven and what is NOT\r\n  285: \r\n  286: * **Proven:** the v2.1 concrete POOLS render subjects again. On `video_art`,\r\n  287:   roughly 3-4 of 8 beats read as a real thing (a radio set with dial-eyes, a\r\n  288:   metal object on wood, silhouetted figures) against 0-2 before.\r\n  289: * **NOW PROVEN -- the decisive arm rendered and published.** Episode\r\n  290:   `signal_lost_echoes_unheard_20260822_202248`, v2.1 on `archival_documentary`,\r\n  291:   `writer_llm=8` with zero fallback and zero replay, `RESULT SUCCESS` +\r\n  292:   `obs_publish OK`, 23:24.\r\n  293: \r\n  294:   **6 of 8 beats render a recognisable subject and 2 of them are people** --\r\n  295:   piano keys, a lit chain, a person in a white coat, a coil of cream rope on a\r\n  296:   wooden table, a man walking a street with a second figure behind him, a radio\r\n  297:   set on a shelf. Two stay weak (a cabinet, a warm gradient).\r\n  298: \r\n  299:   | arm | recognisable | figures |\r\n  300:   |---|---:|---:|\r\n  301:   | v1 prompts, archival | 4 of 4 sampled | some |\r\n  302:   | v2 prompts, archival, LLM leaves | 2 of 8 | 0 |\r\n  303:   | **v2.1 prompts, archival, LLM leaves** | **6 of 8** | **2** |\r\n  304: \r\n  305:   **Beat 5 is the end-to-end proof.** gemma wrote *\"a hand grabs the cream rope\r\n  306:   on the wooden table\"* and the render is a coil of cream rope on a wooden\r\n  307:   table. That leaf is exactly what the pre-fix `_HUMAN_WORDS` rejected as \"a\r\n  308:   person in object mode\", so the frame exists only because the clock-hand fix\r\n  309:   landed. And the hygiene v2 was built for is intact: no cast name in the\r\n  310:   picture, no sentence cut mid-clause, every prompt measured at one CLIP\r\n  311:   window.\r\n  312: \r\n  313: ---\r\n  314: \r\n  315: ## 5. Admissions\r\n  316: \r\n  317: * **No new PBUG and no new Bug Bible entry.** The v1 fragment is reproducible\r\n  318:   from the checked-in composer and is now proven against a published render,\r\n  319:   but it belongs to the already-admitted dangling-preservation class\r\n  320:   (`BUG_BIBLE.yaml` 12.127 territory); this sprint added executable coverage\r\n  321:   rather than a new portable rule.\r\n  322: * **NO VRAM CLAIM.** No ceiling was measured or enforced on these legs.\r\n  323: * **THE RANKING IS THE DRIVER'S OWN EYE, and it is stated as such.** The frames\r\n  324:   were read directly and counted; that is a judgement, not an instrument. An\r\n  325:   agy/Antigravity lane was asked to rank the mp4s and **could not watch video\r\n  326:   at all** -- it returned a critique of the request document and zero ranking,\r\n  327:   which is reported rather than dressed up. A separate Antigravity review of\r\n  328:   the CODE did read the frames and independently counted 0 human faces across\r\n  329:   the v2 samples, agreeing with the driver. One of that review's claims was\r\n  330:   discarded against the real file: it said the Ghost v3 profile still pinned\r\n  331:   Mistral-Nemo, and `ab0a7809` had already promoted it to gemma-4-12b.\r\n  332: * **The boot-time `basline-models` warning is pre-existing and benign** -- ComfyUI\r\n  333:   warns about every sibling directory under `custom_nodes` that is not a node\r\n  334:   pack, and the VRAM workbench is one. It carries a traceback, which cost one\r\n  335:   false leg-fault alarm during this session; it is not an OTR fault.\r\n===== SEARCH =====\r\nrg : The term 'rg' is not recognized as the name of a cmdlet, function, script file, or operable program. Check the \r\nspelling of the name, or if a path was included, verify that the path is correct and try again.\r\nAt line:2 char:323\r\n+ ...  { $n++; '{0,5}: {1}' -f $n, $_ } }; \"===== SEARCH =====\"; rg -n --gl ...\r\n+                                                                ~~\r\n    + CategoryInfo          : ObjectNotFound: (rg:String) [], CommandNotFoundException\r\n    + FullyQualifiedErrorId : CommandNotFoundException\r\n \r\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
